Obama receives Netanyahu
The relationship between Obama and Netanyahu isn't exactly friendly.
The two wanted to talk about the implementation of the international agreement as well as Tehran's destabilizing measures in the Middle East.
However, the meeting will also focus on the conflict with the Palestinians and the two-state solution discussed.
The relationship between Obama and Netanyahu has been strained for years.
Washington criticizes Israel's ongoing settlement construction and accuses Netanyahu of a lack of will in the peace process.
Due to Obama's deal for Iran's nuclear program, the relationship between the two has deteriorated further.
In March, Netanyahu had given a controversial speech at the invitation of the Republicans before the U.S. Congress, which was sometimes seen as an affront to Obama.
The speech was not agreed with Obama, he had rejected a meeting with reference to the upcoming election in Israel.
In an emergency call, professor confesses to shooting his girlfriend
In an emergency call, Professor Shannon Lamb told the police with a somewhat shaky voice that he had shot his girlfriend and that the officers had to come to his house.
Lamb said his "sweet dog," however, was still alive and probably agitated, and he said the dead woman's family contacts could be found on the phone.
Within the house, officers found Amy Prentiss' corpse and a hand-written note scribbled onto a white block: "I'm so sorry, I wanted to undo it, I loved Amy and she's the only woman who ever loved me," according to authorities in the letter and he was signed by Lamb.
There was no indication that Lamb, who taught in two online courses for Delta State University in Cleveland, Mississippi, had already traveled 300 miles to the school grounds, where he shot and killed a popular history professor, Ethan Schmidt at the door to his office, according to police.
Delta State University Police Chief Lynn Buford said university staff heard the shooting at 10:18 a.m.
He said that Lamb made the fatal emergency call at some point afterwards.
By the end of the day, there was another death: lamb took his own life when the police encircled him.
A day after the shooting at the university that forced students and lecturers to hide behind closed doors, authorities are still trying to get a picture of what motivated Lamb.
The details released by investigators at both ends of the state, as well as what students and associates who testified him helped paint a picture of a talented but potentially difficult teacher.
Students said they were looking forward to his classes.
Police in Gautier, where Prentiss died, said he didn't have a history of violence or a criminal history.
Schmidt himself had mentioned Lamb in a book written by him, in which he mentioned the "wonderful people" with whom he shared his academic life.
Both taught in the Department of Social Sciences and History, whose faculty includes 17 members, and many students attended courses of both.
At the same time, there were some propensities to problematic behaviors.
One student, Brandon Beavers, who praised Lamb, said he also seemed a little excited and nervous "as if there was something wrong with him."
Another student, Mikel Sykes, said Lamm had told him that he was dealing with stress at the end of the academic year 2014-15.
Lamb had previously asked Delta State University for a leave of absence for health reasons, saying that he had any health problem in the process.
This year, he taught only two online courses.
Recent changes in the university's employment policy meant the doctorate, for which Lamb had worked so hard, would represent no guarantee for an automatic path to employment as an assistant professor.
University President William LaForge said he knew nothing of a conflict between Lamb and Schmidt, but "of course there was something in Mr Lamb's imagination."
These are questions Lamb can no longer answer.
After escaping from campus, police later found Lamb's trail when he drove back into Mississippi from Arkansas.
Before he could be arrested, Lamb killed himself with an .38 caliber pistol in the backyard of a house about a mile south of his parents' home on the outskirts of Greenville, Mississippi, Washington County Coroner Methel Johnson said.
His car was still running in the driveway.
It wasn't immediately clear why Lamm went to this house, but Johnson said she believed he knew the people who lived there.
Lamb grew up in the area.
Lamb started his work at the university in 2009, which has 3,500 students in a city with about 12,000 inhabitants and taught geography and pedagogy.
He earned his doctorate in pedagogy in the spring.
One of Lamb's longtime girlfriends described him as intelligent, charismatic and funny.
Carla Hairston said she was 15 and Lamm was 20 when they met through mutual friends.
She and her friends were in high school, and he was the cool older guy who tried to teach her guitar playing for a few years.
He was the swarm of women at the time.
All the girls melted there when he was there," said Hairston, now 40 and residing in the Jackson suburb of Brandon.
Lamb and Prentiss have obviously been together for some time.
In the emergency call, Lamb said "I killed my wife," but there was no record of a marriage.
They had a dog named Lightning who lived with them in the brick house adjacent to a Bayou in Gautier.
The police said the dog was in order.
Prentiss's ex-husband, Shawn O'Steen, said they were divorced 15 years ago but remained friends and had a daughter who was now 19 years old.
"She was totally devastated," O'Steen said of his daughter.
She and her mother were absolutely best friends.
O'Steen said he'd never met Lamb, but through his daughter Abigail he'd heard Lamb was interested in music and playing blues.
Lamb met Prentiss when he and Abigail were both performing at a summer fair three years ago.
They later played one gig or another together.
O'Steen said his daughter writes and sings her own music.
Prentiss was a nurse who worked online for various companies.
Mike Shaffer, a bartender and sometimes entertainment coordinator at The Julep Room, a dimly lit bar near Gautier, said he saw Lamb and Prentiss Night for the last time on the night of September 5 after Lamb had finished his guitar and harmonica playing in front of a sparse audience.
There was no indication that anything was wrong.
"Just just a happy couple," he said.
We were cleaning up and joking with each other.
I mean, they've both had a good sense of joking.
Schmidt, the dead professor, led the first-semester seminar program and specialized in Native Americans and colonial history, said Don Allan Mitchell, an English professor at the school.
He was married and had three young children.
He graduated at Emporia State University in Kansas and was president of his brotherhood and student committee.
At the Delta State campus in Cleveland, about 900 people, including faculty, staff, students, and members of the community, attended a candlelight commemoration on Tuesday night.
Schmidt's wife Liz and his brother Jeff Schmidt also attended the vigil, while the university choir sang "Bright Morning Star" and "Amazing Grace."
Classes will resume on Wednesday.
"We're trying to persuade our students to return," LaForge said.
The crisis is over.
This is a day of healing.
Amy and Rogelio Solis reported from Cleveland, Mississippi.
Associated Press Writers Emily Wagster Pettus in Greenville, Mississippi, and Rebecca Santana in New Orleans contributed to this report.
This story has been corrected to reflect that the police are now saying that Lamb made the emergency call after he shot Schmidt, and that Lamm described Prentiss as his wife in his emergency call, but there is no evidence that the two have ever married.
Toys R Us plans to hire fewer workers for the Christmas period
Toys R Us says it won't hire as many Christmas season employees as last year, but the toy and baby products retailer says it will give the employees and seasonal workers currently employed at him a chance to work more hours.
The company said it plans to hire 40,000 people in stores and distribution centers across the country. As of the 201nest, 45,000 workers were still hired.
Most jobs will be part-time jobs.
The company said it will begin job interviews this month and the number of employees increases from October to December.
While the holidays themselves are still months away, the Christmas business is approaching, and companies are preparing to hire temporary workers to equip stores with employees selling, shipping and delivering products.
Michael J. Fox jokes about his 'date' with Princess Diana
The premiere of "Back to the Future" was professionally a big moment for Michael J. Fox.
She was also notable for personal reasons.
"We sat in the theatre waiting for the royals, and I realise the seat next to me is empty, and I'm beginning to realize that Princess Diana will sit next to me," he said in a trailer for an upcoming documentary "Back in Time."
The film begins, and it comes to my mind that I'm a simulated yawn and an arm length away from being on a date with the Princess of Wales.
"Back in Time," a documentary about "Back to the Future," features interviews with Fox, Lea Thompson and Christopher Lloyd, as well as with director Robert Zemeckis and producer Steven Spielberg.
"Back for the Future" premiered 30 years ago.
"It's really for me," Spielberg said, "undisputedly the biggest time-travel movie ever made."
"Back in Time" will be shown on VOD, DVD and in selected cinemas in October.
U.N. chief says there's no military solution in Syria
Secretary-General Ban Ki-Moon says his response to Russia's increased military support for Syria is: "There is no military solution" to the nearly five-year-longing conflict, and more weapons will only exacerbate violence and misery for millions of people.
The U.N. chief once again urges all parties, including the divided U.N. Security Council, to unite and support inclusive negotiations to find a political solution.
Ban told a press conference on Wednesday that he plans to meet the foreign ministers of the council's five permanent member nations - U.S., Russia, China, Britain and France - on the sidelines of the General Assembly's ministerial meeting later this month to discuss the situation in Syria.
He regretted that the break-up in the council and within the Syrian people and regional powers "make this situation unsolvable."
Ban urged the five permanent members to show the same solidarity and unity they have demonstrated in reaching a nuclear deal with Iran in addressing the crisis.
8 polling scores showing that Donald Trump is to be taken seriously.
Some have tried to brand him as a flop.
Others dismissed him as a joke.
And some are waiting for a breakdown.
But no matter how some Republicans are trying to pull Donald Trump down from the top ranks in polls, it didn't (yet) haven't worked.
Ten of the last eleven national polls showed Donald Trump leading in the double digits, and some are beginning to seriously wonder what it means for the chances of nominating real estate mogul.
Of course, it's too early in the electoral term.
None are claiming that Trump is likely to win the Republican nomination.
Experts emphasize that at that time in 2011 Rick Perry's leadership was handed over to the rising Herman Cain, none of whom won even one state in the nomination process.
And there are many reasons why he would struggle in a general election.
But outsider groups like Jeb Bush's Super PAC and the Conservative business group Club for Growth are now recognizing Trump's perseverance and beging letting their dollars flow to overthrow him.
Here are some recent poll scores suggesting the property mogul isn't just a temporary phase:
Trump's popularity ratings have turned 180 degrees.
Shortly before Donald Trump announced his candidacy in mid-Junene, a Monmouth University poll showed that only two out of ten Republicans viewed the real estate mogul positively.
By mid-July, it was 40 percent.
At the beginning of August, it was 52 percent.
Now six out of 10 Republicans have a positive view of Donald Trump.
About three out of ten say they have a negative opinion.
And these figures are held in early states.
A Quinnipiac vote in Iowa last week found that 60 percent of Republicans have a good opinion of Trump.
Two-thirds of GOP Voters GOP would be happy with Trump as a candidate.
In a CNN/ORC poll last week, 67 percent of Republicans said they were either "excited" or "satisfied" if Trump were nominated.
Only two out of 10 say they'd be "upset" if he were the candidate.
Only Ben Carson creates roughly the same enthusiasm as Trump (43 percent say they're "excited" vs. 40 percent who say the same thing about Trump).
The next in terms of enthusiasm?
Marco Rubio with only 21 percent.
On the other hand, 47 percent of Republican voters say they would be "unhappy" or "upkeeped" if Jeb Bush, the establishment's favorite, became the candidate.
A majority of Republicans don't see Trump's temperament as a problem.
While Donald Trump is widely criticized for his bombastic and insulting statements, 52 per cent of Irish voters tending Republicans believe the real estate mogul has the right temperament for a president, according to ABC News/Washington Post on Monday.
The same figure applies in the nation's first primary state, the state of Iowa, where the same 52 percent of Republicans think he has the personality for a commander-in-chief, according to Quinnipiac last week.
Even so, 44 percent think he doesn't have the personality to effectively carry out office, and nearly six out of 10 independents say his temper doesn't fit into the White House, according to ABC/Post.
Republican voters get used to the idea.
If they put on their scholarly hats, Republican voters consider Trump to be real.
When asked who is most likely to win the GOP nomination, according to a CNN/ORC poll from last week four out of ten say Trump is the best bet.
That's a change since when four out of ten put their money on Jeb Bush at the end of July.
Full Disclosure: GOP voters didn't have had the clearest crystal ball in the past.
At that point in the final cycle, four out of ten Republicans thought Rick Perry would win the nomination versus just 28% for final candidate Mitt Romney.
Still, a variety of GOP voters see Trump's campaign as plausible.
Even if Republicans were rallying around another nominee, Trump still beats almost everyone.
Some experts point out that the splintered field is likely to contribute to Trump's leadership, while anti-Trump support is diffusely distributed among more than a dozen other candidates.
But a Monmouth-University poll in early September shows that in a hypothetical head-to-the-head race between Trump and most other Republican candidates, Trump is reapping majority support.
He's 13 points ahead of Carly Fiorina, ahead of Marco Rubio 14 points, ahead of Walker 15 points, ahead of Jeb Bush 19 points and finally ahead of Rand Paul, John Kasich and Chris Christie 33 points.
It's a head-to-head race with Ted Cruz.
The only candidate who beats him?
Ben Carson would overtake the entrepreneur with 19 points in a hypothetical head-to-head race.
A narrow majority of Donald Trump fans say they've formed their opinions.
A new CBS/NYT poll on Tuesday shows that only slightly more than half of voters who support Trump say they've given their votes firmly.
Of course, a lot can happen to change that, and no one can really say never to change your mind.
46 percent said they leave the door open to switch to other candidates.
Still, Trump's strongest competition at the moment is the other outsider, neurosurgeon Ben Carson, but voters who say they've formed their mind are twice as common for Trump.
Six out of 10 Republicans say they're aligned with Trump on the issue of immigration.
Ever since Donald Trump, in his campaign speech two months ago, called immigrants from Mexico "rapists," immigration has been linchpin in the public discourse on 2016.
Some are concerned that Trump's bombastic expression will keep crucial Hispano-Americans away from the Republican Party and damage the reorienting efforts.
But according to Monday's new ABC/Post poll, six out of 10 Republicans say they're aligned with Trump on issues of immigration.
So as long as immigration remains in the spotlight, Donald Trump will apparently do too.
Frustration over the government is climbing to new highs.
Donald Trump and Ben Carson, meanwhile, account for about half the support of Republican voters, largely from their outsider status.
Six out of 10 Republicans say in ABC/Post's new Monday poll they'd rather have a political outsider than someone with government experience.
And they're furious about Washington, too.
A Des Moines Register/Bloomberg poll in Iowa two weeks ago shows that three out of four Iowa Republicans are frustrated about Republicans in Congress, with 54 percent "unhappied" and 21 percent "helly upset."
Munich, Sylt and Co.: Germany's five most exclusive residential areas
Here in the Hobookenweg is the most expensive residential complex - with a view over the North Sea watt.
In the street, a square meter of a thatched house costs on average 73,300 euros.
