Champions League: Bayern dream start thanks to Müller and Götze
Two-time top scorer in Piraeus: Bayern star Thomas Müller simply has a run.
A happy goal from Thomas Müller paved the way for Bayern to a deserved 3:0 at Olympiakos Piraeus - then it ran.
A sovereign kick-off of the Champions League season.
The world champions Thomas Müller and Mario Götze have given FC Bayern a dream start to the "Mission Milan" in the cauldron of Piraeus.
The team of coach Pep Guardiola won their opening game in the Champions League with the Greek record champion Olympiakos in a hot atmosphere thanks to Müller's double pack (52./90.+2) and Götze's hit (89.) deserved with 3:0 (0:0).
The twelfth starting success in series should be the first step towards the final on May 28, 2016 in Italy, where Guardiola finally wants to bring the Henkelpott to Munich in the third attempt.
On his 28th hit in the Champions League to Bayern leadership, Müller received gracious assistance from Olympiakos goalkeeper Roberto, who let pass a shot of the world champion intended as a flank.
"We won't underestimate them," Bayern captain Philipp Lahm had promised before the match at Karaiskakis Stadium in front of 31,688 hot-blooded fans - and Munich kept their word.
They showed a concentrated and dedicated performance without shining.
Piraeus had won his most recent six home games in the premier class, including well-known clubs such as Manchester United, Atlético Madrid or Juventus Turin.
Borussia Dortmund (1:3) also stumbled here in 2011.
Yet Bayern, undefeated in seven European Cup duels with Greek teams, did not want to be the next victim.
They approached courageously, but often too unimaginative in their offensive efforts.
The attacks have too often been initiated by relocating to left-winger Douglas Costa, who rarely came through against former Brunswick Omar Elabdellaoui.
Müller had more luck from the right when his flank smashed in from right half-field in the top left-half.
In the 67th minute, the world champion awarded the possible 2:0.
Robert Lewandowski had the best opportunities in half one.
At first, when Olympiakos striker Ideye Brown struck Poland's header off the line (26th).
Then by direct pick-up that goalkeeper Roberto parried with his knee (35th).
Although Bayern raised their usual dominant game, Piraeus was able to set pinpricks.
Goalkeeper Manuel Neuer, however, was relieved.
For example, when he steered a shot from Ideye over the bar (56th).
In front of Neuer, Guardiola had a chain of four with David Alaba in the centre alongside Jerome Boateng, who did well.
Mario Götze, after muscular problems back in the squad, initially sat outside.
The World Cup hero only gave the green light after the graduation session, said sports director Matthias Sammer on "Sky": "If you want to make something else out of it, that's vicious".
Guardiola also opted against him on the first change when he brought Kingsley Coman for the battered Lewandowski (59th).
Götze arrived late for Arturo Vidal (76.) and made everything clear 13 minutes later, by penalty Müller put one more on it.
Before the game, there had been riots in the stadium.
After a smaller group of partially hooded Bayern fans in their spectator block had apparently provoked the supporters of hosts Piraeus with words and gestures, the security forces intervened and proceeded with batons against the Munich supporters.
At least one Bayern fan were transported from the stadium injured.
Those who smoke have a higher risk of losing teeth
Anyone who smokes not only harms the lungs, but also the teeth - this is suggested by a Potsdam long-term study.
The good news is that the risk decreases quickly if it waives.
Smokers have a significantly increased risk of losing their teeth early.
This is according to the long-term study of an international team of researchers, including several employees of the German Institute for Nutritional Research (DIfE) in Potsdam.
Compared to non-smokers, smokers were at risk of losing their teeth prematurely by 2.5 to 3.6 times higher.
The scientists had observed more than 23,300 participants.
The main causes of tooth failure are tooth decay and inflammation of the gums, the DIfE emphasizes.
For this periodontitis, smoking is a risk factor, the authors point out.
Therefore, the association between smoking and dental loss is due to more frequent gum infections among smokers.
It is not clear to what extent smoking also increases the risk of tooth decay.
However, people who quit smoking could reduce their risk within a short period of time, the researchers write in the Journal of Dental Research.
However, it could take more than ten years for the risk to drop back to the level of non-smokers, explained first author Thomas Dietrich from the English University of Birmingham.
You have to convince people to become non-smokers, explained study leader Heiner Boeing of DIfE: "Smoking shortens the lifetime."
Not smoking is good for lungs and vessels and, according to our findings, also leads to good dental health into old age.
In Germany, over 20 percent of the population aged 65 to 74 are affected by toothlessness, the DIfE said.
Previous research had already indicated that smoking increases the risk of early dental failure.
The new long-term study supports the findings.
Archaeologists come across remnants from late antiquity at Cologne's banks of the Rhine
Once again, archaeological finds have been made on the right bank of the Rhine in Cologne.
Near the construction site for the new Rheinboulevard, archaeologists from the Roman-German Museum have unearthed skeletons and remnants of historic buildings, museum manager Marcus Trier said on Tuesday.
Since the beginning of the construction work on the Rhine, historians have been looking for so-called ground monuments.
For all construction projects in the district of Cologne, the museum is always consulted as a specialist office for archaeological monument preservation.
In the third construction phase of the Rheinboulevard, the archaeologists have now found it.
It had already been known from text documents that a fortress in the 4th century and in the 8th century stood a church, explained Trier.
In addition to the remnants of the fortress and the church tower, a cemetery with around 350 tombs was also discovered, which probably belonged to the church.
Young woman captured by freight train
After a young woman crashed at the station in ­Geilenkirchen-Lindern, the federal police asks the population for help.
Early Wednesday morning at around 1.40 a.m., the woman crossed the tracks coming from the station building at the end of the platform and was captured by a freight train coming from the direction of Mönchengladbach.
By capturing the train, she has sustained the most serious injuries, according to federal police.
She was undergoing emergency medical treatment at the scene and taken to a hospital by rescue workers.
There is no knowledge about the reason for the young woman's nocturnal track crossing, the investigating officers.
The incident may have been witnessed by witnesses or local residents have heard acoustic perceptions.
Residents or passers-by from Linderner Bahn, Ziegelbäckerweg, Thomashofstraße and Leiffarther Straße (L364) are eligible.
Instructions that provide information about the cause of the accident can be given under around-the-clock and free hotline of the federal police by calling 0800/6888000 or any other police department.
European Commission proposes new court in dispute over TTIP
In order to defuse the dispute over the planned transatlantic free trade agreement TTIP, the EU Commission has proposed an extensive reform of the current arbitration system.
The controversial private arbitration facilities for disputes between corporations and states are therefore to be replaced by a more transparent system, which corresponds to significantly more traditional courts in its functioning.
"We want to set up a system that the citizens trust," EU Commissioner Cecilia Malmström said at the presentation.
The Swede intends to introduce the proposal as a European negotiating position in the free trade talks with the USA.
The German government announced support.
TTIP aims to create the world's largest free trade area with 800 million people.
The plans for the reform of the current arbitration proceedings concretely stipulate that the EU states and the USA jointly select independent judges for a new investment tribunal.
They should come equally from the EU, the USA and from third countries.
For the first time, a second instance is also planned.
It would allow you to object to verdicts.
Until now, the parties to the proceedings agreed among themselves on arbitrators, there was no possibility of opposition to their judgments.
In the discussion of the past months, it has become clear that citizens did not trust the old ISDS system in terms of fairness and justice, Malmström commented.
In the long term, it intends to work towards an international investment court.
For example, disputes between Chinese companies and EU states could also be resolved there.
The investor-state arbitration (ISDS) demanded by large corporations in particular are considered one of the main reasons for the great resistance in Europe against TTIP.
The traditional arbitration tribunals are criticized by opponents as a kind of parallel justice, over which companies can claim damages at the expense of taxpayers, obscure national laws or enforce a reduction of consumer and environmental standards.
In initial opinions, anti-TTIP organisations such as Campact described the EU Commission's proposals as insufficient.
Above all, it has been criticized that they should not apply to the already negotiated free trade agreement with Canada (Ceta).
Germany's Economic State Secretary Matthias Machnig (SPD), on the other hand, described the plans as great progress.
The Commission took into account key points in the reform that Federal Minister Sigmar Gabriel worked out with other EU trade ministers in the spring.
The old system of private arbitration tribunals is off the table.
The French government also welcomed Malmström's plans.
The European business association BusinessEurope warned against restricting the possibilities of lawsuits of the economy through too many new rules.
Small and medium-sized enterprises in particular could be disadvantaged by this, it said.
When talks on the TTIP (Transatlantic Trade and Investment Partnership) can be concluded remained unclear on Wednesday.
Negotiations have been ongoing since mid-2013.
However, the issue of investor protection has not yet been discussed with the United States.
Originally, a framework for the agreement should already stand at the end of this year.
This appointment is considered no longer tenable.
Delicate folk by Sufjan Stevens in Hamburg
Eleven years ago, Sufjan Stevens sits on stage at Cologne's Prime Club (now: Luxor).
Next to him is a flipchart on which the shy-looking folk singer has recorded the picturesque US state of Michigan with felt-tip pen.
The entire audience, about 40 people, is almost seized by Steven's lecture.
Based on the basis of various places, which he draws again and again on the map, what his meticulous and delicately arranged songs report about.
Where they originated and what it looks like there, in his homeland.
Since then, Stevens has proven to be a wonderfully creative lunatic.
"Greetings from Michigan: The Great Lake State", released in 2003, cleverly composed songs are released that are harmoniously very close to American folk of the late Sixties, but can drift into the great opulent at any time.
The rumor arises that "Michigan" would be the first part of a bold series: Each of the 50 U.S. states should be sung in succession.
But already in 2004 and in the vicinity of the Rhine "Seven Swans" has already appeared and breaks positively certain expectations.
Reduced Stevens performs here.
Live is enough guitar and banjo for him.
A year later, the New-York-based musician returns to the series.
"Illinois" ("Sufjan Stevens Invites You To: Come on Feel The Illinoise"), a widely acclaimed masterpiece was released in 2005.
Exuberant ideas and stylistic finesse.
The album enters the American billboard charts and is not lacking in any annual leaderboard of the influential music media.
After extremely productive experiments, Stevens then uses very extensive electronic offset pieces on "The Age of Adz" (2010).
This sixth decent studio album is still broadly orchestrated, but worn by programmed sequences or beats and plays with all sorts of effects and reverberation.
The stage shows here have become a colourful spectacle of neon headbands and large fluorescent stage decoration.
Sufjan Stevens is currently touring Germany as part of a five-piece band.
In the dark hamburger More!
Theater he mainly presents "Carrie & Lowell", which has been available since March.
Although almost 2000 guests now sit captivated in their chairs here, the much mature Stevens returns to intimacy and foolk.
Without addressing a word to the audience, the group of excellent musicians plays their way through the broadly enriched album.
From the audience you can hear: The lecture is "opulenter" and you can throw away the "plate basically".
Only an encore Stevens thanks modestly, acoustically plays few older songs and finally bows with roaring applause.
Jeremy Corbyn's debut at Prime Minister's Question Time
Since his election, Mr Corbyn's debut is eagerly awaited at Prime Minister's Question Time
New Labour leader Jeremy Corbyn is scheduled to make his debut at the prime minister's question time and line up with David Cameron for the first time.
Mr Corbyn will rise up to ask the first of his six given questions shortly after noon, with his performance likely to be examined by the media and Labour MPs up close.
He called for "less theaters and more facts" at this weekly event.
He has also said that he could skip some sessions to leave them to colleagues.
The encounter will be the first parliamentary scrutiny for Mr Corbyn's leadership, because it comes after his appointment as a member of the shadow cabinet and after his speech at the TUC anniversary meeting on Tuesday.
In the meantime, the Labour leader's decision to remain in silence on Tuesday while singing the national anthem at a commemorative of the 75th anniversary of the Battle of Britain has provoked criticism from a number of Tory MPs and is at the heart of several front-page stories in the newspapers.
Mr Corbyn's decision not to sing the national anthem has attracted attention
A spokesman for Mr Corbyn said he had "been in respectful silence" and he had acknowledged the "heroism of the Royal Air Force in the aerial battle for England."
But a member of Mr Corbyn's shadow cabinet, Owen Smith, recounted on the BBC's Two Newsnight programme that he had advised Labour chairman to sing the national anthem "independent" of his belief that the monarchy should be abolished.
Nearly a dozen shadow ministers have refused to serve in Mr Corbyn's top squad, and in doing so they invoke differences in economics, defence and foreign policy, while less thand a sixth of the Parliamentary Party originally supported him as leader.
Political correspondent Robin Brant says political differences are "piling up" even within Labour following Mr Corbyn's remarks on the position on the European Union and limiting profits.
Mr Corbyn told the TUC conference that Labour was making changes to remove the whole idea of a limitation altogether.
Hours later, Mr Smith, the shadow labour and pensions minister, said the party was "very clear" that it was only against the government plans to lower the £26,000 limit to £23,000.
Mr Corbyn will be the fifth Labour leader to face David Cameron ever since becoming A Tory leader in Parliament in the last decade.
The Labour leader, who has promised a different policy approach, says he has "crowd-sourced" ideas for questions to Mr Cameron and has been given more than 30,000 suggestions.
The Islington North MP has said the prime minister's question time is too confrontational, and that he will refrain from both "strokiness" and arguing with barbs and has instead set out to focus on serious issues such as poverty, inequality and the challenges facing young people.
Mr Corbyn has said Angela Eagle, the shadow economics minister will represent him if he doesn't participate - for example, when Mr Cameron is travelling abroad.
He has also brought the idea into the conversation that other colleagues occasionally take the floor, and that he approached John Bercow, the Speaker of the House of Commons, to discuss the issue.
When he became leader in 2005, Mr Cameron said he wanted to move away from politics in the style of a "Kasperle theatre," which is often linked to the prime minister's question time. But a few years later, he acknowledged that he had failed in it.
Since it was first broadcast on television in 1990, Question Time with the Prime Minister was regarded as the most important barometer of the verdict of a leader and his leadership in the House of Commons and his position among his parliamentary colleagues, although critics have argued that it has become a caricature and needed far-reaching reforms.
'Shot in Joburg': Homeless young people trained as photographers
The city center of Johannesburg is a hard-hitting place if you're homeless.
But a group of former street children has found a way to learn a skill and earn a living with it.
"I was shot in Joburg" is a non-charitable studio that teaches homeless young people how to take photos of their surroundings and make a profit from them.
BBC News has met one of the project's first graduates.
How to share your stories, pictures and videos with BBC News
Every day BBC News - on television, on the radio and online - bring you the latest stories from all over the world... but what we want to hear are the topics that matter to you.
The role you play in creating the messages is very important.
