Pound rises due to accelerated wage growth in United Kingdom
The pound surged sharply, according to dates indicating an increase in wage growth in the UK's wage growth. At the same time, the FTSE 100 was boosted by the SABMiller bidding target.
Between May and July, earnings excluding bonuses in the United Kingdom grew by 2.9% compared to the same period last year.
This percentage has been the quickest and provided assessments since 2009 about the timing of an interest rate increase.
The pound rose more than 1% against the dollar to $1,5505 and had climbed to €1.3730 at €0.84% against the euro.
The FTSE 100 index closed with a 1.49% increase to 6,229.21.
SABMiller was the outstanding stock in the market and made a 20% jump after takeover offer from rival AB InBev.
A transaction would combine the world's two largest breweries, bringing together brands such as Budweiser, Stella Artois, Peroni and Grolsch.
Burberry shares were 0.9% higher after encouraging news from competition for luxury goods Richemont.
Richemont's latest sales beat all expectations and also showed that sales in China - a key market for Burberry - are reporting growth again.
Shares of Glencore rose 5% after the mining giant said it took $2.5bn through a stock sale as part of its debt cut strategy.
Shares of packaging and paper company Mondi were the biggest losers on the index, down 4.3% after Goldman Sachs set its assessment on "selling" for the company's rating.
JD Sports rose 3.1% after the sportswear company reported a pre-ta-tax result of 46.6 million for the half-year.
JD Sports' chief executive says higher wages jeopardize expansion.
JD Sports managing director Peter Cowgill says a higher minimum wage for UK workers could mean "more purchasing power in the pockets of potential customers."
But it is unlikely that this purchasing power will offset the higher labor costs at his company, he says.
Costs could hit JD Sports' expansion plans, he added, and that could mean there are fewer additional jobs.
Justin Bieber in the capital: On Bieber expedition in Berlin
