For the advertisement in the advertising break of the Superbowl, he conceded three million, a two-year deal with an anti-pimple company flushed many, many dollars into the Bieber account.
And with every doll that looks like him and goes over the counter, Bieber also collects.
When Justin Bieber drinks a coffee, you're amazed at the window pane.
He is said to have captured more than 100 million.
Some say the hype goes too far.
Others stress that Bieber is at #1 in the U.S. and the UK with his new single "What do you mean?"
Bieber himself, whose fourth album is scheduled to follow in November, don't interest the many zeros at first.
At least that's how he pretends.
Pegida poster puts Lebanese on potato kebab idea
Potatoes or kebabs?
It's done both - and that's in the potato kebab developed by Oldenburg takeaway owner Hani Alhay.
Inspired by the saying "potato instead of kebabs" on a poster of the Pegida movement, Alhay put potato and meat together on a skewer.
A poster at a Pegida demonstration with the inscription "Potatoes instead of kebabs," which he happened to see on television, gave him the idea.
Since then, a barbecue skewer has been spinning in his store, stocked with meat and potatoes.
"That's multiculturalism for the stomach," Alhay says.
In the meantime, the Lebanese has registered the idea with the patent office in Munich.
According to the authority, it has recently been protected as a utility model.
Alhay doesn’t want to reveal how much meat and how much potatoes the skewer contains.
Only so much: "It tastes even better together," he finds.
Culinary international understanding is also well received by its customers.
Metcash launches down-to-earthness campaign to tackle Aldi incursion
Metcash is trying to protect the IGA stores from an Aldi march in South Australia and Western Australia.
Grocery wholesaler Metcash has launched a down-to-earthiness campaign to defend IGA retailer's IGA retailers in South Australia and Western Australia in the run-up to a £700m Aldi invasion.
While Aldi prepares the opening of two distribution centers and the first 120 stores in South Australia and Western Australia early next year, Metcash and IGA supermarkets strengthen their relationships with local food suppliers and build the IGA brand through mainstream marketing and marketing via social media, as well as through events such as Adelaide show.
IGA dealers and suppliers are jumping on board, with brands such as ice cream maker Simmo from Dunsborough, which distributes rehearsales in IGA stores and shopkeepers who sell discounted tickets for the South Australian Football League to loyal customers.
Metcash is expected to release more details of its defense strategy on September 29, when the food, alcohol and equipment wholesaler conducts its annual investor strategy day.
There's a lot at stake as South Australia and Western Australia account for almost 30 per cent of Metcash's IGA stores, generating higher profit margins than Metcash's IGA network in eastern states.
Buyers in South Australia and Western Australia are notoriously provincial and IGA retailers have a much stronger market share than in New South Wales, Victoria and Queensland - more than 30 per cent in South Australia and 24 per cent in Western Australia compared to around 14 per cent in the Eastern states.
However, analysts such as Morgan Stanley believe that Aldi will snatch away about five percent of the food market in these two states twelve months after opening its first markets and will take sales away from all existing players, but mainly self-employed retailers.
According to a report released this week, analyst Tom Kierath of Morgan Stanley estimated that IGA retailers could lose $360 million in revenue a year and Metcash $250 million by the end of 2016.
This foresign is based on the assumption that Aldi will open 25 stores in SA and 30 in WA next year and generate sales of $500 million and $600 million in each market.
The loss in the food and grocery business, and volumes on Metcash's high fixed-cost basis, could cost Metcash around $13 million in earnings before interest and taxes, Mr Kierath said.
At the same time, Metcash is losing market share in the eastern states, mostly to Aldi and Coles, although a recent survey by Roy Morgan found that IGA buyers are the most loyal.
Morgan Stanley assumes Metcash's sales in the food and grocery business will fell 3.8 per cent in 2016 by 4.1 percent in 2017 and 2.1 per cent in 2018, reducing market share from 14 per cent in 2015 to 10.3 per cent by 2020.
Last week, leading fund manager Hamish Douglass, the founder of Magellan Financial Group, said at a business dinner in Melbourne that Metcash is likely to disappear within a decade.
Metcash declined to respond publicly to Mr Douglass' comments.
But it's anticipated that a Metcash board member made contact with Mr Douglass last week.
IGA's market share has fallen from 18 percent to 14 percent since 2010, and income from Metcash's food and grocery trade, which supplies about 1,200 IGA retailers, has fallen 43 per cent since 2012, reflecting operational debt mitigation with simultaneous pressure on sales and margins.
However, the wholesaler fights back, investing more than $150 million to match sales prices with Coles and Woolworths on hundreds of products every week and IGA retailers to improve their stores through faster-growing fresh food and deliver a better selection of private-labeling food.
Metcash's chief executive Ian Morrice says price adjustments and store renovation programs will increase in vigour and hopes the wholesaler will return to earnings growth in 2017 for the first time in four years.
Thanasi Kokkinakis is backed by Australian Tennis Federation President Steve Healy
Thanasi Kokkinakis deserves praise rather than criticism for his behaviour.
Thanasi Kokkinakis was one of the "collateral damages" in the recent storm surrounding his friend Nick Kyrgios and, according to the president of Australia's Steve Healy, deserves praise rather than criticism for his own behaviour.
Kokkinakis, the younger of the talented duo, repeatedly referred to as "Special Ks," came into the spotlight through Kyrgio's notorious altercation with Stan Wawrinka at the Montreal Masters.
In a fiery qualifying match in Cincinnati soon afterwards, Kokkinakis and his opponent Ryan Harrison had to be separated twice by the referee, with Harrison exclaiming: "Wawrinka would have had to beat Kyrgios, and I was supposed to beat the kid."
While he was last seen smashing a tennis racket after a cramp thwarted a potential U.S. Open victory by Richard Gasquet, Kokkinakis was steadfastly defended by Healy.
The teenager is currently in Glasgow, competing with Sam Groth for second place in singles behind Bernard Tomic in the Davis Cup semi-final against Great Britain.
"One thing I'm pretty empiful about is the fact that, in my view, Thanasi was drawn a little into the dispute," Healy said.
He broke a racket when he lost because of a cramp into the U.S. Open, but he's not the first player to break a racket out of frustration with himself, and I know Thanasi well enough to know he's not proud of it.
But, really, he behaved extremely well in the U.S. Open, even under the rather provocative circumstances with Harrison and so on, and I think he deserves a lot of recognition.
He's got a great team around him, he's got a great family around him, and I believe he's been treated a little too harshly.
He's a very mature 19-year-old, he's working very hard, and he's fitted into the team and so on, he's never had a problem with the other players, he's respected in the locker room, he's well in shape, he's a very stable and matured young guy, and I believe he deserves to be judged by his own merits.
Healy also supported the decision not to consider Kyrgios in the important preliminary decision, which begins on Friday, and he describes the "common decision" as the right one for everyone, but he assures the man from Canberra to return to the team as a long-term integral part."
"At first, we wish the team was completely focused on the competition and not on all the controveries all around [Kyrgios]," Healy said.
And I believe that Nick very quickly made the ascension, to which people like Federer and Murray have pointed out. He rises in the eyes of the public, and he's made some bad mistakes and deserves all the criticism he's getting, but we're trying to support him, we're trying to make him understand the consequences of his actions understandable. Some time out of the spotlight where he can only think it's appropriate, and it's best for the squad.
Europe's refugee crisis: Hungary declares state of emergency, closes the border, withhelds refugees
Hungary declares a state of emergency and cordoned off the southern border with Serbia on Tuesday and detained all those who tried to enter the country illegally. All with the aim of stopping the influx of migrants.
Chaos followed at the border when hundreds of migrants accumulated in a no-man's-land and Serb officials reacted with outrage.
The people who had fleded from violence stuck for an unknown time in a strip of land on the road between the two checkpoints of the countries and built tents and settled there.
But frustrations increased.
When a police helicopter hovered above them, the migrants chanted "Open the border!" and shouted insults against Hungarian riot police.
Some dismissed food and water in protest.
Serbia's foreign minister declared it was "unacceptable" that migrants would be sent back from Hungary, while more and more arriving from Macedonia and Greece.
(Serbia) wants to be part of the solution and not collateral damage.
Talks with Brussels and other countries must take place in the coming days," Ivica Dacic said in Prague.
The turmoil on the Hungarian-Serbian border did not come a day after the 28-nations bloc at a contentious meeting in Bruss could not agree on a common immigration policy.
However, the ministers agreed to share responsibility for 40,000 people seeking refuge in surprised Italy and Greece. They expressed their hope that eventually - next month or by the end of the year - an agreement would be reached that EU states would take in another 120,000 refugees, including some from Hungary.
German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Austria called for a special European Union summit on Tuesday to discuss the continent's immigration crisis.
Hungary, however, did not place its hopes for an early action by Brussels or from its neighbours.
Tuesday's declared emergency in two southern regions gave authorities more powers to deal with the crisis so they could cordon off roads and speed up asylum procedures.
Hungarian Foreign Minister Peter Szijjarto, meanwhile, said his country was also planning the construction of a barbed wire fence along its border with Romania to stop the influx of migrants through the country - after it ended a fence on the Serbian border.
CBS News correspondent Charlie D'Agata reported that even prison occupants were deployed in the race to create the barbed wire fence between Hungary and Serbia.
In recent months, Hungary has become a significant bottleneck for many war refugees from Syria and elsewhere in the Middle East and the entry point into the European Union.
However, Prime Minister Viktor Orban has insisteded that most of the incoming economic refugees who are looking for a better life, rather than war refugees who are entitled to protection - a view that is strongly at odds with other EU states, including Germany.
The new laws that came into force at midnight in Hungary make a crime the crossing of the border or damaging the 4-meter (13-foot-high fence that the government built along the southern border with Serbia. These laws also set higher prison sentences for convicted human traffickers.
"Due to the situation caused by mass migration, the Hungarian government declares the state of emergency," government spokesman Zoltan Kovac's told reporters in the southern city of Szeged.
We say it very clearly: Illegal border crossing is a crime.
Parliament has yet to procedurally agree to the deployment of the military, which is expected for next week, but reporters from Associated Press have already seen heavily armed soldiers at the border with vehicles and dogs for days.
Gyorgy Bakondi, country's security adviser for Orban, said authorities 45 people while trying to cross the border, 15 others had already penetrated deeper into the country.
They come over by damaging the fence, they are now in police custody and are accused of committing criminal offences under the new laws.
Authorities are about to repair the fence quickly.
Hungarian officials also closed two of the seven border crossings with Serbia on Tuesday morning after a train carriage carrying barbed wire arrived to close the borders.
Chaos surrounded the most important border crossing near Roszke, Hungary, when the Hungarians opened a small office in a metal container to process people's data and squeeze a crowd to push themselves into it.
About 20 managed to get inside, but thousands remained outside.
Another group of migrants blocked Serbia and Hungary's main motorway connection and said they would deny food and water until they were allowed to enter Hungary.
Election-2016: Hillary Clinton's lead over Bernie Sanders halved in national poll
Former U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton continues to hold a double-figure lead in the Democratic race for the nomination at the national level, but Senator Bernie Sanders is gaininging ground against her.
Clinton now has the support of 47 percent of Democratic voters in the primaries (58 percent previously), while Sanders ranks second, with 27 percent (rising 17 percent).
Vice President Joe Biden, who has yet to announce whether he is running for president, receives support from 15 percent of Democratic voters in the primaries.
More than five months before primaries start, most Democratic voters say it's too early to say their opinion is certain which candidate they will support.
But Clinton's supporters are more consistent in her election than those of the other candidates.
Since the CBS news poll, Clinton has lost to a number of demographic groups, while Sanders made some gains.
Clinton holds a big lead in women and moderates, but these protrusions have diminished.
Their men's support has dropped significantly, and Sanders is only 5 points behind here.
Clinton's advantage among Democratic voters under the age of 50 has evaporated, and she and Sanders are now at the same level.
Clinton, however, still has a big lead among older voters.
Last month, Clinton was ahead of Sanders with a double-figure lead at the Liberals, but Sanders now has a 5-point lead at this group.
About half of Democratic voters in the primaries nationwide say they'd enthusiastically support Clinton if she were nominated by the party.
A quarter would support her with some caveats and another 15 percent would only support her because she is the candidate.
Six percent wouldn't support them.
Perhaps unsurprisingly that Clinton's current supporters are especially excited about her.
The views are more mixed among those who don't currently support Clinton - only about a quarter would enthusiastically support them.
While 48 percent of Democratic voters in the primaries say they support Clinton enthusiastically, fewer Republican voters say this about their front-runner Donald Trump (35 percent).
When Clinton's supporters are asked in an open-ended question why they want her to be the candidate, the most common answer is that she has the right experience (16 percent), followed by the view that it's time for a woman to become president (13 percent) and that she's the best candidate for the job (10 percent).
Clinton's supporters also mention their association with Bill Clinton (9 percent).
Approvacent for dealing with the problems and eligibility are also cited.
Since the vice president throws a potential for the presidential office into the balance, 57 percent of Democratic voters of the primaries want to see him in the race - even though a third believe he shouldn't.
When Democratic voters of the primaries are asked who would be their second election for the party, Biden is at the top.
Of Clinton's supporters, more than half say Biden would be her second choice well ahead of Sanders.
Deciding Biden not to run for the Democrats' nomination, Hillary Clinton's lead above Sanders, from 20 points with Biden in the race in the race without him in the race.
Fifty-five percent of Democratic voters see Clinton as a candidate with the best chances of winning the election; however, that figure of 78 percent has dropped last month.
Biden is by far a second in this measurement, but the percentage that considers him to be the "electable" has increased.
Still, when asked what's more important in a candidate, Democratic voters call questions about eligibility.
Similar to last month, not a single Democratic candidate or potential candidate stands out as unacceptable for these voters of the primaries.
The Democratic candidates, or potential candidates, provide good results on some key traits among voters of their party's primaries.
Majorities view Clinton, Sanders and Biden as honest and trustworthy and endowed with strong leadership skills.
Biden scores best in honesty among the three candidates, while Clinton's strength is the lead.
Thirty percent of Democratic voters don't believe Clinton is honest and trustworthy, twice as many say that about Sanders (15 percent), but 27 percent don't have an opinion of him.
Democratic voters of primaries also generally see Clinton, Sanders and Biden ideologically in accordance with their views.
With all of these measurements, about a quarter of Democratic voters have no opinion of Sanders.
Since questions about Hillary Clinton's use of a personal email address and a server continue to be asked during his term as secretary of state, most Democratic voters of the primaries are satisfied with their explanation of the matter, saying it hasn't influenced their opinions overall.
But it's a different story in the American public overall.
Half of Americans are now unhappy with their declaration, while only about a third are satisfied.
Public opinion on this was divided in March, shortly after the problem came to light.
Most Americans said the email controversy had no impact on their general opinions of Clinton, although 36 percent said their opinion of her had deteriorated.
It's mostly Republicans, more than six in 10, who say their views of Clinton have gone worse.
Although Clinton is seen by her party's constituents as honest and trustworthy, this continues to be a problem for her among registered voters overall: Only 32 percent see her as honest and trustworthy, eight percent less than last month and 15 points less since May.
62 percent of eligible voters don't consider them an honest and trustworthy person.
At the same time, 55 percent of eligible voters nationwide also view Trump as unhonesty and untrustworthy.
Clinton and Trump, however, get good marks on leadership.
Of the six candidates tested, Biden, Carson and Bush perform best on honesty.
A majority see Sanders rather than honest, but as with Carson, many have no opinion of him.
The error rate for the sample of 351 Democratic voters in the primaries is 6 percentage points.
Ben Carson advances in statewide poll close behind Donald Trump
Ben Carson sits closely behind Donald Trump at CBS News/New York Time's latest poll
The two candidates, meanwhile, account for half of voters' support in the new poll, and others are dramatically losing hold
Republican voters nationwide continue to support Trump in large numbers. Support is growing 23% from 23% in Tuesday's final CBS poll conducted before Fox News debate last month.
But Carson shot from 6% to 23% over the same period.
The poll also found Carson is well received by demographic groups, surpassing Trump among Republican voters with higher education.
The rest of the field, with the exception of Carly Fiorina and Ohio Governor John Kasich, has teered off with Wisconsin's Governor Scott Walker, who has fallen the deepest, from 10% support last month to now 2%.
Former Florida Governor Jeb Bush, former Arkansas Governor Mike Huckabee and Florida Senator Marco Rubio are in third place with 6%.
The margin of error in the sample of 376 people by Republican voters in this latest poll is plus or minus 6% and was carried out in September.
The latest CNN/ORC poll, released last week, shows Carson's rising in the polls - he's landing at 19% support among Republicans, behind the Trump with 32% support.
ABC News/Washington Post's latest poll also showed Trump well ahead of Carson.
Looking for a Carson rising in the last week, Trump founded some side blows for the former neurosurgeon, even after Carson apologised for questioning Trump's faith.
"We need energy," Trump said last Saturday during a campaign appearance in Iowa.
Pleasant bathing temperatures: The eastern Mediterranean is currently up to 29 degrees warm.
Even if peak values are only measured in the distance, a trip to the Mediterranean is enough for beach holidaymakers to find still warm water.
A swim in the North or Baltic Sea should be a rather short pleasure at a maximum of 17 degrees.
Some people don’t go into the sea anymore: The water has a maximum of 18 degrees in the North Sea this week, the German Weather Service (DWD) announces.
Bathing temperatures, on the other hand, continue to offer the Mediterranean: in the east it even reaches up to 29 degrees.
In the Adriatic it is at the top 25 degrees, in France 23 degrees.
The Red Sea in Egypt reaches 28 degrees.
Five Brotherhood members charged with the death of a Baruch College newbie
Almost two years after a student died during a harassmentable ritual of a student compound in Pocono Mountains, Pennsylvania, murder charges are filed against five people.
Members of New York's Baruch College Pi-Delta-Psi Brotherhood brutally attacked Chun "Michael" Deng during an introductory ritual in September 2013 and delayed his transport to the hospital, which contributed to his death, police said.
Officials said he had been blindfolded with a 30-pound backpack full of sand had been repeatedly assaulted while attempting to approach a target via a frozen field.
Mr. Deng finally complained of headaches, then fell unconscious.
Members of the Pi-Delta-Psi Brotherhood did not call the emergency number 911 after Deng fainted and allegedly waited an hour before taking him to a hospital, according to police.
However, they had the time to change Deng's clothes, googling his symptoms, and Andy Meng contacting the Brotherhood's national president to seek advice, Police Chief Chris Wagner said at a news conference.
"At this point, the members started hiding utensils and fundamentally put the welfare of the Brotherhood above that of Michael Deng," police chief Wagner said.
He could not be resuscitated, and he died of his injuries the next morning.
Following an autopsy report, Deng was repeatedly inflicted injuries to his head, trunk and thigh with blunt force.
"Too many families have been destroyed by the Brotherhood's harassment. At least one student has died from the harassment every year since 1970, Douglas Fierberg, a lawyer representing Deng's family, said in a statement.
Brotherhoods and their members must be held accountable, and this move by authorities is very important.
Monroe's county grand jury recommended a third-degree murder charge for the first five members.
Thirty-two members of the Brotherhood, including Mr Meng, are also charged with harassing attacks and forming a criminal association.
"We not only want to blame the people involved, but if there is an organization that - officially or unofficially - has sanctioned this, we will also hold these accountable," Wagner said at a news conference on Tuesday.
Baruch College banned the Brotherhood, and the National Brotherhood has revoked its affiliation with the local group.
That's what's going to happen when the Fed raises rates
A rate hike is coming, and the home will falter, yields on bonds will rise, and the economy will slide into a recession.
That's what we know.
What we don't know is how long all this will last and how long it will last.
In particular, the story offers little guidance for the economy about the process.
A recession came within just 11 months after the first interest rate hike and lasted 86 months.
The Federal Reserve's aggressiveness in raising interest rates is often, though not always, a factor in how the economy and financial assets are responding.
Therefore, representatives of the U.S. central bank have emphasized so fiercely that investors should not focus on when it begins to raise interest rates, but rather on the period needed for normalization.
In fact, several variables play a role.
In the end, however, market participants might find that all the fuss may have been exaggerated because of a rate hike.
"The Fed's first increase since the global financial crisis will inevitably be interpreted by some as a signal for the end of the "cheap money" era," Julian Jessop, chief economist at Capital Economics, said in a note to clients.
In contrast, we don't expect U.S. interest to the gradual return of U.S. interests to normal heights, but still at too low levels to lead to seismic shock that many seem to fear.
However, that doesn't mean there won't be an impact.
Here's a look based on historical trends on how some areas of the economy might react:
As the market has seen in the last month, anticipating interest rate hikes can make things volatile for a while.
Once the increase arrives, the influence isn't as dramatic.
"There's apparently a tendency for asset income to be choked off 12 to 24 months after the initial increase, perhaps reflecting the delay in monetary policy again," Deutsche Bank analysts said in a more recent study on what's happening after interest rate hikes by the Fed.
More specifically, over the past 35 years, the market is mostly on the upward trend - about 14 per cent - on the road to interest rate, quite flat in the 250 days after (average gain of 2.6 per cent), then back to normal after 500 days, with an average return in the past six past cycles of 14.4 percent, according to a recent analysis that Bob Doll, chief investment strategist at Nuveen Asset Management, published at Barron's.
Deutsche Bank said the impact on the shares tends to occur later in the raising cycle and the yield is starting to diminish.
Recessions are a fact of economic life, but interest rate hikes often encourage them.
In the current case, the Fed is faced with some conditions that didn't exist before and that could accelerate a recession.
What’s highly remarkable here is that gross domestic product will be close to its lowest point with a Fed rate hike.
According to Deutsche Bank, nominal GDP was below 4.5 per cent only twice at 118 rate hikes since 1950.
Although the second quarter of 2015 stood at 3.6 percent, few expect this to continue, expectinging only 1.5 percent for the third-quarter, according to the Atlanta Fed.
Rate hikes at such a fragile economic backdrop could be risky and raise the question "whether it's different this time," Deutsche Bank said.
Market participants have been preparing for a Fed's rate hike throughout the year, and all eyes have been on the Federal Open Market Committee meeting this week, which ends Thursday.
While many strategists and economists believe the FOMC could approve an increase in this session, futures trading only indicates a 25 percent probability.
"In our study, all increase cycles from 1950 to the present have occurred in a super-cycle with increasing momentum in GDP and dwarf pre-recession heights very quickly after the start of the recovery," the report said.
In contrast, this is a clearly slow recovery from what was the worst recession in the observed phase.
The current cycle is by far the longest the Fed has waited since the end of the last recession; the record has been 35 months, and this has been 74 months so far.
The fixed income was also volatile as the market anticipates a rate hike, and the pattern is quite similar to that in the capital market.
The main difference is that the effect on bonds is faster than with stocks, if they change their strategic course.
In the case of bonds, the yields seem to change direction as soon as the first increase/reduction takes place in the cycle.
At the end of the increase cycle, bond yields fall immediately, Deutsche Bank said.
Charles Schwab's strategists believe the rise in interest rates will cause yields between longer and shorter bonds to move closer together and lead to a flattering of the curve.
High-yielding bonds often bring better yields in such a climate, although "we're still cautious about expecting higher yields," Kathy Jones, fixed-income analyst at Schwab, said in an analysis.
What's interesting, Jones thinks, markets have already adapted to Fed strategy expectations.
"In addition to the strength of the dollar and the decline in inflation expectations, there are some signs that the market has adapted to the prospect of stricter monetary policy," she wrote.
Short-term interest rates have risen, the yield curve is flatter, credit margins have widened and volatility has risen - all features of the market if the Fed tightens its strategy.
By and large, companies that handle the greater part of their operations in the U.S. will benefit as interest rates rise and local products become more attractive.
Multinational companies with high debt will be worse off as a rising dollar makes their products more expensive in the global market and also the financing of their debts.
"History shows that "Quality" stocks tend to react extremely positively three months after an initial rate hike," Goldman Sachs analysts said in a report.
Companies with a strong balance sheet have surpassed companies on average by 5 percentage points after each interest rate hikes in 1994, 1999 and 2004.
Companies with high capital returns as well as low volatility in their shares also exceeded their low-quality counterparts by an averaging 4 (percentage points) and 3 (percentage points).
Debt becomes a big problem.
Businesses with a high share of flexible interest rates will lose the most, Goldman said.
Outside pure stock games, consumers will also benefit from the rising dollar.
Savers could see gains through higher yields, even if experts disagree on how quickly that's going to happen.
BBC Radio 3 is a copy of Classic FM, says Member of Parliament Andrew Bingham
Conservative member of parliament criticised the broadcaster for its "dumbing down"
He argues that he is increasingly indistinguishable from commercial rivals.
He also targeted the BBC Music Awards, saying they only imitated The Brits
Parliament colleague Damien Collins attacked The Voice and said that this was not very original.
BBC Radio 3 "is "shifting" to Classic FM, claiming a leading member of parliament and increasing new pressure on the broadcaster to prove it is doing something that private broadcasters aren't doing.
Conservative MP Andrew Bingham also told the BBC bosses that the public service radio station was "dumbling down" and is increasingly no longer distinguishable from its commercial competitors.
"Radio 3 doesn't seem - I don't like to use the word 'dumb down' - but it seems to turn into Classic FM," he said.
He also targeted the BBC Music Awards, which kicked off last December.
The BBC Music Awards, which launched last year, The Brits appear to be just under a different name.
So if you're trying to done something else - these are two spontaneous examples where you're actually imitating what's already in the market," Mr Bingham said.
BBC director-general dismissed the allegations, arguing that BBC Radio 3 was well ahead of its commercial competitors when it comes to live music and enlightenment of the public.
What I'm learning from Radio 3, I won't learn about classical music anywhere else.
And there's a commitment to live music and making music that's really unsurpassed.
"And Radio 3's commitment to finding new music and commissioning new works, I also really think it's important," he said.
Earlier this year, BBC Radio 3 controller Alan Davey argued that harder work had to be done to engage the audience than it did in the past because Brits are less educated about classical music.
However, Radio 3 wasn't the only element of BBC production that came under fire during the hearing in the Committee on Culture, Media and Sport for kicking commercial rivals.
Tory MP Damien Collins attacked The Voice - the BBC's musical talent show bought by Dutch producer Talpa Media for an estimated cost of £22 million.
Do you see why this program is often debated because it isn't original and not particularly distinctive?
Lord Hall insisted that the BBC 1 show - with Paloma Faith and Boy George as judge - was produced "in a special BBC way."
He added that he wishes the company would "find a hit from our in-house stable" the next time it launches an entertainment series.
However, he refused to rule out the ordering of further ranks of The Voice in the future or to spend money by the fee-payers on other entertainment formats.
Internet sales rose the slowest in August and Black Friday may be responsible
In the first three months of 2015, there was a seven percent increase in online sales.
In the last month there was the lowest result since records began in 2000
Online sales intensified three years ago through the use of mobile phones for shopping
Experts believe Black Friday in November slowed spending
For over a decade, virtual shopping has boomed, and many of Britain's main shopping streets have turned into ghost towns.
Yet experts say there are now signs that online sales are stagnating after new figures showed only seven percent in the first three months of 2015.
Total spending for August presented the lowest increase in growth since records began in 2000 with only a five per cent increase year-on-year.
Experts say there are signs that online sales are stagnating after new figures indicated only seven per cent growth in the first three months of 2015
"If you're looking at growth rates for previous years and going back to 2000, then that's always evolved over time," Andy Mulcahy, editor of the IMRG sales index, told the Guardian.
He explained that online sales intensified three years ago when shoppers started making purchases with their phones.
It meaned that more people could shop online and that people could do this in different environments, such as at home on the sofa.
But now that's leveling out, and we'll possibly see growth diminish.
The Black Friday phenomenon, which was imported from America, is cited as one reason for the slackening sales.
Experts believe shoppers are holding back their purchases ahead of this event, which takes place on the last Friday in November.
Last year, the average number of shoppers gathered around their computers to make their bargains five times, and some retailer websites crashed.
Experts believe Black Friday, where shoppers fought each other because of bargains, could stop shoppers from making purchases.
A study by the British Retail Consortium founded that slightly more than £1 was spended online from £5.
For some products, such as televisions and washing machines, more than a third of purchases are now carried out via web shops.
At the same time, more than a quarter of people buy clothes and shoes online rather than the fashion chains in the inner cities.
Muslims bury those killed in Mecca in crane accident - bin Laden's family is sanctioned
Bodies of 29 dead are carried through the streets by Muslims at first burials in Mecca
Saudi Arabia blames the collapse in part on the huge Saudi bin Laden group
The Ministry of Health said a total of 394 people were injured after the crane crashed
Hundreds of Muslims gathered when 29 of the victims were carried through the streets to bury those who were killed when a crane collapsed at Mecca's large mosque.
Mourners, dressed largely in white, filled the route to pay their respects when the first of the dead embarked on their last journey to Al-Moaissem Cemetery.
The funeral procession passed by the Sacred Site, where only days earlier a construction crane plunged through the ceiling of the mosque and then upon the prayers.
The bodies were cleared for the funeral when the Saudi king imposed a sanction over the Bin Laden family's construction company because of the disaster, which also left 394 injured.
The bodies of 29 prayers that were killed when a crane crashed through Mecca's Great Central Mosque were carried through the streets.
Among the dead was Qasim Akram from Bolton, Greater Manchester, father of four, who was on his first pilgrimage when the crane crashed down.
Mr Akram was at the Grand Mosque with his parents before the start of the annual Hajj.
Saudi officials have already firmly pledged that the Hajj will move to Mecca with thousands who will continue to come to the city.
Today King Salman said the bin Laden group shouldn't have left the crane's arm when it wasn't used.
An Arabic version of the decree, published by the state-run Saudi Press Agency, said that the bin Laden group was co-responsible for the collapse.
The news agency's English-language service did not report this detail, but said the company's leadership was prohibited from travelling abroad.
Earlier this week, Salman vowed to revealed what caused the fall of the crane that plunged into a courtyard of thousands of Muslims gathered in front of the Hajj, which takes place later this month.
The Bin-Laden group did not issue any statements about the collapse of the crane, and its representatives were not available for comment.
The Royal Decree also rebuked the group because of non-current security measures and because it did not cooperate with meteorologists.
Dozens of cranes surround Mecca's large mosque as part of the massive construction project led by the bin Laden group.
The Bin Laden family has been associated with Saudi Arabia's ruling family for decades and undertakes larger construction projects.
Al Qaeda's late leader, Osama bin Laden, was an apostate son expropriated by the family in the 1990's.
The crane's crash came in the midst of strong winds and rain, just days before the arrival of millions in the kingdom for the pilgrimage required at least once in the life of every healthy Muslim.
Officials said 158 people remain in hospital.
Government officials ordered 1 million rial (£174,000) to be paid to the relatives of those killed and the same sum to permanently injured.
Other injured receive half of that amount.
B&Q Boss Says the Time of Cheap Eastern European Craftsmen is Over
Britons are increasingly employing craftsmen, says Veronique Laury
The number of qualified craftsmen from Eastern Europe have passed
As a result, 200 more Screwfix stores targeting craftsmen are opened
At the same time, Kingfisher will close 60 B&Q outlets nationwide
By Rupert Steiner, City chief correspondent of the Daily Mail
It is alleged Britons put down their hammers and brushes in favour of the use of skilled Eastern European craftsmen who carry out the works in their home
If you can cleverly handle a hammer or a brush for painting walls, these days you seem to belong to a minority.
Because Britons are increasingly employing craftsmen to carry out home improvement jobs in their homes, according to the company's chief executive behind B&Q.
Véronique Laury, the new managing director of Kingfisher, who owns the hardware store chain, said that a large increase in the number of skilled craftsmen from Eastern Europe offering services at lowest prices has since become obsolete.
That’s why Kingfisher plans to open 200 more Screwfix shops aimed at craftsmen and closing 60 B&Q outlets.
Mrs Laury said she wanted to employ more women in B&Q shops because eight out of ten purchasing decisions in the store are made by women.
At the moment, the men predominate the employees.
She said it was necessary to face growing demographic trends showing more female single households making more renovations than in the past.
Mrs Laury said: "There's still a majority of men working in our stores.
We need to shift this more strongly towards a balance.
In principle, half of our customers are made up of women with 80 percent of choices about buying items by women.
Women play a big role in the decision-making process.
Mrs Laury, who is one of only five women at the top of a UK leader listed in the FTSE100 Index, has also restructured her top team to reflect an equal number of men and women into leadership positions.
Kingfisher is almost the only companing exactly 50 percent of the team with women, including its management and Chief Financial Officer.
Mrs Laury said: "This was a deliberate decision - diversity is an important issue here.
It was not difficult to find good women for our board of directors.
Kingfisher reported a decline in half-year profit and revenues after the group shouldered the cost of closing the stores.
Due to the trend, Kingfisher, which owns Screwfix and B&Q, will open 200 new stores.
Aich/Dob's officials in court for infidelity
At the regional court of Klagenfurt on Wednesday, two officials of the Austrian volleyball runner-up Aich/Dob had to answer for infidelity and tax evasion.
The defendants pleaded not guilty.
One of them had also been charged with abuse of office.
He was legally acquitted of this accusation in May.
The current process has been adjourned.
According to Chief Prosecutor Robert Riffel, it had emerged in the preliminary investigation that the defendants used funds from club accounts through which they were allowed to dispose in their functions for private purposes in 2006 to 2011.
He estimates the damage at around 400,000 euros.
Sports officials are also charged with financial offences.
This fact was eliminated by the presiditor of the Schöffensenat, Judge Michaela Sanin.
It is to be negotiated at a later date.
In order to denounce infidelity, the defense stated that at no time the club had at any time an asset disadvantage.
The sports officials would only have charged expense allowances and led the sports club, for which they worked from 1982 to 2011 and in which they would have invested a lot of time and money, out of the debt - the liabilities amounted to around 600,000 shillings (around 44,000 euros) in 2001.
"At that time we were faced with the situation: do we continue to run the club or do we close?" one of the defendants, in the bread profession large company auditor of the tax office.
With his colleague and co-defendant, he then decided to take on this task.
With all risks.
"We've shooted up money, laid down our life insurance policies as collateral at the bank," he said at the interrogation.
It has been agreed with the association: you can manage the money freely, only no debts may be made.
In addition, in the case of a corresponding financial success, the payment of the expense compensation had been decided retroactively until 1991.
Therefore, a part of the expense compensation had only been paid out years later, namely from the year 2003, when there had been a plus on the account.
Therefore, flat-rate and non-detalute debits also appeared, the former chairman explained the processes.
The accounts and saving books managed the two defendants together, the second defendant said.
Why the payouts had not been disclosed to the association in detail, the judge asked.
The association was not interested in detail in the financial settlement, only the debt freedom was important, according to the answer.
When investigations for abuse of office began in 2011 against the one functionary, an amount of 120,000 euros was transferred to the association's account.
These were reserves, the defendants said.
This securities account ran in the name of the second defendant and, according to his statement, was not known to the association.
"Why?" the judge wanted to know.
For a long time, the respondent did not know an answer to that.
Then he finally said that he had believed that this was the best for the club.
If anything had happened to him, no one would have known about that money, the prosecutor put in the room.
The co-defendant had been informed, justified the official.
This referral has been worded as "damage indemnity."
Why compensation?, judge and prosecutor wanted to know.
The first defendant then stated that he disagreed with this formulation, because the association had never suffered any damage.
But what am I supposed to do when the lawyer says, "You're all off the hook with him."
"Am I a legal representative?" he asked.
Furthermore, the functionary said that he had never received a cent salary, he had only charged mileage money and diets.
On diets, he had invoiced the association 1,000 euros per month, but the actual effort was much higher, he added.
The defendants could not provide exact documentation for the early years.
These had not been stored after an audit in which everything had been judged correct, they explained.
The second defendant had also received mileage money.
All corrections had been made in cash, he had to drive from his place of residence Völkermarkt to the bank in Bleiburg, he explained in his statement.
The main trial will continue on September 23 with the questioning of witnesses.
Hewlett-Packard cuts another 30,000 jobs
In addition to three-year staff reductions, the computer giant will cut further jobs in the company's splitting.
U.S. computer giant Hewlett-Packard (HP) job cuts have no end.
As part of its restructuring, the U.S. technology group plans to cut 25,000 to 30,000 jobs in its corporate customer division.
As a result, costs of $2.7 billion (€2.4 billion) will be saved, the company said on Tuesday.
The move will result in a special charge of around $2.7 billion, which will be incurred from the fourth quarter.
The three-year reduction of 55,000 jobs has not been completed.
At the end of the 2013/14 financial year, HP still employed around 302,000 people.
After-exchange, Hewlett-Packard shares declined 2.3 percent.
The group from Palo Alto, California, is currently splitting with printers: the computer and printer business is to be bundled in a new company this year - separate from the more promising areas with computer servers and data storage for companies as well as other services.
Both divisions are to be listed on the stock exchange.
Another disappointing PC sales as well as weaker demand from corporate customers for its services, the world's second-largest PC maker after Lenovo.
In the third fiscal quarter at the end of July, sales of PCs and printers declined 11.5 percent.
Revenue fell 8 percent to just under $25.4 billion.
As a result, revenues declined in 15 out of 16 quarters.
HP is respondinging with a split and massive job cuts to change in the industry.
The 76-year-old company, led by Meg Whitman, reacted late to the fact that customers are increasingly buying smartphones and tablets instead of conventional computers and doing more and more business online.
Whitman's restructuring plans envisage the total reduction of around 55,000 jobs.
