The general public can visit the IAA from Saturday.
The organizers of the Auto-Verband VDA expect as two years ago with about 900 000 visitors.
Röszke: Outbreak of violence at the Serbian-Hungarian border
The Hungarian police have used tear gas and water cannons on the Serbian border near Röszke against several hundred angry refugees.
Euronews correspondent Dániel Bozsik witnessed the outbreak of violence at the border checkpoint.
He reported by phone:
A group of refugees broke through the border.
Behind it, a police line had formed and behind the officers stood a water cannon ready.
40 to 50 refugees began throwing rocks, sticks and plastic bottles according to officials.
Officers initially used pepper spray uninterruptedly.
After that, the police used tear gas.
Lots of tear gas was sprayed towards the refugees.
With this, the group was dissolved.
The refugees have now withdrawn somewhat waiting.
A pregnant and apparently injured woman was carried away from the zone.
Children who inhaled the tear gas screamed.
Other refugees slumped and suffered shortness of breath.
Serbian's press officers also suffered injuries from the use of tear gas and pepper spray.
Time to decide on interest rates for the U.S. Federal Reserve
The world observes when the U.S. Federal Reserve comes together on two days, Wednesday and Thursday, to make the important decision on whether or not to raise interest rates for the first time in nearly a decade.
A survey of 80 economists polled by Reuters founded that a little more than half, who still believed last week that the Fed was going to do so, now thinks the Fed will stay a little longer and will keep interest rates at the current level of - 0.25 percent.
It's a historic event because it's been seven years since the Fed set interest rates on NULL.
"For the first time, we're approaching a situation where a major central bank is potentially leaving the zero range and starting a process of normalizing interest rates," said Angel Ubide, a senior employee at Peterson Institute for International Economics.
The U.S. economy is at a relatively good level of performance, trillions of dollars are added to the balance sheet and generate only low inflation.
However, the Fed can't ignore the less rosy outlook on global conditions.
She has warned markets to adjust for an increase, but the signs indicate that the chances against such a move are good.
The decision is to be announced at 20:00 CET on Thursday
Death shooter at Delta State University left apologies
After shooting and killing his girlfriend in Mississippi on Monday morning - and before shooting and killing his colleague later in the day - Shannon Lamb wrote a note to say that he was "sorry" in the first murder and wanted "he could undo it," authorities announced on Tuesday.
Police in Gautier, Mississippi, said Lamb, a lecturer in geography and social science education at Delta State University, called the emergency number at around 10.00 on Monday morning
and said he shot someone and killed someone.
Lamb told the emergency call center employee that the police had to send police officers to his home.
He said "I shot my wife last night" and he also mentioned that there was a "sweet dog" in the house but "won't bothere anyone," but will probably be excited.
The police asked the caller for his name, but he didn't give him.
Lamb gave no explanation for his gunfire.
When police entered the scene, they found the body of Amy Prentiss, 41, and a handwritten note from Lamb, 45, which said: "I'm so sorry, I wanted I could undo it.
“I loved Amy, and she’s the only woman who’d ever loved me.”
After writing the note, Lamb drove 300 miles to Delta State University, where he killed Ethan Schmidt, a professor of history, before finally taking his own life on Monday evening when cops surrounded him.
University President William LaForge said he knew nothing of a conflict between Lamb and Schmidt, but "of course there was something in Mr Lamb's imagination."
Despite reports on Monday that Lamb allegedly believed Prentiss also had a relationship with Schmidt, authorities said on Tuesday there was "no information, no evidence" for a "triangular relationship."
Prentiss was described by Shawn O'Steen, who testified that they had been married for about seven years as a "good person."
The two were divorced 15 years ago but remained friends.
O'Steen said he and Prentiss had a daughter who's 19 and she was "devastated."
He said Prentiss her daughter were "absolutely best friends."
Prentiss worked online from home, according to Linsday Knowles identified by the Sun Herald as a close friend of Prentiss.
Knowles said Prentiss, who owned a dog named Lightning, had been meeting with Lamb for about three years.
"She always wanted to help other people," Knowles said
Police said Lamb had called relatives at a certain point on Monday to tell them "he's not going to jail."
When officers caught up with him on Highway 1 near Greenville later that night, Lamb drove his car to the side, fled on foot, ran into the woods and killed himself with a gunshot.
Lamb received a doctorate from Delta State University in the spring of 2015, according to his life on the university's website.
stars who have lost their lucky charm
