All of this will certainly take its toll.
Eurowings: Lufthansa restructures the Group
Lufthansa is planning a major corporate restructuring.
The Lufthansa Group wants to save half a billion euros a year with a better organization alone.
150 of the world's approximately 1000 executives will cost the group's conversion jobs.
However, there are new negotiations with the pilot's union.
Lufthansa is rebuilding its corporate structure and upgrading the low-cost subsidiary Eurowings.
The goal of the realignment is an earnings contribution of around 500 million euros per year from cost and revenue synergies, the airline said on Wednesday.
Among other things, there will no longer be a board member for the passenger business at Lufthansa in the future.
The low-cost airline Eurowings should be developed as independently as possible.
In the future, Karl Ulrich Garnadt will be responsible for Eurowings in the board team led by CEO Carsten Spohr.
With the realignment, Spohr underlines the importance of Eurowings, with which the Group intends to compete against Ryanair and Easyjet in the future.
To date, the organization of the 120 0000-employee company has been complex.
The airlines operated under the umbrella of the Passage Airline Group, which represents three-quarters of the annual turnover of €30 billion.
New negotiations with the pilots
Negotiations are to resume in the collective bargaining conflict with the pilots' association Cockpit (VC) after the court-stopped strike this Thursday.
This was communicated by the union.
The topic of the talks should be the retirement arrangement for pilots.
Cockpit wants "in a year with a predicted record result" to achieve viable results as quickly as possible.
The conflict between the union and the airline has led to various strikes in recent months.
In addition to the transitional supply of the pilots, the savings concept "Wings" is the background.
It stipulates that in the fight against competitors, Lufthansa outsource routes to the Group's own low-cost line Eurowings.
Under the title "Morgenzug to the South" paintings by Leena Krüger can be seen in the Künstlerhaus.
The Finn, who has long been based in Göttingen, has put together a selection of titleless works for the White Hall of the House, which encourage her viewers to travel mentally.
Manchester United's Luke Shaw can't play for months because of double fracture
Luke Shaw suffered a double fracture of his righ leg in Manchester's Champions League defeat at PSV Eindhoven.
While this caused the left full-back to crying and will exclude him from playing for at least six months, Louis van Gaal isn't giving up hope Shaw can play again this season.
The injury was caused by a ruthless attack by Héctor Moreno in the 15th minute after 20-year-old Shaw slumped into PSV.
He was treated on the field for nine minutes and needed oxygen before being rushed to hospital.
Van Gaal said: "He has a double leg fracture and lies in the hospital."
He'll be moved tomorrow, or maybe a day later, and then operated on in Manchester.
It's terrible - a boy who comes to Manchester United at 18, it's very hard and then plays fantastic, and then that happens.
In the locker room, he had an oxygen mask on.
He was crying.
I'm not a doctor, but if you have a double fracture, it'll take six months, and he won't play in the group stage.
I'm hoping he can play again this season.
Moreno seemed happy to have escaped a red card and imposition of a penalty.
Nicola Rizzoli, the referee, didn't punish the Mexican.
Van Gaal suspected the Italian referee was mistaken.
You can judge for yourself when I say a penalty and a red card were due.
It was a very bad attack with both legs.
A tweet on Shaw's account read: "I thank you all for your messages. Words can't describe how broken I am, my path to recovery begins now, I'll come back stronger."
Manchester United suffer the loss of Luke Shaw in defeat at PSV
The England defender has an extra incentive to recover until next June with the European Championship in France.
Phillip Cocu, the PSV coach, said: "It's a very bad injury."
When a player leaves the pitch with this kind of injury, everyone is very sad about it.
It's never the intention of our players to hurt an opponent.
Moreno suffered a leg fracture when Mexico played against Van Gaal's Holland squad at the 2014 World Cup.
"He's having a very hard time because it wasn't his intention to hurt the opponent, and when you then hear it's a very bad injury, that's not good," Cocu said.
It was not possible for me to see the moment.
The only thing I could see was the ball travelling in a different direction so that it looked like he [Moreno] had touched it, but I couldn't see the attack on Luke.
Speaking to SBS6, Moreno said: "I know how it feels, I know how havy something like this can be.
I'm feeling very bad about it, I'm so sorry.
PSV player Andrés Guardado has also been rushed to hospital - after a foul by Chris Smalling.
Surely David Cameron knows The Lorax better?
It's one thing all parents will soon learn: if your child loves a book, it won't be long before you almost memoriously.
Unless you're maybe David Cameron.
When he was asked by a provider of childcare systems to name his favorite children's book, the prime minister opted for The Lorax, Dr. Seuss' environmental fable published in 1971.
It's been a difficult choice, Cameron writes in a message on SuperCamp's website, and continues: "funny, moving, creative and with a powerful message, it's a book that I like to read to my kids, because there always seems to be a picture or message we've missed so far."
All well and good, except that the following paragraph, in which Cameron allegedly describes the book's plot, instead reproduces a summary of the 2012 film version that added new characters and details to expand the fairly short original text.
"In the midst of the walled city of Thneed-Ville, where all nature has gone and even the air is a commodity, a boy named Ted hopes to win the heart of Audrey, the girl of his dreams by fulfilling her desire to see a real tree," one readies. A love interest was Dr. Seuss, whose real name was Theodor Seuss Geisel and who died in 1991 but completely unknown.
As if that wasn't suspicious enough yet: the text presented by Cameron reads in part like the summary of the Lorax film, which is presented on the Internet Movie Database's website and thus begins: "In the walled town of Thneed-Ville, where everything is artificial and even the air is a commodity, a boy named Ted hopes to win the heart of Audrey, the girl of his dreams."
Although he admits to being a pragmatic father, it seems unlikely that Cameron is so unfamiliar to the plot of the book. Here an unnamed boy hears how the local environment is ravaged by the money-greedy, tree-sleeping, thne-knitting once-ler despite the protests of the eponymous Lorax, a squealing, main-beared creature "speaking in the name of the trees."
Most likely, such a relatively low PR task was delegated to a helper who didn't read The Lorax 20 times in a row to a child.
A Downing Street spokeswoman said the text was likely to have come from Cameron's constituency office.
SuperCamps confirmed that the text was delivered by Cameron's staff but gave no further immediate comments.
Adam Lyth was clearly threatened by exclusion from England's squad, but it's still hard
Encountering Pakistan in the United Arab Emirates is a difficult task, but when you see the squad England chose for the Tour, a repeat of the 3-0 Test series defeat is not on my card.
I wouldn't rule out an away win.
Alastair Cook's side may be less experienced than the one three years ago, but there are good spin players in the batting line-up, and the community spirit is strong; they won't wear scars from 2012, just a youthful-positive attitude.
