Zafar Ansari joins the squad as a back-up spinner, and in my limited dealings with him, he impressed me very much as a young man.
People might look at his stats - an average of 31 with the racket and 35 with the ball - and think it's a punt, but there aren't a large number of backup spinners raising their hands and the coaches have opted for youth.
He's going to learn a lot on this tour.
Aside from the squad, England have made a few smart additions to the coaching staff.
The appointment of Mahela Jayawardene, the Batsman from Sri Lanka, as an adviser for the Test matches could be a Masters coup.
He's one of the nicest guys in the game who will bring a wealth of experience as he's played in all conditions and against all attacks.
Paul Collingwood comes in for the white ball phase of the tour and brings energy and a powerful effort into the set-up.
I've always felt he's created for the international cricket competition, as a player or as a coach, and like Mahela, he'll have insights that we can benefit from.
Sometimes new voices in the dressing room can bring fresh air, even if the news is in line with what has been said.
Colly's team-mate at Durham, Ben Stokes, isn't used at the one-dayers, Root received a similar break against Australia and for my part don't have a problem with it.
Some won't agree, coaches and team presenters don't determine the schedule.
And you have to remember that, as with the players, their jobs depend on the results.
Such decisions are not made easily.
The Brexit (Brexit = Britain's exit from the EU) could gain ground if the economy gets into a doldrums by 2017.
David Cameron's unsophisticated renegotiation strategy to keep Britain in the EU will get deeper into trouble with each passing day, even without the choice of ingenious Jeremy "Is it yes or no?" Corbyn the leader of Labour or the TUC, which fiercely quarreled over Europe on Tuesday.
Foreigners also have their Corbyns and Nigel Farages.
A European diplomat friend said the other day that some member states, as deeply entangled in various crises as the EU, and not always out of its own guilt, Britain's self-created agenda with divided concessions and disruptive treaty changes, which they mostly don't want.
In Brighton, the TUC decided at its annual meeting that it did not like them if they endangered social protection.
We might as well call it Dave and George's selfie agenda.
Prime ministers and finance ministers climb at summits or bilateral meetings in foreign capitals, take a picture of themselves in front of a famous local monument, with or without local sizes, then come home and say they win allies for reforms.
It's always about me.
My European friend, who wants an outward-looking Britain inside the EU tent - "Don't let us alone with France," Berlin says - and lets it see through that more and more short-sighted ministers and civil servants in some capitals have come to the point where they don't care whether we stay or go.
Germany's cautiously calm Chancellor Angela Merkel is an ally, but she has been in power for 10 years and wants to leave office before it looks bad for her, as has happened with the exaggerately long-long perseverants like Thatcher (11-year-old), Mitterrand (14) and Kohl (16).
France's François Hollande looks at his re-election or his replacement in 2017.
He's also a fading force.
Poland may soon have a nationalist right-wing government, Spain a left-wing one.
It's hard to imagine they're going to rip a leg to help the selfie-Brits, whose energy should be better used to prevent Scotland's exit from the UK - something they also don't want because most have similar separatist tendencies.
Therefore, some such ministers may even intentionally plan unhelpful remarks, entirely in the SNP style, while Britain's 2017 referendum campaign to speed up the Brexit procedure, my friend suspects.
"The renegotiation was a bust" and "Britain has gained nothing of value from renegotiation."
Europeans know how predatory Fleet Street can be at its expense, and what EU sceptics the oligarchy of press barons is, as they were during Tony Benn-inspired referendum in 1975, which supported our entry in 1973 with a 2:1 ratio.
You may weakly remember Boris Johnson, the arch-euro opportunist who made a name for himself as an "EU-straight banana" Brussels correspondent in the early 90's.
With all his madness, Ed Miliband knew who his enemies were.
These included the medley of tax-fearing villains, phone-hacking foreigners, and the pseudo of non-internals who own most of our major newspapers.
Actually, Tony Blair thought similarly about them, but found that if you're sitting with a hot gorilla in a cage, you'd better distribute bananas to distract him rather than become his lunch and dinner.
In fact, Miliband was more Blairian than Blair himself when he defied Cameron's swirls of referendum, because Blair had put the principle above the EU's unpassed constitution, which was then rejected by French and Dutch voters in 2005, who thus opened a backdoor for him.
The press has never forgiven him that either.
But the nice Jeremy Corbyn, who gave me a friendly slap on the back at Westminster on Monday - I told him he didn't need to squander any more time for me - seems in jeopardise of repeating Neil Kinnock's boycott Murdoch's strategy of the 80's, a mistake then and now.
It may take, but he has to come up with a coherent strategy to win over the media, not with me, but certainly with Andrew Marr, whose BBBC sofa he left on Sunday.
Corbyn's official position on Europe is - I express it kindly - in development.
He says that we should stay in a reformed EU.
Well, most people outside Ukip's traditional blue-dye tendency and the wilder climes of Tory's Little-Engländerism, anyone can probably say yes to that.
It's what Hilary "A Benn, but No Bennite," Benn said on radio and television this week, when the scout struggled within him not to admition that Corbyn's appointment of provocative John McDonnell as shadow chancellor was a bad idea.
It reminded me of the 70's when his old father on television struggled to be disloyal towards his Labour cabinet colleagues without going too far and losing his cabinet job.
Hilary's struggle was even more attractive.
But struggling to stay "in all circumstances" - Benn's expression - wasn't what McDonnell said, nor what JC told his fellow contestants and deputies after all the reports at their close-up on Monday night.
Whatever Corbyn's toxic bottom-up image wants to convey - keep in mind that he's even touched me - news discipline remains important in the age of TV around the clock and, above all, the whirlpool of social media, a hotbed of paranoia and betrayal.
It's right not to give Cameron a free ticket to Europe that allows him to take Labour's support in the referendum campaign a matter of course, as McDonnell said.
Simon Jenkins thinks like that.
But McDonnell and Corbyn's fundamental ambiguities on EU membership - in line with their Bennite siege economy, their anti-capitalist past - is no longer just a matter of esoteric discussion among left-wing dissidents, for which narcissism of small difference is a central principle of dogmatic disputes.
As with his ambiguities to Nato - Tom Watson is firmly convinced that JC won't launch a campaign to leave - foreign diplomats will be required to try to find a sense in everything for their superiors at home.
Why should Paris or Berlin waste political capital - they also have suspicious voters - to make concessions to the Britons when this can all be pointless?
The U.S. is already quite disappointed by the weak military performance in Iraq, Libya and Syria, even with a cost-cutting by the Tory government in power, which I was reminded of what British Influence Security Forum called itself at the launch on Monday.
Experts criticize Public Health England report on e-cigarettes
Claims by a state-funded agency that e-cigarettes are less harmful than smoking originated from a meeting of 12 people, partly with links to the tobacco industry, researchers said.
Experts who write in the British Medical Journal (BMJ) joined Lancet by criticising the evidence used by Public Health England (PHE) in their report on e-cigarettes.
PHE published the "principle report" last month, describing it as a "comprehensive review of the evidence."
However, some researchers have questioned the resilience of the data, pointing to the links between some experts and the tobacco industry.
An editorial in the medical journal Lancet last month attacked the "extraordinarily windy foundation," on which PHE established its most important conclusion.
Two other researchers write in BMJ and have asked if the claims "build on stone or sand."
Martin McKee, professor of European public health at the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine and Simon Capewell, professor of clinical epidemiology at the University of Liverpool, said: "A fundamental principle of public health is that strategies should be based on demonstrating effectiveness."
They said the public expects PHE's assertion that "the current best estimate that e-cigarettes are about 95% less harmful than smoking" are based on a detailed review of evidence and modeling.
In fact, this statement comes from a single meeting of 12 people convened to develop a multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA) to lead their opinions on the damage of various nicotine-containing products to a synthesis; the results of the meeting were summarized in a research report.
McKee and Capewell said that a sponsor of the meeting was a company called EuroSwiss Health, whose managing director is said to have previously received funding from British American Tobacco (BAT) for an independent study.
He also signed BAT's references on public health in a sustainability report, they said.
One of the 12 participants in the meeting said it would receive funds from an e-cigarette maker, but not the funding he is said to have received, according to reports from tobacco company Philip Morris International, they added.
The reasons for the selection of members of the panel will not be delivered, but that includes several well-known e-cigarette champions, some of which also indicate industry funding in the paper.
Some other participants in this session are not known for their know-how in tobacco control.
PHE's tobacco management also attended at the meeting.
The group's "significantically admits" there's a lack of "tough evidence of the damages of most products on most criteria," McKee and Capewell wrote.
However, none of these connections or restrictions are discussed in the PHE report.
McKee and Capewell said PHE's claims that "there's no evidence so far that e-cigarettes serve as a pathway to smoking for children or non-smokers" were premature.
Prof Kevin Fenton, director of health and well-being at PHE, said BMJ's claims had already been responding beforehand.
He said: "E-cigarettes are substantially less harmful than smoking."
One in every second lifew smoker dies from their addiction.
All the evidence suggests the health risks of e-cigarettes are low by comparison, but that we continue to have the long-term effects to study.
PHE has a clear obligation to inform the public about what the evidence proves and does not, especially if there is so much public confusion about the relative dangers compared to tobacco.
Nearly 80,000 people die annually from a disease related to smoking, and smoking costs £2 billion a year the National Health System.
By clearly presenting current evidence that e-cigarettes aren’t without risk, but represent only a fraction of the harm caused by smooking, we fulfill our national jurisdiction.
BBC should abandon EastEnders, says former Channel 4 chef
Former Channel 4 chairman Luke Johnson has said the BBC should stop broadcasting programs like EastEnders and The Voice. He said commercial rivals would "give their right arm" for hit shows such as the Albert Square Soap.
Johnson said the broadcasting fee should only be used to make the kind of programs not broadcast by other UK broadcasters.
He criticised the BBC for placing public-law programmes likely seen by a smaller audience into hard-controversial windows of opportunity against the most popular shows of their rivals and by broadcasters such as BBC4.
After BBC director-general, Tony Hall, said the company wanted to do more in partnership with other organisations, Johnson said he had spent six years without success trying to come into contact with the BBC.
"I have problems with the idea that BBC is commissioning or broadcasting programmes that would also be clearly shown by unsubsidised rivals," Johnson told the Oberhaus communications committee as part of his inquiry into the BBC's future.
If you want to hold the broadcasting fee, which I don't think is a good idea in the medium or long term, you should at least get your production on programs that others don't do or commission.
They are sending The Voice in a world-class window of opportunity because they want to achieve the broadest possible support for their future.
The idea that no one else would show this is a joke, it's ridiculous, it's nonsense of course.
Johnson said: "I don't see why a regressive tax funding programs that could also be funded without subsidies.
The Voice is one such program, EastEnders is another, admittedly both in-house productions.
Honestly, other channels would give their right arm for it.
I'm trying to see how this is supposed to be public television.
I don't think this would diminish the BBC at all.
Johnson, an entrepreneur for series who ran Channel 4 from 2004 to 2010, said the BBC was to blame for the hunt for ratings "because the ambitions of the people who work there and because they believe this is the best way to secure long-term support."
His commentary on the BBC's most popular broadcasts reflects what Sky's former director Tony Ball suggested in his MacTaggart lecture in Edinburgh in 2003 that the BBBC should sell shows like EastEnders.
Johnson said that the rather public-law niche programs "should "go to BBC4 where you can't quite measure the audience, but they're fulfilling their duties, and they can argue when they're going on their hypocrimate missions to justify €4 billion of broadcasting fees, "Well, of course, we've created all of these obscure programs that no one's seen."
They put them in a window of opportunity where nobody ever wanted to see them.
Commentingating on the broadcast fee, Johnson told the committee: "I urge you to find a more regressive system in terms of conditions who can get the best value out of it.
Of course you all think, we all think you're getting great value here for £150 per year, but for people who is a lot of money for the £150, ask me if that's true, because they don't consume much from the BBC.
Former Sky and Channel 5 boss David Elstein, who also testified before Tuesday's committee, demanded that the BBC should review its Reithian mission to "inform, educate and entertain."
He said very little of the BBC production would now be officially classified as an education - he said it was 1% of its TV production and 0.1% on the radio - and she should take an example at public television stations in Australia and Canada to "informing, enlightening and entertaining."
On the question of partnerships, Elstein said the BBC should do more in areas such as those of the Proms, which he said that only a handful will be shown on television, and with the commercial broadcaster alliances to make everything available online.
'Sonogenetics' can control brain cells through sound waves
The video shows the nematodes changing the direction at the moment they are irradiated with sound pulses.
Scientists have bred worms with genetically modified nervous systems that can be steered by outbreaks of sound waves.
The tiny nematodes change the direction at the moment they are irradiated with sound impulses, which have too high frequencies for human hearing to be heard.
The pulses work by turning on motoneuron cells that are genetically modified to carry the membrane channels that respond to ultrasonic waves.
More on this topic: Researchers read and write brain activity with light
The researchers said the worms showed the power of a new procedure, called Sonogenetics, in which ultrasound can be used to activate a number of brain, heart and muscle cells outside the body.
Sreekanth Chalasani, a researcher at the Salk Institute in La Jolla, California, told the Guardian that the procedure could one day replace deep-brain stimulation, an invasive procedure that releases electrical impulses into people's brains to treat the symptoms of Parkinson's disease.
Threadworms usually don't respond to ultrasound, but Chalasani found that they did if they were surrounded by a liquid with microscopic air bubbles.
The blisters, he found, amplify the ultrasonic waves, which then penetrate into the worms.
The amplified ultrasonic waves act on structures called TRP-4 ion channels and found in the membranes of some of the worm cells.
The sound waves cause the opening of these ion channels and activate the cells to which they are assigned, according to a Nature Communications report.
To create ultrasound-guided nematodes, Chalasani genetically altered the worms in such a way that some of their motor neurons carry TRP-4 ion channels.
When he applies ultrasound to the altered creatures, the sound waves are amplified by the microbubbles and transferred to the wormers, where they turn on the changed motor neurons.
The procedure has some similarities with Optogenetics, a groundbreaking tool that allows scientists to turn neurons on and off with pulses of light.
But Chalasani said Sonogenetics might have some advantages over this technique.
In contrast to light, which has to be transmitted at an optical fiber to the desired location in the brain, low-frequency ultrasonic waves can pass tissues unhindered and can thus be sent into the brain from above on the skull.
"We believe that with gene therapy and a therapeutic virus it is possible to temporarily make the targeted human neurons susceptible to the ultrasound signal in a clinical setting for certain neurological treatments," Chalasani said.
Other applications could focus on muscle cells and insulin-producing cells, he added.
Illegal torture devices - right on your doorstep!
The ads shame Britain's arms trade
Amnesty International Anti-Weapons Fair Video.
Terrible Murdered Drones!
Ankle shattering leg cuffs!
Cluster bombs.
And electric shock sticks that cause excruciating pain, but leave no trace!
The cheerful voice and cartoon images leave no doubt that for the world's largest arms fair is a combative parody produced by Amnesty International to put the British government's readiness to move guns to repressive repressive regimes via the Defence and Security Equipment International Jambore at the ExCeL Centre in London's Docklands.
DSEI Weapons Fair: Authoritarian regimes flock to London
But a separate campaign also leads to many posters that can be seen at bus stops and on the subway trains and that require a second look.
