Transport for London doesn't find this postering "vandalism" funny.
One of the parodie ads produced by Special Patrol Group.
After Gavin Grindon, curator of Crueol Designs and lecturer at the University of Essex, artists become more critical and try to bring political works to the audience outside of galleries.
Many artists get to work with activists within protest movements, noticing that they have to move outside into the real world so that their art has an impact on society.
Another of the anti-arms trading posters greets commuters on the subway.
The Amnesty video was viewed more than 100,000 times in 24 hours via Facebook and originated through the advertising agency VCCP, which works non-professibly with the graphic artist and voiceover artist who also works for free.
VCCP creative director Matt Lever was amazed at the silencence that prevailed around advertising for the Arms Fair - through a vacancy of the ExCeL calendar - and thought, "Let's give them the campaign they're trying to avoid."
They played with the invention of a more realistic shady company advertising, but "by blazing with bright colors and animation, it remains sticking more strongly."
"They've done a fantastic job by helping us raise awareness about the sale of illegal torture devices at the fair," says Tom Davies of Amnesty.
What we need now is an intervention by the British government and the guarantee that torture equipment will not be traded illegally on our doorstep.
Perhaps the most creative art around the arms fair comes from the area within ExCeL, where exhibitors are spending fantastically euphemistic products.
In a press release, Saab discloses an order from the U.S. Army about "the AT4CS RS shot down from the shoulder."
This includes a "uniquivalently shaped battlehead that delivers prominent after-effect effects inside the target."
Whatever that does, it won't be pleasurable.
Eight golden rules for living in student dorms
Eleven years ago, as a self-forgotten 19-year-old, I moved into a student dormitory, still believing in Beret's and moral relativism, but it's only now I remember the tense smiles of those I've annoyed - and the numerous, long-breathed complaints about others.
Most I've learned can be distilled into eight simple rules.
The entry into student dormitories ranges from slightly nerve-wracking to absolutely terrifying.
It's temptating to chat: people tell where you're coming from, whether and where you've taken sabbatical year, where you're standing in relation to Corbyn and why you're "honestly don't love or hate Marmite because seriously, guys, it's just a yeast extract HAHAHA, I'm in my room."
Take a breath.
And help others do the same - ask questions and show interest.
It directs the conversation and helps you figure out the people that you like as opposed to those you wouldn't talk to, even if your eyelashes were on fire and these fire extinguishers.
Leave people.
People's rooms often turn into drinks receptions, movie-night venues, and community meeting places, but they're also the places where people dress and their underwear.
Referred to: Don't Think about starting a relationship at university
Introverts are given rough treatment at the university.
Your room should be a refuge, but many student dormitories form a room-to-room strolling culture that is friendly and social and can lead to lifelong friendship.
This can be hell for someone trying to recover.
Nothing in the culture allows you to say, "I like you, and I want our friendship to keep going, but you have to go away so I get back the energy I've used to interact with you."
Try to feel when a fellow student is exhausted by conviviality - and send him a text message instead of knocking.
Speaking of knocking: do it.
A guy in my student halls of residence had the notion that an unlocked door was an invitation.
He saw a few things.
Making friends through baking.
Beginninging the university at the height of back-mania, knocking on the doors of people to introduce yourself with a plate full of brownies is less strange than welcoming the 1950s "Welcome to the neighborhood, I've baked something for you. So, and now please smile when I ask you a few personal questions."
Offering homemade treats is a good way to make friends.
Your roommates feel obligated to conversing and may end up your face associate with a delicious surprise.
Of course, this depends on whether you're a decent baker.
Poorly mixed lumps of flour, sugar, and fat cause people to put themselves asleep when you knock.
Stealing food is a common crime in student dorms.
Of course, stealinging chicken is a theft, but is it taking a drop of milk for your tea too?
Verify this with the dairy owner.
Chances are that they say to serve themselves, even if they're cooking inside.
If someone steals your stuff, that's annoying, and you have my sympathy - but try to deal with it more balanced than my roommate, who added a detergent to her milk to punish thieves.
She's in prison now.
You shouldn't make any noise that keeps people away from sleeping or at work.
It's not just about being a decent person, it's that you're, in the truest sense of proverbs, of the people sitting in the glass house.
A girl in my hallway asked her neighbors if they would mind making drum & bass a little quieter, because it was three o'clock in the morning, and she had an exam in the morning.
They reacted to it with even louder music.
When they finally stopped at 5 a.m., she kindly let her neighbors sleep for another two hours before she bleaked her own music, locked her doors and embarked on her three-hour exam.
Wouldn't it be romantic if your future spouse would randomly move into the same dormitory?
Conversely, it wouldn't be abysmally embarrassing if you had to hold a roommate for your future spouse, get close to her again, and then have to see her every day, and perhaps witness how she pulls over all the mutual friends?
Of course, the heart goes its own ways - but if a hot roommate is definitely not your future spouse, at least try to persuade your abdomen a little sanity.
In the thrilling moment you noticing that someone has left their keys in their unlocked room, the boundary between an ulk and bullying is finer than it seems.
It's a good prank, but it's only taking moments for him to become a boomerang.
Teach a valuable and amusing lesson about safety by hiding - but never snort someone's room with toothpaste and shampoo.
Close your door and place your keys in a glass of water, which you then put in the freezer - but do not staple their possessions on the ceiling more than twice.
Terraced houses for refugees, single parents, low-income earners
The real estate entrepreneur Markus Gildner builds terraced houses in Eckental in central Franconia for people who don’t have a lot of money.
For example, for refugees - but also as a signal: "Not only refugees need living space, but also the single mother and the low-income earner."
"I'll show you how you can still build cheaply and well today," the 44-year-old says in the interview
Thousands of refugees will push into the housing market.
The entrepreneur Markus Gildner thinks he knows how quickly cheaper housing can be created: He builds terraced houses for refugees in a project.
Mr Gildner, many refugees will push into the housing market.
What solution do you offer?
Markus Gildner: The mass accommodation in containers or gymnasiums has no sustainability.
Because the people stay here to a large extent.
In view of the flow of refugees, we will also need sufficient housing in the coming decades.
A very banal, three-storey terraced house is the solution.
Gildner: A terraced house needs the least space.
It facilitates integration.
This is different when you put people in apartment blocks.
Each of the six houses, which are currently being built in the pilot project, consists of three housing units for four asylum seekers each.
Each unit has two bedrooms, a bathroom, a kitchen and a common room.
It may be that many refugees will say it's peace again, I'm going back.
Then the houses can be easily rebuilt according to the respective needs.
What's the cost?
Gildner: The price for 160 square meters of living space per house amounts to 184 000 euros, excluding land.
It's manageable.
The construction took six months.
It's appropriate.
Have the building codes complicated their plans?
Gildner: In terms of construction law, I went through my project correctly.
It always makes particularly complicated the threatening sword of Damocles special construction and the parking space regulation.
Creepy.
It is stilled that whoever manages three apartments in a terraced house needs at least three pitches.
Only in the cities lacks the space for it.
Refugees and asylum seekers don’t have cars.
How did local residents respond to their terraced house project?
Gildner: There were some who accused me of creating luxury buildings for asylum seekers.
Which's definitely not true.
But aren't youghening the competition for affordable housing?
Gildner: It’s true that we’ve had a construction boom in Germany in the last five years, but luxury apartments have been built that hardly anyone can afford.
My project has the message, even ordinary people need housing.
Not only refugees, but also the single mother or low-income earner.
I will show how you can still build cheaply and well today.
That's what it's about.
Markus Gildner (44) studied microsystem technology in Regensburg and initially dealt with environmental analyses.
iOS 9: How to get the new iPhone operating system when Apple update is released
The new operating system can be requested by going to the Settings app, tapping "General" and then selecting "Software Update."
When it's ready, the phone gives you the option to download and then install.
Some have encountered problems after installation because they get an error message that indicates that the "software update failed".
To get around the issue, users are advised to either wait for the rush to recede or try to update through iTunes.
The phone itself will guide you through the rest of the process.
That can potentially mean deleting apps to make room - but Apple has made that more unlikely by slimmering the update. Likewise, it's going to be easier to do this if you use a special tool to delete apps and then reinstall them once the process is complete.
It's worth making sure your mobile phone is charged during the update or is near a charger so that the battery is charged.
If the charge is too low, Apple won't allow the update to continue, as the phone may turn off halfway and this could lead to a termination of the update.
