For three days now, Panama's authorities have been rippinging the 4500 plants and burning them.
Men beat 17-year-old Afghan
Witnesses are looking for the police for an incident in Bad Soden-Salmünster: On Friday evening, around 23.45 o'clock, three young men were walking from Bad Soden in the direction of Salmünster.
According to the police, one of the group, a 17-year-old Afghan, followed his two friends at a distance of about five metres and shouted that he should be waited.
At that time, the group was at the height of the bft petrol station.
There were also three people on the premises of the gas station.
One of these men screamed at the younger and slapped him in the fist in the face.
The victim suffered a nasal fracture and bleed heavily from the nose.
When his two friends rushed in and spoke appeasingly to the thug, he first let go of his victim.
From his friends, the injured young man has now been brought home.
The perpetrator tracked the group with a car up to their apartment and demonstratively drove by several times.
When the vehicle could no longer be seen, the injured person went outside the building to smoke a cigarette.
There he was attacked again by the thug and another male person.
With the help of a baton, the victim's face and thighs were punched.
The injured had to be taken to a hospital for medical care.
So far there is only a vague perpetrator description.
One of the men was about 1.80 metres tall.
The second racket is said to have been about 1.65 metres tall and was dressed in jeans and a brown-and-white shirt.
Police are asking witnesses and whistleblowers to come forward by calling 06181/100-123.
Dr. Frank Müller takes over Praxis Felstehausen and invests €310000
Silvia Müller-Gongoll and Frank Müller (from left) take over the practice of Karl-Heinrich and Ursel Felstehausen.
The mayor Bernd Bormann, business promoter Uta Seim-Schwartz and Asendorf’s mayor Heinfried Kabbert are also pleased about this.
In many places in the countryside, doctors are missing - Asendorf, on the other hand, is lucky: Dr. Frank Müller will take over the Felstehausen practice there on 1 October.
While Ursel Felstehausen is retiring, her husband Karl-Heinrich will still stand by his successor in the next few months.
After a conversion phase, Müller reopens the practice on 12 October, until then he is investing 310000 euros in the premises at St.-Marcellusstraße 6.
It was sympathy at first sight, when the Felstehausens got to knew Frank Müller and his wife Dr. Silvia Müller-Gongoll years ago, who is currently completing her specialist training in the Verden hospital and also wants to enter the practice next year.
"We've always had in the back of our minds that the Müllers would fit well into this practice," says Dr Ursel Felstehausen, laughing.
“When it happened, I just called her.”
The married couple Müller liked the idea of running their own practice in Asendorf in the future.
"The proximity and constant contact with the patients are very attractive for us," says 39-year-old Müller.
“We want to get to know and accompany the families over the years.”
As an advantage, he also sees to be his own boss soon.
And so it was agreed, the Müllers bought the house with the practice.
However, they will continue to live in Bruchhausen-Vilsen.
The two-time family man Frank Müller also grew up there, his medical studies in Hanover followed by wards in several doctor's offices as well as in the hospitals in Sulingen, Nienburg and Rotenburg.
He is currently working as a senior physician in Sulingen, and in 2009 he was appointed a senior emergency physician in the district of Diepholz and volunteers in the DRK readiness Bruchhausen-Vilsen.
From 19 September, the practice will be prepared for the new boss.
While surgeon Dr. Karl-Heinrich Felstehausen works as a family doctor, his wife Ursel (internist) specializes in gastroenterology.
The Müllers, both internists, want to settle down as family doctors.
"That's why endoscopy comes out," says 38-year-old Silvia Müller-Gongoll.
After the conversion, the rooms will look more modern.
We will offer more ultrasonic examinations as well as long-term and stress ECGs and need a consultation room more.
In addition, the computer is completely renewed.
Müllers will receive a grant of a total of 50000 euros as part of a funding programme from the district (25000 euros) as well as from the velvet municipality (12500 euros) and the municipality (12500 euros).
"This shows how proud and glad we are that the practice will continue," emphasizes velvet community mayor Bernd Bormann.
According to Silvia Müller-Gongoll, about 2000 patients come to Felstehausen practice every quarter, compared to about 500 in northern Germany.
There is yet another family doctor's office in Asendorf.
A positive side effect of Frank Müller’s practice takeover: According to Ursel Felstehausen, the pharmacy remains in Asendorf.
If no successor had been found, she would have left.
By the way, if you visit Müller’s office hours, you continue to look into familiar faces above all: take over the seven medical assistants and the cleaner from the Felstehausens.
With their farewell, an era ends in Asendorf, after all, the physicians have practiced there for 30 years.
"In the beginning we only had two rooms," Ursel Felstehausen recalls with a chuckle.
She already feels a little wistfulness after a long time.
"But we continue to live in Hohenmoor and will certainly see many patients often," says the internist.
Her husband adds with a laugh: "We have a 6000-square-foot property."
“So far I’ve always pushed myself before gardening, now it could be different.”
In addition, the couple has two grandchildren and likes to travel.
Croatia: "We're letting the refugees through"
"Now border controls will also be ramped up at our southern border," Interior Minister Johanna Mikl-Leitner said.
The checks at the Hungarian border remain true in parallel.
It is still clear that anyone who wants to apply for asylum can also make this "of course".
The supply and safety of people is in the foreground.
But it is also clear "that it needs clear signals," said the minister.
People need to know that we don't accept borderless migratory flows, even across the Austro-Slovenian border.
It needs a controlled approach.
Croatia considers 'corridor' - Slovenia denied
The Croatian government had previously announced that Slovenia also wants to allow arriving refugees to Austria and Germany and possibly also build corridors for the refugees.
However, Interior Minister Vesna Györkös Znidar did not see it that way in Ljubljana.
The idea of corridors is "absolutely unacceptable" and Slovenia will intensify the monitoring of the border - especially with Hungary.
On the other hand, the border with Croatia is already heavily controlled as an external border of the Schengen area.
As the minister pointed out, however, Slovenia does not want to lock down its borders for refugees.
"Each EU member has to fulfil their commitments, otherwise the system cannot work," Györkös Znidar stressed.
As the Croatian government announced, the first refugees had already trieded to enter Eastern Croatia at the Tovarnik/Sid border crossing in Eastern Croatia in order to dodge registration.
The Croatian police were able to pick them up in the nearby corn fields.
According to local media, police vehicles with new refugees have been constantly arriving in Tovarnik, Croatia.
These are mainly Syrians and Afghans, including numerous women and children.
Emergency services and the Red Cross are also already in use.
In the coming days, his country expects around 4000 refugees, Interior Minister Ranko Ostojic said.
In addition, buses from Presevo on the Macedonian-Serbian border, which had previously brought migrants to the Hungarian border, would be diverted towards Croatia, Croatian and Serbian media reported on Wednesday.
However, the government in Belgrade denied that it is to instruct people to take certain routes: "Neither can we, nor do we want to do so," it said.
According to the media reports, a first bus with around 50 refugees arrived in the Serbian border town of Sid in the morning, in the course of the day, further buses are expected.
Via Croatia, Slovenia and Austria to Germany
Occupants had been travelling through Serbia all night from the Macedonian border, some 500 kilometres away.
"We've heard that Hungary was closing, so the police told us to drive here," one of the refugees said.
They wanted to travel through Croatia, Slovenia and Austria further to Germany.
This could actually lead to a slightly further west to the postponement of the previous Balkan route Turkey-Greece-Serbia-Hungary, already predicted by observers.
Croatia: "Comprehensive possibilities for border control"
Croatia's Interior Minister Ostojic had previously stressed that in the event of a strong influx of refugees, his country had prepared corresponding plans for possible exceptional situations.
He did not give details, but he pointed out that Croatia, as a non-Schengen member, had "comprehensive options for border control."
Strict checks are carried out in order to qualify for membership in the Schengen area.
Slovenia has belonged to this since 2007, the EU partners have consistently issued a good certificate to the country as far as the standard of border controls.
On the Serbian side, there are still about 300 refugees behind the Hungarian border fence.
According to Belgrade media reports, they would have spent the past night at the Horgos border crossing, which leads to Röszke.
According to the Hungarian authorities, the Horgos and Backi Breg border crossings remain completely closed.
Whether the remaining refugees now want to travel to the Croatian border is still unclear.
Serbian Interior Minister Nebojsa Stefanovic tried to reassure Wednesday morning at a local appearance in Horgos: He considers the debates on alternative routes of refugees, after the Hungarian border is now closed, to be premature.
It wouldn't be very high refugee numbers at the moment.
One could not yet say whether they would continue their journey via Hungary, Croatia or any other country.
Helpers advise migrants - dangerous - alternative routes
However, private Hungarian refugee workers have already begun to distribute maps to the refugees on which alternative routes through Croatia are drawn.
However, the border crossing between Serbia and Croatia is not dangerous: in parts of the border area there are still unvacated minefields 20 years after the end of the Croatian war.
On Wednesday afternoon, Croatian police announced that a team of mine clearers had been sent in the border region.
