Trials against 32 other defendants in the case continued.
Most of the defendants are from Ukraine and Georgia.
The perpetrators are said to have acteded in both the United States and Eastern Europe.
Ecumenical Hospice Aid Mannheim has existed for 20 years
There is a lot of activity around Mannheim’s Paradeplatz.
People handle their errands, sometimes quick steps, sometimes comfortably strolling.
They drive to the two stations of the Mannheim Motor Show at Paradeplatz.
Railways hold.
Pulsating, blazing life with wonderful autumn sunshine and in the middle of it four large panels each with the headline "Before I want to die"...
With this action, Ecumenical Hospice Relief drew attention to its 20th anniversary and is confronted with mixed reactions.
"It's about life, that's why we went into the middle of the city with the action in the middle of the city and among the people," says Josefine Lammer.
The head of Ecumenical Hospice Relief is available to passers-by for questions and conversations as well as the volunteers.
While some passers-by spontaneously reaches for the chalk and supplements the sentence by "acquiring my life", "being able to read", "driving through the USA", "stoping the war" or "don't suffer for a long time", others stop, read, say sentences such as "I'm not that far yet" and then move on.
Again and again, small discussions about the action itself develop, about life, the handling of dying.
Peter Hübinger is also currently on site.
"Only if I realize that the end of this world will come for me at some point can I live responsibly," says the director of the Diakonisches Werk Mannheim, which together with the Caritas Association is the carrier of Ecumenical Hospice Relief.
Therefore, this publicity action towards the 20-year-old is good.
However, it is understandable that not everyone on the boards wants to communicate publicly, after all, it is a very personal matter.
According to Josefine Lammer’s experience of death and dying, although the hospice movement in recent years has contributed a lot to opening people.
An assessment that Angelika Godefroid shares.
The death and grief attendant is one of the volunteers from the first hour.
Confronted with the topic through personal experience, she read a lot of literature and decided to get involved in euthanasia.
When the Ecumenical Hospice Aid was looking for volunteers by call, it contacted immediately and was present at its foundation in 1995.
Godefroid belongs to a team of 45 volunteers - 43 women and two men - that accompanies sufferers and relatives in private households, hospitals, nursing homes and in the Sankt Vincent hospice.
They're all carefully trained.
Angelika Godefroid can deal well with her task and the experiences.
"I see life from a different perspective, consider it precious and appreciate the moment," she says.
The offer of the outpatient Ecumenical Hospice Service is free of charge and non-denominational.
The financial support of health insurance companies and donations from relatives and associations makes it possible to provide training as well as further education and supervision of the dying and mourning companions.
Every two years, training courses for new employees are offered.
The plaques described on Saturday are now on display in the house of the evangelical church (M1, 1a) and in the house of the Catholic Church (F2, 6).
Further personal requests can also be recorded on postcards there.
FedEx earnings mixed, financial outlook for 2016 lowered
FedEx reports disappointing results for the final quarter, and the delivery giant lowered its profit outlook to a full year because of weaker demand for freight services and higher costs in its ground transportation service.
The firm also said it plans to hire about 55,000 seasonal workers for the holiday.
At that time last year, FedEx announced that the company wants to hire 50,000 seasonal workers for the holiday period.
The stock fell above 2.5 percent in premarket trading 45 minutes before Wednesday's opening.
FedEx Corp. said making a profit between $10.40 and $10.90 in the fiscal year, which ends next May, 20 cents less than on the previous forecast.
Analysts were expecting $10.84, according to a FactSet survey.
The lower outlook comes despite price increases, which will average 4.9 per cent and take effect on January 4 and despite higher surcharges on surcharges of heavy and large parcels levied from November.
The company said it nonetheless expects earnings to increase year-on-year due to cost reductions, higher revenues, and growth in online commerce.
Chairman and CEO Fred Smith said that the company "produces solid returns considering the weaker-than-expected economic conditions, especially in manufacturing and global trading."
Plans for holiday employment were announced a day later than that of rival UPS, which plans to hire 90,000 to 95,000 additional workers, about the same number as last year.
In the quarter, which ended on August 31, FedEx earned $692 million, 6 percent more than in the previous year.
Earnings per share were $2.42 per share, just behind the $2.44 per share that twelve analysts surveyed by Zacks Investment Research predicted.
Revenue rose 5 percent to $12.3 billion.
Six analysts surveyed by Zacks expected €12.23 billion.
FedEx has seen a decline in international priority shipping in recent years, which brought sales in its express shipping unit to stagnate.
But the boom in online shopping has boosted results at FedEx Ground.
In the last quarter, sales on ground transportation increased by 29 percent, but the operating result was basically flat because of higher costs for larger packages and self-insurancy.
The Express unit recorded higher revenues due to higher prices, while earnings declined due to higher labour costs in the freight business.
In premarket trading, about an hour prior to opening, FedEx shares dropped $3.85, or 2.5 percent, to $150.15.
On Tuesday, they closed with an 11 percent loss for the year and 17 percent below a record high on June 11.
At their most anticipated meeting in the year, Federal Reserve strategists spend two days this week sitting around a 27-foot Honduras-Mahogany table to decide if it's time to raise the key interest rate for the first time in nearly a decade.
Threatening above them at one end of the decorated, two-storey boardroom, there is a mural of the United States.
The card is a reminder that Fed Chairman Janet L. Yellen and her colleagues are supposed to put the nation's economic interests before all other things.
The central bank has a two-fold order from Congress: maximizing U.S. employment and keeping prices stable here.
But since the great recession, the Fed leaders have acknowledged that there is an unwrited third mandate: financial stability.
And that third mandate may be the X-factor in a decision that's too tight to predict. It'll be announced on Thursday whether the U.S. economy is ready to start to be weaned from the unprecedented incentivent by the close-to-zero interest rate since the end of 2008.
Although most data from the economy shows it's growing solidly, recent turbulences in global financial markets could frighten the already cautious Fed representatives to step up that volatility by raising the rate on federal bonds - even by just a quarter of a percentage.
"Obviously, the labour market and inflation will first and foremost be the most important thing they observe," said Lindsey M. Piegza, chief economist at brokerage firm Stifel Nicolaus & Co.
"But I think if they were looking for a pretext not to raise the interest rate, the heightened uncertainty surrounding the global market is enough justification to continue to wait," she said.
There is no consensus on what the Fed is going to do, what is unsettling the financial market in itself.
About half of economists recently surveyed by Bloomberg have predicted a rate hike this week.
Financial markets, however, are expectinging to await the Fed, with a key indicator displaying only a 30% chance of an increase.
This indicates a drop in the stock market in case the Fed raises the rate, unless the strategists mitigate the blow by promising that another increase is far away.
On Tuesday, the Dow Jones Industrial average jumped 228.89 points, or 1.4% to 16,599.85.
The action was sparked by expectations that Fed policy will maintain the phrase this week, said John Lonski, chief economist at Moody's Capital Markets Research Group.
It'd be a surprise if the Fed were to raise the sentences at that point," he said.
If she does, "she would have to go very far in her statement to calm financial markets so they don't panic."
Other experts argued that a rate hike would reassure financial markets because it would eliminate uncertainty about when the Fed will trade.
Just a month ago, the Fed seemed on a good way to raising the rate by 0.25 percentage points this month, the first small step in a slump in monetary conditions that would test the strength of recovery in the U.S.
Yellen had said in the spring and early summer that she was expecting an increase in the rate this year, despite complaints from liberals that more economic progress would be needed.
Although job creation slowed in August, improvement in the labour market this year was solid.
The unemployment rate fell to 5.1% last month, the lowest in more than seven years and close to the level the Fed seems full employment.
Wage increases have shown signs of recovery.
And although inflation was constantly below the central bank's annual target of 2%, Fed representatives said the main reason was the temporary effect of significantly lower oil prices.
The economy "can handle higher interest rates and needs them because of tension in the labour market," said Ian Shepherdson, chief economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics.
But financial markets around the world shruggered at the end of last month after China depreciated its currency.
The Dow Jones Industrial average fell more than 16% below its peak in May, when investors feared that a slowdown in China's economy would throttle global growth.
The market has recovered somewhat this month, with the Dow now being nearly 10% below May's high.
For Fed representatives, market turbulences affect the complex calculation of when to raise rates.
"If the events in the financial markets and the news about the weakness of China's economy hadn't occurred over the past few weeks, they'd put the reins in this session," said Stephen D. Oliner, a senior research fellow at the UCLA Ziman Center for Real Estate and a former employee of the U.S. Federal Reserve.
Now he predicts she's going to wait for at least a month to get a better grip on the impact.
"There's a lot of uncertainty about what the impact will be on the U.S. economy, and there's almost no reliable data describing developments in China," Oliner said.
The health of the global economy is none of the Fed's main concerns, so it should not be technically involved in its decision-making.
But in the end, the Fed's monetary authority has become the world's monetary authority.
The central bank's influence has been enormous in recent years.
The mere hint from then-head Ben S. Bernanke in 2013 that the Fed could "narrogate" its monetary expansion sparked confusion around the globe.
In a world where markets and economies are increasingly intertwined, Fed representatives may be expected not only to monitor developments elsewhere, but also consult with other central banks and experts to gain a better understanding of the global economy.
Their decision-making is "more art than science," said Zachary Karabell, head of the global strategy for wealth management at Envestnet Inc.
Jeffrey Lacker, president of the Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, VA. noted this month that "the Fed has a history of overreactions to financial market movements that are apparently not linked to economic fundamentals."
Lacker, a voting member of the Federal Open Market Committee that advocates for an interest rate hike, cited decisions in 1998/1999 as an example.
A financial crisis in Asia led the Fed to cut its key interest rate by 0.75 percentage points in three sessions "despite limited recognizable impact on growth in the U.S." Lacker said.
A year later, Fed representatives reversed those cuts.
The Fed should put aspects of financial stability first only during a serious crisis such as the 2008 market collapse, said Adam S. Posen, former member of the Bank of England's Committee on Interestinary Determination.
Recent turbulences do not come close to this level, he said.
"She certainly doesn't want to get into the situation where she's afraid of a rate hike because markets are upset," said Posen, president of the Peterson Institute for International Economics.
The Federal Reserve Act still contains the double mandate and doesn't mention financial stability, and until that's changed, I don't think it should rush ahead of time.
Do you need a great Thai market?
Try LAX-C in Chinatown.
Yes, Chinatown
The market/warehouse opened in 1997 and is owned by LAX-C Inc.
LAX-C is often referred to as Thai Costco and is a Thai wholesale market that also sells to the general public.
Yes, he is located in Chinatown and not in Thai Town.
The market/warehouse is a single point of contact and offers a confusing number of fresh, frozen and dried goods, popular Thai and Asian snacks, and dishes, furniture, religious jewelry and Christmas decorations.
There's even an auto section if you need engine oil.
The store is huge, and it takes over an hour to stroll through the aisles.
If the whole shopping makes you hungry, there's a meal for you in the LAX-C BBQ Express.
The cooking kitchen (good and inexpensive) in this store is located near the cash registers and has various takeaway options.
What's here: Do you need something - anything - Thaish?
LAX-C probably has it.
Products are inexpensive and a number of them are sold in large quantities.
Fresh produce includes Asian specialties and regional Thai herbs, fruit and more.
The meat counter is extensive, as is the fish counter - the staff behind the counter will purify and fillet the fish and even fry for you on request.
A good part of the store belongs to the massive freezing department. Here you'll find frozen curry leaves, bitter melon and galgant, whole ducks, fish, beef blood and bile, pork's natural intestine, fish balls, regional sausage, commercially prepared food and more.
Who buys here: restaurant owners and chefs, also individual families and individuals.
The wide aisles are suitable for shopping carts and forklifts.
You'll find here: fresh Monthong Durian next to the box office.
Where it is located: The store is located in an industrial area of Los Angeles on the edge of Chinatown and downtown - a few blocks away from Philippe The Original.
Parking: LAX-C has its own park area, along with other shops and restaurants.
Even if the store is well attended, it's not hard to find a parking space.
Hoyerswerda: Small life support for refugees
