Brazil's paediatric society Rio Grande promotes diet for healthy breastfeeding
The Brazilian paediatric organization SPRS seeks to educate women in a shocking way about the importance of good diet during breastfeeding. It does this with the presentation of images of babies sucking on breasts painted with depictions of unhealthy foods.
Breast milk consists of a variety of proteins, fats, vitamins and carbohydrates that give babies all the nutrients they need to build a strong immune system.
Due to the additional energy requirements, pregnant women and young mothers are advised to increase their daily calorie intake by about 500 calories per day.
However, new research suggests that higher calorie intake isn’t the only dietary adaptation that mothers should make.
A recent study led by Robert Waterland, a professor at Baylor College of Medicine in Texas, showed that a healthy diet of pregnant women actually affects their babies' genes and protects them from the development of tumors.
Unfortunately, an unhealthy diet of the child can negatively affect the immune system.
Exceptions for the new Bernried sports hall
The municipal council Bernried approved the construction application for the €2.3-million sports hall.
And he made an exception.
The roof may become flatter for solar system.
The plans for the long-awaited gymnasium construction at Bernried's sports grounds are progressing step by step: In the most recent municipal council meeting, the elaborated building application was unanimously approved without major discussions.
Previously, the panel had decided, among other things, an application for a building law "exception" or "liberation" from the valid development plan.
The "exception" was about the permission for a special form with regard to the roof construction.
Accordingly, a "saddle roof with non-centrime first and roof overhang" is to be erected.
The background to the planning is the planned use of the roof area for solar systems: "This gives us a wonderfully large southern roof together," explained Mayor Josef Steigenberger in the meeting.
The "liberation" was again about the roof inclination, which is supposed to be below ten degrees for energetic reasons - unlike prescribed in the development plan.
The volume of the hall is thus significantly reduced.
A "liberation" became necessary because the development plan does not allow "exceptions" with regard to the roof inclination - whereby the Bernried municipal council usually proceeds rather restrictively in the case of exemption applications.
However, gymnasium construction is not a recurring project: "We do not create a precedent with it," says Steigenberger.
The gymnasium construction at the sports center is a joint project of the municipality and the SV Bernried.
Around €2.3 million is included in costs.
Grants are expected from school and grassroots sports funding.
In the next procedural step, the building application for the 18 x 36-meter hall will now be submitted to the district office for examination.
Clinic Peißenberg moves to Schongau
Peißenberg Hospital is to be closed earlier than planned.
At the same time, Dr. Wilhelm Fischer (66) will become the new Medical Director at Schongau Hospital.
The station and the majority of the employees move from Peißenberg to Schongau.
This is provided for by the surprising plans of the hospital GmbH of the district, the district councillor Andrea Jochner-Weiß confirmed.
However, a decision on this will be made by the Supervisory Board and will meet next Wednesday, 23 September.
Should the plans be implemented, all of the nearly 50 employees at the hospital in Peißenberg would continue to be employed.
The vast majority should move to Schongau with fishermen and the still-existing internal station.
The rooms are available.
According to Jochner-Weiß, employees who cannot go to Schongau should be offered a job in Weilheim.
The employees are informed about the plans.
The changer was triggered by the termination of Dr. Andreas Eder, who has only been medical director of the Schongau clinic since the beginning of the year and is now switching to a private practice in the immediate vicinity of the hospital.
According to Jochner-Weiss, the name "Fischer" came into play, who agreed to take over the management from 1 October and move to Schongau with his team.
For the house in Schongau, this would in any case mean an "evaluation" and a broadening of the offer in the field of "internal medicine", according to Jochner-Weiß.
Fischer is a specialist in pacemakers and defibrillators.
Schongau (180 beds) specializes in gastroenterological diseases on the Inner Station.
Temporarily for the next two years Fischer and his team, if the supervisory board agrees, will run both houses until next year, the former Knappschaft hospital, which still has 35 beds, is to be finally closed in Peißenberg after almost 140 years.
Until now, the district had planned this for 2018, when the currently ongoing redevelopment at the Weilheim hospital is completed.
Landrätin Jochner-Weiß, who is also chairman of the supervisory board, countered rumours that the house, which is owned by the Bundesknappschaft, is to be used in the future as accommodation for asylum seekers.
"I rule that out." As it goes on with the property, Jochner-Weiß could not say that there were several considerations.
Carita's short-term care and day psychiatry are currently housed there.
"What better could not happen to us, such an opportunity does not come back," said Fischer about the quasi-movement to the Lech, which had been received by the employees "positively and very understandingly".
There was no real perspective for the house, where Fischer has been working as chief physician for over 30 years.
He had been asked by Schongau colleagues to take over the post of medical director.
Peißenberg Hospital (once 100 beds) has repeatedly been threatened with closure over the past decades, departments have been closed, for example surgery and obstetrics.
