The Criminal Investigation is Investigating.
Missing 16-year-old from Rhineland-Palatinate Victim of violent crimes
A suspect has led the police to the corpse of 16-year-old from Rhineland-Palatinate, who has been missing since Saturday.
In interrogation, the 20-year-old admitted killing the youngsters and throwing her body into a canal in Rockenhausen.
A 16-year-old from Rhineland-Palatinate, who has been missing since Saturday, has fallen victim to a violent crime.
The body of the young woman was found on Tuesday evening in Rockenhausen near Kaiserslautern, as prosecutors and police announced on Wednesday.
Suspicious is a 20-year-old also arrested on Tuesday evening, who confessed to the crime and led the investigators to the location of the body.
The 16-year-old had been reported missing on Sunday morning after failing to come home the night before following a visit to an autumn festival in Rockenhausen.
When searching for the young woman, investigators initially encountered personal belongings of the victim.
During interrogations in the circle of friends and acquaintances of the missing, a suspicion against the 20-year-old finally arose.
The alleged perpetrator then led police to a canal in Rockenhausen, where the body was found.
According to the authorities, the detainee admitted in the police interrogation that the 16-year-old had been killed and placed the body in the canal.
The 20-year-old was to be brought before the magistrate on Wednesday. Investigators charge him manslaughter.
Information on the specific circumstances of the offence is intended to bring the further investigation.
Refugees need accommodation: Study warns of housing gap
The situation in the German housing market is tense.
More and more refugees are also looking for an affordable place to stay.
The pressure on the housing market is growing.
Social housing is facing a challenge.
The distribution struggles in the German housing market have long since begun.
With incoming refugees and job seekers from the EU, more and more people are pushing into a market that is already overloaded in many major cities.
They compete with low-income citizens for the last affordable housing.
To meet the demand for affordable housing, 400,000 new units would have to be built each year by 2020.
This is the result of a study on behalf of the "Social Housing Association".
By way of comparison, 260,000 units will be completed in Germany this year.
This is missing 140,000 apartments.
Refugees are no longer obligated after the first three months of their staying than asylum seekers to live in an initial reception accommodation.
Many then bump into the housing market and line up in the long lines in front of the rental apartments.
According to the Federal Statistical Office, however, less than half of all asylum seekers lived for rent last year.
However, the negative development of the housing market is not only due to the increasing number of refugees.
The study also accounts for significant omissions in housing policy in recent years.
Germany is pushing ahead with a housing deficiency that is getting bigger every year.
Currently, around 770,000 residential units have already been missing in the metropolitan areas, complains Matthias Günther, author of the study.
Many professionals therefore necessarily lived in large cities in shared rooms.
Young adults stayed involuntarily longer in the "Hotel Mama" because of the doldrums on the housing market.
In the major cities, the number of square meters per capita has fallen again for the first time in recent years.
To solve the problem, the "Social Housing" alliance proposes a fresh start.
In doing so, new incentives are intended to boost social housing again.
Thus, the real estate transfer tax could be waived and the property tax for subsidised on a temporary basis.
In addition, according to the alliance, attractive tax depreciation options are necessary.
The support program for refugee accommodation of Staatsbank KfW is already well received by cities and municipalities.
A good week after the start on Monday evening with 184 million euros had already been retrieved more than half of the available around 300 million euros.
Now the project could even be increased.
Refugees have free guidance: Croatia and Slovenia set up corridor
There is practically no getting through to Hungary anymore, as more and more refugees choose the way via Croatia.
Zagreb announces it will grant transit via Slovenia to the north without hurdles.
However, the route is still dangerous.
Croatia intends to reach an agreement with neighbouring Slovenia on the establishment of a corridor for refugees.
"I've spoken to the Slovenian interior minister, and if it's necessary, we're going to organise a corridor," Croatia's Interior Minister Ranko Ostojic said.
Slovenia is located between Croatia and Austria and already belongs to the Schengen zone.
The EU country Croatia is not yet a Schengen country.
After the closure of the Serbian-Hungarian border, hundreds of refugees left for Croatia to get from there to Western Europe.
500 people had crossed the border at Tovarnik, reports the Croatian television channel RTL.
Hungary had sealed off its border for refugees the day before.
New laws that came into force on the same day also make ordinary asylum procedures in Hungary virtually superfluous.
Croatia wants the refugees to pass through.
Croatian Prime Minister Zoran Milanovic declared: "You can travel through and we are preparing for this possibility."
These people are there, it's women, children and men who want to live and achieve something.
However, it is people who did not want to live in Croatia.
The Croatian police had in the morning picked up the first refugees on the border of Serbia to the EU-neighbouring country Croatia.
According to media reports, most were from Syria, Iran and Afghanistan.
They came to the Serbian border town of Sid both by bus and taxis.
Immediately after their arrival, they would have tried to get on foot across the green border into Croatia.
At the Croatian-Serbian border, the refugees do not come across border fences.
For this purpose, the border strip poses other dangers: There are still sharp mines from the Yugoslav civil war in the 1990s.
Mine-roomers are now to quickly create a safe passage.
After the closure of the border, however, there are hardly any refugees coming to Hungary.
On Tuesday, the police counted 366 apprehended people.
Shortly before the goal on Monday it had been 9380.
The new Hungarian laws classify the overcoming and damage of the border fence as a criminal offence.
Therefore, 35 criminal cases were initiated in the southern Hungarian border town of Szeged.
Convicts face prison sentences of up to five years and deportation from the country.
According to the Immigration Department, a total of 94 asylum applications were received on Tuesday in the "transit zones" newly set up at the border, almost all of them - 93 - at the most important transition Röszke.
Among the applicants are 13 families with young children as well as two elderly people.
As of Wednesday morning, there were still 40 refugees.
Practically, however, none has a prospect of being granted asylum in Hungary anymore.
According to the Hungarian pointion, Serbia is a "safe third country".
The rejected asylum seekers are to be deported there.
Only a few hundred refugees gathered in front of the "transit zone" in Röszke.
In Röszke, workers began to clean up.
They collected the rubbish that tens of thousands of people had left on the side of the road in recent weeks.
Barriers were dismantled and one of the reception camps in the border town was rebuilt.
Nobody wanted to comment on the goal of this conversion.
Only a few refugees can reach Austria after the closure of the border between Serbia and Hungary.
The country has also stepped up its border controls.
According to government figures, however, around 20,000 migrants were still in the country.
In Salzburg, about 1400 people are waiting for the journey to Germany.
The train traffic from there to Germany was stopped in the morning on the instructions of German authorities.
In Freilassing, smaller groups of refugees regularly arrived on foot.
Mayor drove 'stranded' across border
"We were busy cleaning up after Sunday's bridge festival, when suddenly ten people with travel suitcases stood at the entrance of the tent," said Wernstein's mayor Alois Stadler.
Tourists, they wanted to take the train to Passau.
On Sunday, however, Germany had surprisingly suspended rail traffic with Austria because of the flow of refugees, it was an end for them in Wernstein," said Stadler, who immediately offered his help and chauffeured the stranded together with two other helpers and their cars across the border into the nearby Passau.
Previously, we have tasted the unexpected guests, with snacks and drinks.
We've just said we'll take them to Passau by car.
"This was self-evident for us," said Stadler in the OÖN conversation.
They were very grateful.
