The Brulljesmacher from Kallstadt
Donald Trump's Palatinate ancestors came from a place whose inhabitants are called show-offs.
This trait also connects some with the billionaire, who wants to become U.S. president.
Donald Trump (69) is always good for fuss.
Whether he expresses xenophobicism, approaches journalists rudely or reveals gaps in knowledge in international crisis policy - the candidate for the U.S. presidential office vigorously stirs up the election campaign.
And perform well with that.
This is also registered in the Palatinate Kallstadt, where the grandparents of the real estate billionaire come from.
The New Yorker with the hair dryer is known to many in the wine-growing village on the Weinstraße.
Some in the 1200-inhabitant place is related over -zig corners to him.
His grandfather came from Kallstadt in the Palatinate: Donald Trump, here in campaign mode.
Kallstadt-based Simone Wendel made a film about Trump.
And what do villagers think about his goal to become the world's most powerful man?
"I think they find it kind of exciting, but they aren't so deeply impressed in their Kallstadt style," says Simone Wendel.
The Kallstadt native has made an amusing documentary about the place and his famous offspring, which, in addition to Trump, also includes the ketchup Heinz family.
Trump, who is not known for modesty, also comes to speak in "Kings of Kallstadt" (2014) - so with the statement that he would certainly also be successful if he lived in Kallstadt.
To the king of hearts he did not bring it there.
Although there is respect for the performance of the famility, but also tends to think that Trump is "already a bit a big mouth," says Wendel.
Coincidentally, the nickname that Kallstadt has in the perimeter is "Die Brulljesmacher" - to high German: the show-offs, she says.
So Trump comes out of the village of show-offs.
Trump's success story began in 1885, when the later grandfather emigrated and opened a restaurant for gold diggers in the U.S.
The real estate giant's cornerstone was initial land purchases in New York.
But the life of the grandparents was not free of tragedy: their desire to return to their homeland in the long term failed on the Bavarian state, to which the Palatinate belonged at the time.
He did not accept Trump because he had once left without authorization, had declared the director of the Institute of Palatinate History, Roland Paul, at the start of the film.
Trump is the grandson of an “illegal emigrant.”
This is in contrast to the sometimes marked statements of the descendant.
He had repeatedly spoken derogatingly about Mexican immigrants in the U.S. and demanded the construction of a border wall to deter illegal immigrants.
"The way he's bulging, it's not that sympathetic," says Hans-Joachim Bender, a distant relative of Trump ("The grandma was a born Trump, the grandpa was born Heinz").
The retired winemaker sees the American rather distant, "he's always so efferventive."
Does he think Trump will come?
"I can't imagine that," says the 72-year-old.
"He's never cared about Kallstadt."
The pension landlord Veronika Schramm mentions, like some Kallstadters, that a descendant of the Heinz family donated something for the church organ during a visit, which was generally well received.
"I can't imagine that from Donald," the 68-year-old says.
“I’d think it’s better if he didn’t win the election, because “he has such radical views, I don’t know if that’s good.”
If those who were in charge at the time of his grandfather in the United States had been like him, "he would never have arrived there at all."
"There are more interesting topics than him," says winemaker's daughter Sarah Bühler.
And as president?
"It's not my president," she says.
“The grapes will ripe even without President Trump.”
Wendel's film, for which Trump has long been interviewed, have seen 12 000 moviegoers, and interest is also awakening in the United States.
