Four people, including a 4-year-old boy, were killed in southern Nepal after police fired rubber bullets into a crowd demonstrating against the proposed new constitution, an official said Wednesday.
The violence erupted on Tuesday night in Rupandehi district, about 175 miles west of the capital Kathmandu.
Bishnu Prasad Dhakal, senior district official, said more than 1,000 protesters had gathered outside a police station, with some of them throwing rocks and incendiary bombs.
Mr Dhakal said the police used tear gas and then shooted the air before resorting to rubber bullets.
20 police officers and five civilians were injured, he said.
It was not immediately clear why the rubber bullets used, which are known that can kill but which are traditionally used as a less less less death, apparently produced such a high death rate.
The clash was the latest in weeks of violent protests in Nepal's southern plains, where members of Madhesi's ethnic group say new provinces formed under the proposed new constitution would diminish their political influence.
There have also been protests in western Nepal from the Tharus ethnic group, who want their own state.
At least 40 people, including 11 police officers, have been involved in violent clashes since the 10.
August killed when the four major political parties signed an agreement to divide the country into provinces - an important step towards a new constitution, but a highly contentious issue in Nepal that has been centrally governed for centuries.
Working on a new constitution has been ongoing since 2008, but lawmakers accelerated the process after the devastating earthquake that killed thousands of people in April.
Legislators this week voted on certain clauses in the draft constitution in Kathmandu, and officials have said the new constitution could go into effect on Sunday.
