"It's impossible that everyone can come to Europe" - Dalai Lama - RT-News
Europe cannot take in all refugees, the Dalai Lama said, adding that the real solution to the refugee crisis lies in the Middle East.
The Tibetan-Buddhist leader called for ending the violence in refugees' home countries.
The Dalai Lama addressed the problem of the ongoing refugee crisis at the beginning of his ten-day visit to the UK in his speech on the occasion of the opening of the Dalai Lama Centre for Compassion in Oxford, dedicated to ethics studies.
He demanded that under the circumstances the interests of humanity should be placed before the interests of individual nations or even continents.
He also stressed that the Western European countries' response to the refugee crisis was admirable, but not appropriate to the situation.
"It's impossible that anyone can come to Europe," he said in his speech.
Although the Dalai Lama praised Germany's and Austria's efforts to deal with the crisis, he emphasized that it was only a temporary solution.
“Taking care of several thousand refugees is wonderful, but in the meantime you need to think about long-term solutions on how to create real peace and real development, mainly through education, for these Muslim countries,” he stressed.
Ultimately, we need to think about homicide in their countries being diminished.
We need to reduce the use of violence.
"The use of force has never solved these problems," he added.
The Dalai Lama addressed the issue of violence and also commented on George Bush's actions after the 9/11 terrorist attack, pointing out that the U.S.'s violent reaction entailed a chain of uncontrollable events.
After 9/11, I expressed my condolences in a letter to President Bush, and I told him that the way to solve this problem lies in non-violence.
"I know his motives were good, but he used violence, and this has provoked unexpected consequences," the Dalai Lama said.
Four-fold increase in intake of hand sanitizer by children over the past 4 years
Poison control centers in the U.S. report a nearly 400 percent increase in the number of children under the age of twelving heavily alcoholic hand sanitizers, according to the Georgia Poison Center.
According to Dr Gaylord Lopez, director of the Georgia Poison Center, cases of revenue from hand sanitizers in children under the age of 12 who report poison control centers rose from 3,266 in 2010 to 16,117 in 2014.
"Children are getting these products more frequently, and unfortunately, a percentage of them end up in the emergency room," Lopez told CNN.
He said that some children intentionally drink disinfectants, while some do it to impressive their friends or because of a test of courage on social media.
Videos on YouTube show how adolescents drink disinfectants only because of a cheap experience.
Adolescents allegedly mixed disinfectants with alcohol-containing mouthwater to get a harder drink.
Younger children can be attracted by attractive scents of disinfectants.
"A child doesn't think this is bad for them," Lopez said.
Many of the more attractive (hand disinfectants) are those that are perfumed.
There are disinfectants with strawberry, grape and orange scent, which are very appealing to children.
Hand sanitizers contain somewhere between 45 and 95 percent alcohol.
Especially in young children, even a few splashes can lead to alcohol poisoning.
"It's highly concentrated alcohol," Dr. Stephen Thornton, medical director of the Poison Emergency Call Center at the University of Kansas Hospital, told Fox 4 in Kansa City.
So, you wouldn't leave a sip of whisky standing around, but people leave these hand disinfectants within reach of children, and if kids get to it, then it's a quick way to consume a lot of alcohol.
Nhaijah Russell, a six-year-old girler who had recently taken four splashes of strawberry-scented hand sanitizer at school, was taken to an emergency room for treatment.
Her blood alcohol concentration was 179, twice higher than the threshold, which is legally considered drunk in an adult, said Dr. Chris Ritchey, an emergency room doctor who treated her at Gwinnett Medical Center outside Atlanta.
Nhaijah stalled her words and wasn't able to leave when she arrived at the emergency room.
Doctors monitored her overnight at a separate children's hospital for signs of brain trauma as the girl had fallen through the alcohol and bumped her head, Ritchey said.
"That was very scary," Ortoria Scott, Nhaijah's mother, told CNN.
It could have been deadly for my child.
Lopez has advised parents and teachers to use non-alcoholic products or disinfection wipes and store hand sanitizers out of the child's reach.
Beyond alcohol poisoning, some disinfectants were linked to deaths.
In 2013, two women in Ontario women died after swallowing hand sanitizers containing a toxic undeclared ingredient.
Employees of health authorities suspect that the product contained methanol, a deadly solution, rather than ethyl alcohol, which has been listed as an active ingredient.
In January, three fourth-grade students in New York state planned to poison their "common" teacher by laying out antibacterial products around the classroom," according to a police report.
The teacher is highly allergic to hand sanitizers and banned her from her room.
Police viewed the thwarted plan as "chattering" and referred disciplinary action to the school's authority.
