Why I'm witnessing an execution in Oklahoma
Slightly later today, except in the case of a late legal intervention, I will watch as a man is put to death.
If it happens, and I hope it doesn't happen, I'm going to sit in a spectator gallery in the death chamber at the Oklahoma State Penitentiary when Richard Glossip is executed through the poison injection.
He asked me to be one of the four official witnesses at his death.
The invitation took place for the first time in January via his best friend, and although I was shocked, I agreed.
As the day approached, I began to be afraid.
So, why should I do it?
I agreed because I thought it would be a great way to tell the whole story about the death penalty.
It would offer a unique perspective.
It's true that journalists are allowed to see executions in the U.S., and since there are often more applications than places available, a macabre lottery takes place to decide who'll be elected.
Oklahoma has reduced the number of media places from 12 to five.
Whoever is selected will observe it emotionlessly.
I can't do it.
I'm going to sit next to Richard Glossip's closest friend, with whom I've developed my own relationship over the course of the months with phone calls and a two-and-a-half-hour visit.
I like this guy.
We often laugh a lot when we talk.
The prison authorities say I can't be a witness and a journalist, so I won't be allowed to take a notebook and a pen to record what I see.
I'll just have to try to remember all the details.
An Oklahoma journalist accused me on Twitter of abandoning my impartiality.
Here's the lesson.
Tell the prisoner's story as he wants, and you'll get access.
I complained to her.
She conceded that she "could have formulated it better," but she still thinks it's wrong what I'm doing.
Richard Glosssip had problem with some of the things I said and wrote.
He doesn't tell me what I have to write, and I wouldn't, even if he did.
I've told him that he can remove me from his list of witnesses at any time.
But he wants an international reporter to be present to write there.
When he dies, he thinks the movement's public relations will help against the death penalty.
Kim Bellware, a Huffington Post reporter, will sit next to me.
If you want to know more about the case, please listen to the series of podcasts I've made.
I don't want to go into all the details here again.
I can't say with certainty that Richard Glossip is innocent.
His actions following the assassination of Barry Van Treese would make him guilty of being a beneficiary of the offence.
But I think there's a strong likelihood that he isn't guilty of murder.
I certainly believe there isn't enough evidence to justify a death sentence.
I think his execution is wrong.
And from that perspective I'm going to see him dying.
I can't claim to be truly objective.
There will be other reporters you can turn to for it.
What you're going to get from me is what it's meant to see a man die an unnecessary death.
Man persuaded to get out of the Canning River after wet police chase
The Canning Vale policeman remained dry as he persuaded the man from the Canning River.
An attempt by a man from Perth to hang up police was unsuccessful when he found himself in shallow and swampy water, just metres away from a police officer trying to "gently persuade him to return to the shore."
Canning Vale police were summoneded to a house peace breach incident in Beckenham on Tuesday.
