Peter Moody claims stewards tried to spy on him and threates to end racing
Peter Moody alleges Racing Victoria stewards attempted to install a spy in his stables last year and threatened to end the race immediately.
He aimed the stewards and the integrity department of Racing Victoria, claiming that they were carrying out a personal campaign against him with Lady Tatai's late scratching for a race day treatment, the final straw on Wednesday.
Moody told Fairfax Media that he had always tried to do what is best for racing and he found himself in difficult conditions when it came to the positive cobalt swab sample at Lidaris that he can't explain.
He had held back 18 months because of the espionage allegations and didn't want it "looked like too-hung grapes."
"I didn't intend to say anything, and I've sat on it for a long time, but sometimes you're so frustrated that it all comes out at once," Moody said.
You know it went on.
Sometimes you react, and you have to live with the consequences, and I have to live with what I've said.
I'll go home tonight and sleep well.
I'd like to walk around raised heads, and I want my owners to think I'm trying my best for them.
If they start thinking I don't, I shouldn't be a coach anymore.
I'll have to think about what I'm doing with my license.
Earlier, Moody had let the bomb go off in an interview with racing.com that the Integrity Department had attempted to spy on his stable.
"Eighteen months ago, Terry Bailey, Dayle Brown and Dr. Brian Stewart were sitting in a room with a man - whom I'm going to name when I'm being asked - and offered him employment in my stables to provide information about what I'm doing inside my stables," Moody said on Racing.com.
You obviously think I'm a scammer.
If that's the case, they're certainly supposed to grasp their noses because they've failed in trying to catch me.
Robert Roulston, former Chairman of RVL, David Moodie, acting President of the RVL and Bernard Saundry, current CEO of the RVL, were aware of this at the time and are still today.
Should this lead me to think it's going to be personal?
Has the RVL behaved cowardly and perhaps blames people outside coaches for what's going on in the industry?
Am I bad for the industry?
Am I that bad for the industry?
Maybe people out there think it's me.
Maybe they have to take my license from me and cast me out, and I have no doubt that what I'm saying will make them think about it now.
But I've now got to the point where I don't care, and that makes me sad.
This makes me really sad.
I'm going to put the pressure on them and urge them to make a decision.
I've got the support of my family and I can go.
The trainer's frustration levels have built up with the long-lasting cobalt investigation at Lidari, and late scratching caused him to say he could "throw his licence over the table."
Moody, best known for his preparation of undefeated champion Black Caviar, described the race day treatment rule "ridiculous" after a pack of mud or clay had been applied to the horse's legs, which violated the rules.
“It’s against the rules of racing.
I have to take full responsibility for that," he said.
“One of my employees mistakenly applied mud on the leg.
It could've been like that last night.
It could've been like that all day yesterday.
But it also had it that way on race day.
Moody could face a three-month disqualification for treatment with the pack on race day. An investigation into that matter has been opened.
It's a joke.
People who make these rules don't know anything about the horse.
"We all see that we need rules, but here you've gone too far," Moody said.
