Melbourne Storm coach Craig Bellamy strikes back against rivals over claims to wrestling
Melbourne Storm coach Craig Bellamy has described criticism of his players' tackling as an "agenda-setting" of two Premier League favourites of the NRL.
Sydney Rooster's coach Trent Robinson said the referees have allowed the Storm players "wrestling battles" with his team during the high Storm win in the qualifying finals in Sydney on Friday night.
Brisbane Bronco's coach Wayne Bennett made a barely-veiled note to the Storm team after his squad scored the final victory against North Queensland Cowboys on Saturday night. He called the game a "model example" of the rugby league and said the two from Queensland weren't "too good" in wrestling.
Bellamy defended his squad's tacklimg and said they were focused only on their "contact" at the tackling.
Bellamy added that criticism from two leading coaches on his squad makes him confident as it means they were worried about being beaten by the Storm team.
"That keeps popping up - I didn't heard Trent Robinson talking about wrestling when they beat us 24:2," Bellamy said Wednesday.
I can guarantee that we haven't changed anything about our defensive system or techniques in the whole year. But over the past six weeks we've made our contact at Tackles a real focus, and that's what the turnaround has yielded for us.
Trent has been there for three years now, and he obviously has a great squad to training. He's therefore probably not used to losing, so it was a bit of a shock for him.
It’s funny how this always appears at this time of year.
Bellamy has attacked Bennett's idea of "exciting football," reminding his mentor that Bennett's St George Illawarra Dragons won the Premier League with "boring" football in 2010.
Wayne speaks of exciting football.
He compared his game on Saturday night with ours on Friday night and how exciting their game was," Bellamy said.
I remember St George being criticized as a boring team in 2010, and Wayne said he's going to take care of shit about it.
But now he has a team that can play and that we see as thrilling, especially its young players with their speed.
This is the ball game you want to play if you have these kinds of players.
He made that statement in 2010, now he wants everyone to play his kind of football - not every team has two players like Anthony Milford and Ben Hunt, so they can play like this.
I'm building stronger confidence because they're saying these things because they're a bit worried about having to play against us.
Six weeks agove, they probably didn't think about us when it came to the Big Games.
Bellamy also pointed out that the game Storm against Roosters scored a higher result than Bronco's game against Cowboys.
The Bronco's game was arguably a good game, I haven't lookeed at it yet, but what was the result? 16 : 12 ?
Our game was 20:18, there's been two more attempts in our game, but is that an exciting game?
Or are taking breaks and no points anything exciting?
For me, it's the game plans.
If they mention us, it means that we will still meet them at one stage.
The Storm team has been free this weekend as they have gained the right to receive either North Queensland Cowboys or the Cronulla Sharks next Saturday night in the pre-finals at AAMI Park. Ticket sales will begin starting next Tuesday morning.
Serbian with £200,000 bail after drug charges
A man who may face a life sentence over his attempt to take possession of 40 kilograms of cocaine smuggled into Queensland has been released on bail of $200,000.
Marko Maksimovic, 29, was one of the five men arrested last month after federal police found 100 kg of the drug on the Solay yacht, which came from South America via Vanuatu and docked in Coomera in August.
Maksimovic was seen him meeting his co-defendants at the marina over the course of the morning and later at Café Coomera Roadhouse, where police found 40 kilograms of the drug in a Toyota echo.
Despite a potentially life sentence for attempting to own a commercial amount of cocaine, Maksimovic's lawyer argued that there was no evidence that he attempted to own the entire amount and that in the cafe might have been for an innocuous reason.
The Supreme Court judge, Peter Flanagan, was concerned about the danger of escape at Maksimovic.
He has clear relations with Serbia.
He often travels to Serbia," he explained to the court on Wednesday.
In the criminal case, he lied to the police about his whereabouts.
Maksimovic's mother and stepfather had offered a $200,000 bail as collective to achieve his release.
"Following reading the affidavits of the two individuals, it's clear that the imposition of $200,000 is a real financial burden," Judge Flanagan said.
But that alone won't be enough to stop him from not appearing, he warned.
Maksimovic has been released on bail under the conditions that he surrenders his Australian and Serbian passports, reports to the police daily and adheres to a curfew of 18:00-06:00.
Accident on A5 near Karlsruhe: cab tears apart
In a rear-end collision with three trucks on Autobahn 5 near Karlsruhe, a truck driver was seriously injured on Tuesday evening.
Two of the three trucks involved had braked in front of a permanent construction site in the direction of Basel.
The third driver noticed it too late and tried in vain to dodge to the left at the last moment, the police announced.
The cab of the cause of the accident completely tore apart.
The driver was taken to a hospital seriously injured.
The A5 between Bruchsal and Karlsruhe-Nord was completely closed for six hours until 2 a.m.
This resulted in damage in the amount of 122,000 euros.
Stevan Hogg used phone of girls to lure paedophiles to send pictures
A paedophile used the phone of an 11-year-old girl to request images of child abuse.
Stevan Hogg took the girl's phone as she was asleep, logged into an online messaging service from which he received a series of indecent images of children sent to him by another user.
The girl later woke up and discovered that the phone was missing and found it in the hand of sleeping Hogg.
She looked through it, founded the confusing messages and immediately alerted her mother.
The police took care of it and saw the five images, then analyzed computers found at the address and discovered "concerning" web searches that used terms to search for indecent images of children.
Hogg then claimed to the officers that he did, as it would "lead paedophiles on the internet" and he then "pretended to be a police officer to scare them."
Prosecutor Eilidh Robertson told Dundee Sheriff Court: "he said it was an addiction - that he was addicted to scaring people.
He admitted that the searches on the computer were carried out by him, but said it had happened because he wanted to claim to be a policeman to scare paedophiles.
He was asked about his conversations with the unidentified person who sent him the pictures and whom he asked to send even more photos.
He said that he did it to lure her.
He said he's sick in his head when he's drinking, and said he then wanted to see images of rape and murder.
MS Robertson added: "When the girl found the news on the phone, she called her mother and was crying.
She then also saw the indecent pictures on the phone and confronted the defendant.
The girl was interviewed and said the defendant used computers and Playstation to talk to girls who looked much younger than him.
Hogg, 23, Ward Road, Dundee, pleaded guilty in the indictment of taking indecent images of children on June 14 last year, violating the conditions on January 25 of this year and not appearing in court for a hearing on March 24 this year.
Defensive lawyer Gregor Sim asked that Hogg not appear in the register of sexual offenders, as one could argue that "no significant sexual element" could be found in his offense.
Sheriff Alistair Carmichael postponed the verdict until October to obtain background reports on social work.
Hogg was granted bail in this case, but was held in pre-trial detention for hearings on other outstanding cases.
He was included in the sexual offender's directory before the date of the verdict.
Smugglers dodge small border crossings with Germany
More and more refugees are being apprehended away from major checkpoints at the German-Austrian border.
Many smugglers, for fear of discovery and arrest, increasingly left people at smaller border crossings
out on the Austrian side, said a spokesman for the federal police.
More than 1000 refugees have been apprehended in Passau and Rottal-Inn districts since Tuesday.
They had reached the German side on foot over bridges and dams.
There they are received by federal police officers and forwarded for registration.
According to federal police data, significantly more refugees arrived at Munich's main station compared to the start of the week on Wednesday.
Around 700 people had arrived in the state capital by 8.00 a.m. alone, a spokesman said in the morning.
After the reintroduction of controls at the German-Austrian border, 1759 refugees had reached the city on Tuesday.
Stuttgart: Foothill of tropical storm "Henry" expected in the south-west
Meteorologists warn of storm gusts in the south-west from Wednesday afternoon.
Due to a low-pressure area over Western Europe, warm sea air is currently flowing into the country, said the German Weather Service (DWD) in Stuttgart.
The strong low is the foothills of the tropical storm "Henry", which had moved across the Atlantic from the Bermuda Islands in recent days.
Violent storms and thunderstorms are expected mainly in Baden.
According to DWD, gusts of 60 to 90 kilometers per hour can still be expected in the Black Forest, the Swabian Alb and in the Bodenseekreis until Thursday morning.
On Friday, the sun can be seen again longer.
The crowd does it: Every two years, the car manufacturers at the IAA show their novelties.
In 2015, there is above all movement in the compact cars, which are currently selling particularly well.
Cars from the compact class are currently in particular demand: According to the Federal Motor Transport Authority (KBA), around 28.1 percent of new registrations accounted for this segment in August alone.
This is also reflected at the IAA (Audience Days 17-27).
Between all the futuristic concept vehicles and new luxury and luxury cars, many new compact models in particular will be presented this year.
One of the biggest premieres for the German car industry is the Opel Astra, which will stand at the dealerships in the autumn.
"The Astra is an extremely important model for us," Opel CEO Karl-Thomas Neumann said at the trade fair.
It belongs to the core of the brand.
So that the volume model also sells well, the new Astra should not become much more expensive.
Opel expects an entrance below the biggest competitor VW Golf, which starts at €17 €650.
The current Astra costs from 16 990 euros.
The Astra is available with a new 1.4-litre engine with 74 kW/100 hp.
In addition, the Hessians offer further diesel and petrol engines from 70 kW/95 hp to 147 kW//200 hp, which include three-cylinder petrol engines for the first time.
Unlike the most important competitors VW Golf and Ford Focus, the Opel Astra is initially only available as a five-door.
As a second variant will follow in the spring of the Kombi, which can also be seen at the IAA.
And in addition to the Astra at the IAA, what else is being driven up with new compact cars?
Renault Mégane: Renault sends the fourth generation of its Astra competitor Mégane into the race.
At the trade fair, the Mégane can only be seen as a five-door in a conventional and an emphatically sporty GT version.
He will go on sale early next year, then also as a station wagon.
Infiniti: The Infiniti Q30 is completely new in the segment.
The Nissan offshoot wants to find its position in the rapidly growing segment with the new model.
The compact Infiniti with its curved lines fits fully into the brand’s design line.
The basic model will have a gasoline engine with an output of at least 90 kW/122 hp.
However, there is also a diesel with 80 kW/109 hp or 125 kW/170 hp.
Suzuki Baleno: Also new in the segment is Suzuki Baleno, which celebrates its world premiere in Frankfurt.
The model is offered with gasoline engines, including a newly developed 1.0-litre turbo engine and an output of 82 kW/111 hp.
According to the manufacturer’s specifications, the maximum torque is 170 Newton meters (Nm).
Baleno’s sales launch in Europe is in the spring of 2016.
DS 4: Not completely new, but completely renewed is the compact class model DS 4, which can be seen at the IAA.
On the outside, the designers of the noble Citroën offshoot put their hand on the front: here, the radiator grille was redrawn and the changed headlights were equipped with LED technology.
In the interior, the infotainment system in particular has been modernized, the number of buttons has been reduced by a touchscreen and Apple's CarPlay was integrated for the first time.
In addition, a number of revisions and new variants such as the refreshed Mercedes A-Class or the Peugeot 308 GTI are joining the IAA in the compact class.
JVA Berlin-Tegel: Five square meters of cells are inhumane
The Federal Constitutional Court has justified an ex-detainee of the Tegel correctional facility, which had been housed for months in a too small cell.
Due to the violation of their human dignity, detainees are generally entitled to financial compensation if they are accommodated in too-small cells.
This was decided by the Federal Constitutional Court in a decision published on Wednesday.
According to the judges, those affected can even hope for money if they have only been imprisoned for a few days (Az.: 1 BvR 1127/14).
Thus, an ex-detainee with his constitutional complaint was successful in Karlsruhe: He was housed in a solitary cell with around five square meters of floor space between June and November 2000; the toilet was not spatially separated.
After that, the man, who according to his lawyer is now back on the loose, had been moved to a larger cell.
His official liability lawsuit because of the undignified prison conditions against the state of Berlin failed.
However, the constitutional judges overturned the verdict of the Berlin Court of Appeal and dismissed the case for re-examination.
They see the man's human dignity violated.
Chamber court now decides on the amount of compensation
Even if, for legal reasons from the period in question, only a few days of imprisonment were decisive for the compensation, there was a claim for compensation, it said.
Otherwise, "a stunting of the legal protection of the personality would have to be feared."
The Supreme Court must now clarify the amount of monetary compensation.
Constitutional judges have often strengthened the rights of convicts in the past.
Thus, they did not award damages to a prisoner until April, because he was locked naked in a cell.
According to a decision made in 2011, the multiple occupancy of a cell without a separate toilet is also unworthy.
In addition, Karlsruhe also confirmed the Berlin Constitutional Court on Wednesday, which had attesteded a detainee in a parallel case in November 2009 an inhumane accommodation.
Over three months, the then plaintiff was accommodated daily for between 15 and 21 hours in a similar cell as in the Karlsruhe case.
Berlin's prisons had also hit the headlines in recent weeks because of lack of staff.
Prisoners are therefore repeatedly included for 23 hours, the aggressiveness increases, even brawls should have occurred because of the tense situation.
According to the judiciary press office, 21 positions in Tegel are not filled due to the lack of young talent, 19 are to be eliminated in 2016.
Angela Merkel and Refugee Policy: "We can do this"
Immigration of refugees is an enormous challenge - the chancellor is determined to accept it.
What's coming to Germany?
According to forecasts of the Federal Ministry of the Interior, around 800 000 asylum seekers will come to Germany this year, according to Vice Chancellor Sigmar Gabriel (SPD), Germany is even able to accept one million refugees.
In view of the unusual challenge, Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) has demonstrated determination: "I say again and again: We can do this, and we can do it".
The following overview conveyes the following overview with Germany.
According to different projections, between 350000 (estimation of the Federal Ministry of Construction) and 400000 (estimation of the Pestel Institute) apartments are needed annually - for the next five years.
This is almost twice as much as has been built so far (250000 apartments).
However, the lack of housing is particularly dramatic with affordable rental properties: 60000 to 80000 social homes fall out of the rental price fixing every year, and refugees will also apply for these particularly cheap apartments.
Federal Building Minister Barbara Hendricks (SPD) therefore wants to double the funding, from currently 518 million to one billion euros.
From the Pestel Institute’s point of view, this is far too little: 80 000 social housing units per year would have to be created and the federal government would have to invest funding of 6.4 billion per year.
Scientists and Federal Minister of construction agree that there must also be more tax advantages for investments in housing construction.
According to estimates by the Institute for Labour Market and Occupational Research, it can be assumed that in the long term 55 percent of refugees in Germany can be employed.
However, the refugees in Germany would not be systematically asked about their qualifications to help them get started, criticizes Claudia Walter, project manager for integration and education in the Bertelsmann Foundation.
For the integration of refugees, however, it is crucial whether they find work in Germany.
Work is the key for social contacts, for appreciation in the host society, but also for the self-esteem of immigrants, says migration researcher Dietrich Thränhardt.
According to Thränhardt, there has been a political turnaround in Germany: from the ban on working for refugees to the recognition of work.
While there used to be a ban on work for asylum seekers of up to five years, this was reduced to three months in November 2014, as was the residency requirement that restricts asylum seekers to one place of residence.
In society, also because of the shortage of skilled workers, the majority has prevailed that refugees should be given access to the labour market more quickly.
A major hurdle for access to the labour market is a lack of knowledge of German.
So far, there is not yet a sufficient range of general and professional language courses.
Research also shows that workplaces are most frequently conveyed through personal contacts.
According to Thränhardt, the construction of networks is therefore the royal road to work integration.
The Berlin school administration has determined that only a few refugee parents give their children to the kindergartens, an estimated ten to 15 percent: "First of all, it is very important for the uprooted parents to keep the family together," says Ilja Koschembar, spokesman for youth and family in the Berlin Senate administration.
In order to convince the family of the advantages of the day-care center, the administration distributes handouts in various languages.
Should the need for educators rise sharply due to the large number of refugees, the Berlin Senate would try to increase training capacities - for example, by allowing more technical schools.
In the countries, pupils with no knowledge of German are prepared for lessons in welcome classes.
However, they are not always taught by fully trained teachers, but, as in Berlin, also by teachers who only have one qualification in German as a second language.
There is no shortage of them, says Beate Stoffers of the Berlin school administration.
The German Student Union estimates that about 20 percent of refugees want to study in Germany.
In principle, no federal state prohibits asylum seekers or tolerated their studies - the only exception so far has been Berlin, but Interior Senator Frank Henkel (CDU) has now abandoned his stance.
Refugees willing to study could facilitate access to the university.
Refugees with a residence permit or tolerance no longer receive benefits under the Asylum Seekers Benefits Act when they start their studies.
In principle, the latter do not receive a bafög, as they do not fall under the circle of persons entitled to bafög.
Tolerated refugees must have lived in Germany for four years so far in order to be Bafög-eligible, from 1 January 2016 this range is shortened to 15 months.
Steffen Krach, Berlin’s State Secretary for Science, and the German Student Union also have to be closed, this funding gap must also be closed.
The students should get Bafög immediately.
Islam will play a more important role in the future, because many refugees are Muslims, and for many, religion is an important part of their identity.
The state should successively grant Islam the same rights as the churches have, and the existing state-church right to a new state-religion relationship.
It would also be important to establish an Islamic welfare association that professionally takes care of the new arrivals.
The rights also include responsibilities.
Thus, the representatives of Islam must agree on clear contacts and be willing to cooperate with the state in many areas and give it certain possibilities of control.
This also includes the willingness to crack down on religious fundamentalism.
The chairman of the Central Council of Muslims, Aiman Mazyek, expressed concern on Thursday that religious conflicts could also be carried into Germany.
Those who wanted to carry out such conflicts in Germany "have immediately forfeited their right to stay here, in Germany," he said in the Bavarian Radio.
He proposed translating the Basic Law in Arabic.
In the future, integration will still be an issue for the whole of society than today.
This will also have to change the majority society, the reduction of discrimination and racism becomes more urgent.
As a result of European decisions, Germany has had an anti-discriminatory body and a general equality law for almost a decade.
But it learns very slowly, as the NSU murder series showed, in which police and authorities systematically ignored the racist motive.
Equal opportunities for everyone: What migrant organizations and the interest groups of non-white Germans have long demanded and what international agreements such as the Anti-Racism Convention Germany obligate must become a reality in the next few years, if the country is to remain peaceful and its new citizens use their opportunities - for the benefit of all.
In general, Germany is able to provide medical care to the previously expected refugees, says President Frank Ulrich Montgomery.
"As far as man- power and capacities are concerned, we can do that," he told the Tagesspiegel.
However, additional money must flow into the system.
To avoid bottlenecks, Montgomery calls for all arrivals to be handed over health cards immediately upon registration.
Services provided could later be billed with the federal states.
And in the long term, it is highly questionable to dissipate so many people with the limited catalogue of services provided for by the Asylum Seeker Benefits Act.
Otherwise, the doctors above all create the language barriers.
Already so far, the Berlin health scientist Thea Borde has proven, migrants are better educated about their diseases than other patients.
Not only in clinics, but also in doctor's offices, interpreting costs would have to be covered, she demands.
However, many refugees would already be helped with more general medical contact points in the emergency ambulances.
and with a different diagnostic look.
Among refugee children, for example, there are significantly more untreated disabilities, and adolescents are about HIV education.
However, German doctors would be able to cope with the partly other other clinical pictures from their education, Montgomery thinks.
"Parasite infestations or tuberculosis are not so difficult to detect". And as far as the shortage of doctors is concerned, relief may even be in sight by the refugees.
The Syrians have a highly developed education system, a number of refugees are even doctors.
However, in order to practice, they would have to prove their qualifications and possibly take exams again.
According to estimates of the Federal Psychotherapist Chamber (BPtK), at least half of the refugees arriving in Germany are mentally ill: Most suffer from post-traumatic stress disorder or depression.
Refugee children are particularly vulnerable, explains Dietrich Munz, president of the BPtK.
A recently published study by the Technical University of Munich shows that one in five Syrian refugee children arrives traumatized in Germany.
Anyone suffering from post-traumatic stress disorder suffers, among other things, from so-called flashbacks with shortness of breath, dizziness, heart racing and fears of death.
Other symptoms include sleep and concentrating disorders, severe terribity and emotional numbness.
In order to prevent the symptoms from becoming chronic, an early treatment is necessary, Munz explained.
To date, however, only four percent of mentally ill refugees receive psychotherapy.
Although mentally ill asylum seekers can submit an application for psychotherapy in the social welfare offices, the processing usually takes several months.
In addition, in the social welfare offices, specialists or doctors who are not trained for mental illnesses would decide whether therapy is necessary or not.
This often leads to misjudgments, says Munz.
Manege invites you to dream
With the new "Passionship", the Circus Probst draws on the traditions of circus art.
The aim is to captivate every age of fascinating acrobatics, animal dressage and unique entertainment.
Now the Circus Probst invites you to dream into the manege on the brick wash.
“Our passion is the circus.”
"I can't imagine living without my circus family," says Stephanie Probst, animal teacher and junior boss.
"Passion" - under this motto is the current manegen staging.
For more than 33 years, the circus has been in the hands of the Probst family.
The gift to inspire lies in the family’s blood.
"It's important to us to get people away from their daily grind and take them to a completely different world for two hours," stresses the 27-year-old junior boss.
"Manegen cocktail with cosmopolitan flair," is what director Reinhard Probst calls his concept.
50 cities on the almost 3 000 kilometre tour can now experience this flair.
The program of the circus impresses with talented artists from all over the world.
Awarded at the international circus festival in Monte Carlo, the Russian Stoliarov Clown Family captivates the audience with wit and charm.
The Castillo Brothers also understand their craft.
With the heroes of the silent films, the Cuban duo wants to easily record.
Ease and agility - with these words they identify.
Her humorous art on the mast is supposed to take women's hearts by storm.
In addition to numerous salmon salvos, the acrobatics of the Circus Probst also fascinates with sensitivity and articulation.
Balancing with the unicycle on a ball?
It's not a problem for He Yuan.
Snake people fascinate and terrify at the same time.
Wang Lin from China is familiar with the oldest form of acrobatics like few others and fascinates with its mobility.
60 employees from twelve nations offer diversity in every respect.
"The animals belong to our family," Stephanie's mother, Brigitte Probst points out.
Grievances in keeping circust animals are all too often an issue at various animal welfare organisations.
Stephanie Probst also comments on this: "If our animals aren't doing well, we're not doing well either."
Around 80 animals from five different continents enjoy each petting unit.
The "four-legged artists", as they are affectionately called, every wish is read from the eyes if possible.
We want to work with the animals and have fun with them.
If the animal doesn't feel well, it doesn't wan't want to get into the crowd either.
The top priority, therefore: take good care of the animals.
Specifically dedicated to her Zebroid Jumbo, a mixture of a ShetlandPony stallion and a zebra mare, the Probst family is proud.
The two animals lived together for eight years in an enclosure and nothing happened.
"But then, all of a sudden, our little jumbo was there," Brigitte Probst recounts.
The zebroid isn’t ready yet for the crowd, but it’s the unique mascot of the Circus Probst.
There should therefore be something for every visitor.
"One thing is certain," says Stephanie Probst: "Whether five or 1 200 spectators, we always do our best and are there with all our hearts".
Circus Probst invites you to its performances every day at 5 p.m. and 8 p.m. from 17 to 20 September.
On Sunday, the program begins at 11 a.m. and 4 p.m.
Thursday is family day, for the performance at 5 p.m. the prices are discounted.
In addition, the animal show is open daily from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.
Zimbabwe's Robert Mugabe gives false speech in parliament
Mr Mugabe, Africa's oldest leader, fell from a podium down the stairs earlier this year.
He remained unharmed, but the video of the case quickly circulated via social media.
Parliament's opening was also clouded by claims by opposition MPs that they had received anonymous death threats and they were warned against accompanying Mr Mugabe's address with booing calls.
Last month they had booed him and interrupted him in parliament during his State of the Union address - this is the speech he repeated on Tuesday.
Movement for Democratic Change (MDC) Spokesman Innocent Gonese said that seven opposition politicians have received text message messages on their cellphones that warned them against disturbing Mr. Mugabe's address.
"The news comes from a number that isn't displayed, but it has the title 'Death,'" he told journalists after Mugabe's speech.
It warns the affected members of parliament that immunity ends in parliament, and as soon as they leave parliament, their parliamentary immunity is no longer effective.
The chief spokesperson said Morgan Tsvangirai's party was "worried" about the dangers to politicians.
Hewlett-Packard will cut up to 30,000 jobs
Hewlett-Packard is expected to cut 25,000 to 30,000 jobs as part of the restructuring and saving efforts at the company's services.
HP will later split into two publicly traded companies later this year, separating the computer and printer business from its faster-growing corporate hardware and service operations.
Expected job cuts will result in over $2.7 billion from the fourth-quarter, HP said in a statement.
"These restructuring measures will allow for a more competitive, sustainable cost structure for the new Hewlett-Packard enterprise," said Meg Whitman, HP's chairman and chief executive, who is scheduled to lead the unit after the break-up.
“Hewlett Packard Enterprise will be smaller and more focused than HP is today, and we have a broad and in-depth portfolio of stores that support companies in transitioning to a new style of business,” Whitman said.
As a stand-alone company, we are better positioned than ever before to meet the changing needs of our customers around the world.
Hewlett Packard Enterprise has more than $50 billion in annual revenue and "will be focused on deploying previously unmatched, integrated technology solutions for businesses," according to a statement from the company.
It divides a company founded in the 1930s by graduates of Stanford University Bill Hewlett and Dave Packard to manufacture electrical equipment and whose Palo Alto Garage was called "the birthplace of Silicon Valley."
HP has undergone a massive restructuring to manage the departure from traditional PCs towards mobile devices.
HP's measure, the world's second-largest PC maker and one of the largest U.S. tech companies, is the latest in this area, based on the belief that tightly-focused companies perform better.
FC Arsenal captain Mikel Arteta says team-mates will have to stand behind injured Jack Wilshere ahead of game against Dynamo Zagreb
Wenger emphasized to Wilshere that it is only September and if his rehabilitation goes according to plan, there is no reason why he cannot play again before Christmas.
This optimistic scenario already means that Wilshere will surely miss at least the next four games for England and, given his history, his involvement in the European Championship next summer is certainly still uncertain.
The great shame on this current problem is that Wilshere had finished the last season strongly after a separate ankle injury and then found his way back to his old momentum during pre-season.
"This kind of injury doesn't threaten the career," Wenger said.
It's a bone that didn't heal well.
That's not a bigger matter.
This was just an accident.
It wasn't related to his ankles or with the injuries he had before.
This may be true from a purely medical point of view, but as Wenger also acknowledged, there is the associated danger of further injuries whenever a player tries to return in the middle of the season.
An unpleasant pattern also develops.
Premier Injuries Ltd's investigation records 23 differing illnesses of Wilshere since its debut six years ago.
He has also played in just 65 of Arsenal's 157 league games in the past five seasons.
The broader picture for Arsenal is that they have been one of the two Premier League clubs in six out of eight seasons since 2007/08, which have the most injury days.
Wenger accepts the potential of a vicious cycle, but Arsenal believe an improvement on the injury quota will continue during last season.
"Sometimes when you come back from an injury, you'll have a susceptible period of time, a time when you need to strengthen your body and gain fighting power," Wenger said.
Jack's young enough to overcome that.
I hope his body stabilises, I'm confident he's going to do it and he'll go through a career of the kind he deserves.
Arsenal begin their Champions League season on Wednesday night against Dinamo Zagreb ahead of Saturday's matches and against Tottenham Hotspur next Wednesday, and Wenger is adjusting a rotation within the team.
Héctor Bellerín and Aaron Ramsey have not travelled to Croatia, which means Mathieu Debuchy and Arteta are likely to start.
Wenger can also start with Kieran Gibbs and Olivier Giroud instead of Nacho Monreal and Theo Walcott.
"I'm trying to maintain balance and give the players who need to take a little breather," Wenger said.
Although Arsenal are clear favourites to progress through Group F, the Maksimir Stadium in Zagreb is among the playgrounds with the greatest intimidating effect in Europe.
Dynamo is also unbeaten after 41 games after the "invincible" campaign launched last season.
The stadium was rocked by an earthquake on Tuesday evening, measuring 3.2 on the judge's scale.
This year it was the 10th in Zagreb.
Wenger is of the opinion that Arsenal have drawn the lesson from last season with the Champions League capitulation against Monaco and remain driven by the distant hope of leading the club to its first European Cup triumph with his management.
"This drive is immense," Wenger said.
It's never worked out at Arsenal.
We were very close to it in 2006, but on the other side I've been in business long enough to be realistic.
We're ambitious, but we'ren't dreamers.
FriendsFest: the comedy show that gives us serious lessons about men's friendships
The absence of similar depictions in shows is obvious, especially with regard to how the need for men to open up has now become even more desperate than ever, as evidenced by suicide rates among young men at a 15-year high.
In 2013, it was the main cause of death among men aged 20 to 45 in the UK, and it remains three times more common in men than women.
In an effort to tackle the issue, the Campaign Against Living Miserably (CALM - campaign against a pathetious life) announced 2015 as the man's year, with the aim of questioning the culture that prevents men from seeking help when they need it.
Friendship is at the heart of fighting to overcome this challenge.
A recent study among more than 2,000 high-school students with depressive symptoms found that if the sufferer had a sufficiently high percentage of friends with "healthy mood," his chances of recovery doubled.
For those who are not depressed, a circle of mentally healthy friends halves the risks of mental problems.
But, of course, you have to have friends so that a friendship can work.
In the age of social media-led communication, with its emphasis on style about substance, the immediacy of the answer is often more important than meaningful contact.
Our digital lives have doubled the need to appear strong, fun-loving and successful, even if in reality isolation dominates.
This superficial success is dominated by what psychologists call extrinsic values; money, image, social status, everything at the expense of intrinsic values - our inner longing for personal development and friendship that affects our mental health much more profoundly.
And as a video game inventor and billionaire recently explained, money really can't buy happiness.
The nice thing about Friends was that this focus was turned upside down.
Each character was not defined by his work, but by his personality, which shone through the interaction as a friend.
The humour of Joey and Chandler's frequent hugs, moments while watching football in cozy armchairs, and the longing of Ross for Rachel - all of which came from the knowledge that men can definitely relate to it, even if they often restrict from fully exploring their feelings.
As a telephone chaplaincy employee explained it to me, "my listening role for people who feel depressed and suicidally-prone, have really clearly shown me how even the smallest interactions of friends can make a difference."
The men who contact us often feel unable to talk to friends.
The old idiom "I'm getting along with it" has an enormous effect, like the feeling that it's perceived as a weakness when talking openly about problems."
Friends is one of the shows that comes to my mind when I try to offer longer-term coping mechanisms.
She clearly presents the small moments of men's friendships.
As much as my objective outsider status over the phone may help people in moments of desperation, long-term support comes from a personal network of family and friends.
And so, if you're visiting Comedy Central FriendsFest this weekend with the completely redesigned backdrop in celebration of the day, don't you take your friends with you for a drink at Central Perk.
Or, even better, if you see someone sitting around in Starbucks, give them a smile and say hello.
You'll never know the difference your friendship might make.
Friends will be broadcast on Comedy Central from start to finish - the entire 236 episodes - for #FriendsFest
Di Maria meets to help PSG crush Malmö
Angel Di Maria celebrates with Blaise Matuidi and Edinson Cavani after his hit against Malmö.
Di Maria scores on his debut in the Champions League on Tuesday and helps PSG move past Malmö.
Here's the match report and the reaction to the game.
Angel Di Maria and Edinson Cavani held course when Paris Saint-Germain kicked out its Champions League season with an extremely comfortable 2-0 victory against Malmö at Prinzenparkstadion on Tuesday.
Di Maria scored less than four minutes after the start of his Champions League debut as a PSG player who put the French champions on their way, but after that they missed a number of occasions before finally increasing their advantage when Cavani scored a header in the 61st minute.
With Real Madrid and Schachtjor Donetsk also in Group A, Paris needs a strong start against the Swedish champion, as they not only aim for qualification for the knockout rounds, but an improvement on the way to the quarter-finals in each of the last three campaigns.
But while they got the points, it wasn't a class play from Laurent Blanc's team with Zlatan Ibrahimovic's missed chances in front of goal. He was later substituted in the game against his hometown team and against the club, with whom he began his illustrious career as early as 1999.
"It's not easy to win at home or away in the Champions League. Therefore it's a good start to win 2:0, causing our opponents problems and looking backing back relaxed, even if there are certain things that we still have to perfect over the course of the season," Blanc said.
“That’s a good sign.
We wanted to win and we won, although I regret that we didn't score another one or two more goals."
Ibrahimovic returned to the Paris team after his injury in one of three changes that was able to hold a 2:2 draw on their own pitch against Bordeaux in the First Division at the weekend, but he was knocked out by Di Maria in the fourth minute.
The Argentine winger has been referred to as the man who can take PSG to the next level in Europe when he was taken over by Manchester United last month, and he hinted at why, when he ran into Marco Verratti's pass behind the Malmö defence, before closing past Johan Wiland nicely into the long corner of the net.
However, if anyone believed that PSG would score a big win, he was disappointed.
Malmö, who beat Celtic in a play-off to reach that stage, competed at kick-off with nine national players on the field and was a tough opponent with a five-man defence and two lined up deep in midfield.
But except for a shot from Nikola Djurdjic, who walked wide past Kevin Trapp's posts in the 34th minute, they offered little in attack.
Instead, PSG sometimes gave himself up, with some mispasses and bad finishes, not least from Ibrahimovic, who pitched at no fewer than five tries alone in the first half and was then given up after Cavani deployed him after the restart.
Nevertheless, he proved much more skill when he brought his fellow players into the game, and it was a pass from Ibrahimovic that Di Maria converted into a turning shot that was steered past the post by Wiland in the 52nd minute ...
The second goal finally fell shortly after the hour mark thanks to Cavani. The Uruguayan scored his sixth goal of the season after Ibrahimovic fished up a left flank from Maxwell.
That ended all thoughts that Malmö could get back into the game, and only an excellent intervention by Wiland from the shortest distance to stop David Luiz could keep the end result low before substitute Ezequiel Lavezzi was knocked off a goal at the end.
Malmö's coach, Age Hareide, later admitted that PSG played a class over his team, and the Norwegian said: "Our last Champions League game was in December last year.
We've only played in the Swedish league that doesn't have the same level.
The qualifying matches aren’t enough.”
Hat's off to Paris!
It's been a great game from them!
Glencore raises $2.5 billion through stock sales
Switzerland-based mining giant Glencore, which is affected by collapsing commodity prices, raised £2.5bn on Wednesday through a stock sale as part of its huge debt repayment plan.
London-registered Glencore said in a statement that it has sold approximately £1.6 billion worth of new shares to pay off its own debt.
The company, which has lost 57 percent of its market value this year, is struggling with falling commodity prices as China's economic downturn pushes up demand and has a devastating impact on markets.
The issuanciation of subscription rights earned Glencore's shared price in trading early Wednesday morning a rapid rise to the top of the London Stock Exchange.
The stock jumped 2.77 percent to 131.60 pence on the FTSE 100 index, which opened 0.69 percent higher.
Glencore last week announced the $2.5bn share sale as part of the broader plans to repay around a third of its $30bn debt.
The company sold the new holdings at 125 pence per share, amounting to a 2.4 percent discount on Tuesday's closing price.
There were 1.3 billion shares worth up to 9.99 percent of the group.
Concerns regarding long-term inhibited growth in China have cut iron ore prices by about half, while coal, copper and other commodity prices have declined by 20 to 40 percent.
Refugees arrive in Weimar
On Tuesday noon, 49 people reach the city administration in Weimar after a mostly long escape.
Refugees come from Syria, Albania, Afghanistan, Macedonia and Somalia.
The youngest arrival is a six-month-old girl from Syria.
White House confirms Xi Jinping's visit to Washington
Chinese President Xi Jinping will make a state visit to the U.S. at the end of the month, the White House confirmed in a statement Tuesday.
The visit, Xi's first official one in Washington, will take place on September 25 and reciprocates President Barack Obama's trip to Beijing last November, the statement said.
U.S. president and first-lady Michelle Obama will also host Xi and his wife Peng Liyuan at a state dinner this evening after the two leaders have held bilateral talks.
"President Xi's visit will provide an opportunity to expand American-Chinese cooperation on a number of global, regional and bilateral issues of common interest, but at the same time will also give President Obama and President Xi an opportunity to tackle areas of disagreements constructively," the statement adds.
XI, whose visit was initially announced in February, is expected to meet several U.S. technology leaders in Seattle on his way to Washington before moving on to the U.N. General Assembly in September.
Obama and Xi are expected to raise a number of contentious issues, including Washington's concerns regarding alleged cyberespionage by China and its increasing self-confidence in the disputed waters of the South China Sea.
440-horsepower sports cars borrowed and driven to scrap
A 29-year-old wanted to fulfill a dream and borrowed a Ford Mustang.
On the rain-soaked highway came the rude awakening.
With a company in the district of Gotha you can fulfill the dream and once a V8 US sports car Ford Mustang Boss 302 with 440 hp and drive.
That's how a 29-year-old from Gotha County did.
On the A 71 near Arnstadt, the dream was over and over.
The 29-year-old lost control of the sports car, got into a slingshot on wet road and drove into the guardrail.
The sports car suffered total damage, no one was injured.
The property damage is estimated at EUR 40,000.
Xavi: Pep Guardiola prevented move to Bayern in 2008
World and European champion Xavi has been playing for al-Sadd Sport Club in Qatar since summer 2015
Bayern were obviously on the verge of signing Spain's superstar Xavi a few years ago, who played for FC Barcelona at the time.
Eight times Spanish champion, three times cup winner, four times Champions League winners, twice club world champions, two times European champion, once world champion - the title list of Xavi is breathtaking.
In the summer, the 35-year-old left FC Barcelona after 24 years and moved to al-Sadd Sport Club in Qatar.
But in an interview with Marca, Xavi now reveals that he was already facing his departure from Barcelona before the Euro 2008.
“They told me that Barça wanted to sell me.”
"Madrid won everything at the time, we didn't win anything," he recounts.
FC Bayern also registered this.
Xavi: "There was an offer from Bayern."
“Rummenigge wanted me.”
As a reminder: In the summer of 2008, Jürgen Klinsmann took over FC Bayern.
Guardiola was the reason Xavi didn't switch
However, the change did not come about.
And the decisive man for this is working today for the German record champion: Pep Guardiola.
Xavi explains why: "Barça signed Guardiola and I played a great European Championship."
“Pep told me that I wouldn’t change, that he couldn’t imagine the squad without me.”
“He got me with that.”
Who knows how Bayern's 2008/2009 season would have gone with Xavi.
Xavi stood on the turf for Barca in the quarter-finals of the Champions League against Bayern over the entire distance, knocked out the Reds (4:0, 1:1) and celebrated the premier class triumph at the end.
Matt Damon downplays diversity in filmmaking
Matt Damon confronted African-American filmmaker Effie Brown during a discussion about the diversity and said she wasn't needed behind-the-scenes.
During an episode of Sunday Night of HBO's Project Greenlight, the veteran actor Brown interrupted when she emphasized the need for alternative perspectives with regard to a black protagonist in the project's chosen script.
"If we're talking about diversity, then don't do it with the film's casting," Damon assured.
Brown reacted in shock, with insulted disbelief and shouted "Hoo! Wow. Okay" as the actor's comments cemented themselves.
Damon and Brown joined producer colleagues Ben Affleck and the Farrelly brothers to discuss the show finalists.
"I'd like to urge people to think about - whoever the director is going to be - how they treat the character of Harmony," Brown initially said.
The only black person, and she's a hooker beaten by her white pimp.
Dear White People's producer campaigned for finalists Leo Angelos and Kristen Brancaccio for the directing rights to the film; Damon retorted that in the end "could give us something we don't want."
After the discussion aired, social media exploded before disapproval over Damon's comments.
Twitter user Mr Pooni expressed his anger over the clip by writing "Matt Damon is talking about the only black person in the room, so he can explain his diversity - that's so WHITE that it hurts."
The tweet earned more than 5,000 retweets and 4,000 likes since its release.
Damon's comments come at a time when accurate racial representation in the media is at the forefront of societal discussion.
After teenage Michael Brown was killed by a white police officer in 2014, the Black Lives Matter movement took effect nationwide, urging legislatures and authorities to impose discriminatorising practices against colorful.
In February, singer Prince made an allusion to the movement during the introduction of nominated albums of the year at the Grammy Awards.
"Album's silent matter," (albums still have their meaning) he said.
Like books and black lives, even albums still have their meaning.
More recently, leaders of the Black Lives Matter movement have released a statement in response to the question of Fox-News personality Elisabeth Hasselbeck about whether the group should be considered a hate group.
"The Black Lives Matter network is a love group," the leaders said.
It was recently announced that Damon was on a list of entertainers, producers and media mogules who will be invited to visit the pope to discuss the depiction of the Catholic Church in the West.
Whether he accepted the invitation or has already met the pope is unclear.
'American Ninja Warrior' crowns first champion
Professional sports climber and assistant Isaac Caldiero became the first athlete to win the American Ninja Warrior on Monday night and take home the €1 million Grand Prize.
The competition show, which just finished its 7th season, had never witnessed a competition participant who was able to complete all stages of his obstacle course.
During the season finale, two men, Caldiero and cinematographer Geoff Britten, made it to the finals.
One of the last task was climbing on a 75-foot-long climbing rope in 30 seconds.
Britten completed the task within time, making him the first athlete to complete American Ninjni Warrior's strenuous Phase 4. However, Caldiero ended this challenge a full three seconds faster, making him the winner.
Caldiero spoke to E!news after the hard-fought win, still under the shock of winning the £1 million Grand Prix.
"I mean, it's amazing thinking about, I've never earned more than $10,000 or had it to live," Isaac E! said of his heaps of money.
All of a sudden, like I can't even imagine what it's like... the future is unknown.
Lead producer Kent Weed sat down with The Hollywood Reporter to discuss the finale and why Britten, though he was the first ever to finish the course, doesn't receive any prize money.
According to the rules, the money goes to the fastest person.
"If there's more than one finalist, it'll get the one with the fastest time," Weed said.
How much the money is a wonderful prize and changes life, it receives the great gratification.
I don't think he harbors any resentments about it.
He's just so happy doing what he's done.
He received crowds of awards from fans.
He's such a great guy and a family man.
He's a hero for his children and his wife.
Geoff said to me, "If I should be beaten by someone, then I'm glad it's Isaac."
And I think Isaac would have felt the same way if they've swapped roles.
America Ninjni Warrior has been renewed for an eighth season, with filming starting in the spring of 2016.
Detroit Tigers sneak past Minnesota Twins
MINNEAPOLIS--The second half of the season wasn't particularly funny for the Detroit Tigers.
They used to be one of the best teams in baseball once, today the Tigers are in last place in the American League Central.
The Tigers are reduced to their role as spoilers but played this part perfectly on Tuesday against the Minnesota Twins with 5-4 at Target Field.
The Twins squandered a chance to rise within a half of the Houston Astros for the second American League wild-card spot.
For Detroit, if only for one night, it was a return of the three-letter F-word that was so elusive over a large part of the second half of the season.
"All victories are fun, no matter how it comes about," Tiger's manager Brad Ausmus said.
Tiger pitcher Alfredo Simon maintained his early offensive support when Detroit scored three times in the first two innings, then once in the sixth and then again in the ninth.
Simon (13-9) pitched 6 times in the 1/3 innings and allowed three runs.
It was only his third successful start in his last nine games.
"I want to end strongly this year," Simon said.
It's hard to throse every game in the bullpen, and that's why I want to go into the game very focused.
Tiger-hitter-designate Victor Martinez had three hits and was in two runs, his first RBIs since mid-August.
