He didn't do particularly well, but he caught the right spot.
Hughes was in a pitch-count, scoring no points in the third inning before being replaced by reliever right-hander A.J.Achter.
Hughes resulted in three runs on six hits and a walk with four strikeouts.
NOTIONS: Tiger's RHP Anibal Sanchez retired because of an injury to the throwing shoulder.
He is examined by Dr. James Andrews, who underwept Sanchez's shoulder in 2007.
Sanchez, who has been on the injured list since mid-August because of a shoulder strait, is due to be activated for kick-off on Wednesday.
With Sanchez out of the game, LHP Daniel Morrirs will be removed from the injured list to start against the Twins.
Norris with 1-1 on four starts with the Tigers since he was acquired in exchange with LHP David Price on July 30
He's been missing since August 20 because of a strain in his right hip joint.
The Tigers and the Twins will finish their streak of three games at Target Field on Wednesday
Norris (2-2.4.43 ERA) will play Minnesota's RHP Ervin Santana (5-4, 4.73)
Hundreds of refugees to be accommodated in Wiesbaden
A train with hundreds of refugees is on its way to Hesse.
His arrival was expected for the night on Wednesday.
The people arriving in it are then to be accommodated in emergency shelters in Wiesbaden, as the city of Wiesbaden announced on Tuesday evening.
She was expecting 450 to 750 arriving refugees.
Several sports halls of the Hessian state capital had been prepared for this case in recent days.
Up to 1000 refugees can be accommodated there temporarily.
They are then to be redistributed to other municipalities.
In the past ten days alone, 7500 people had arrived in Hesse, according to Giessen.
Frankfurt and Hanau have also set up emergency shelters in sports halls because of the large number of refugees.
Ischinger: German military measures in Syria an option
The head of the Munich Security Conference, Wolfgang Ischinger, considers military measures with German participation in Syria a possibility.
"I believe the decisive reflection on military options for action must not take place without Germany," he said.
He accused the European states of "collective looking away."
Four years ago, it was glad that the chalice of military involvement in Germany had passed.
"Now the conflict lands crashingly on our doorstep," he said on Deutschlandfunk.
The former German ambassador in Washington also called for a massive increase in international aid on the ground for the refugees.
Austria controlled at southern and eastern borders
After Germany, Austria has also reintroduced checks at its southern and eastern borders.
Austrian Interior Minister Johanna Mikl-Leitner said on the Austrian television channel ORF on Tuesday evening that no one would be sent back to Hungary even now.
Germany also did not send refugees back to Austria.
"No, that's so agreed," she explained.
Asked about an agreement according to which Germany could accept a certain number of refugees from Austria per day, Mikl-Leitner said there had already been talks in this regard between the Director-General for Public Security and representatives of Germany.
Concrete figures did not name them and referred to talks on Wednesday in Berlin.
In recent days, tens of thousands of refugees had come to Austria via the so-called Balkan route from Hungary.
Their destination is mostly Germany.
Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) and her Austrian colleague Werner Faymann, together with several other EU leaders, requested a special summit for next week.
Germany had begun temporary border checks on Sunday.
So far, the EU states have not been able to agree on a common approach in the crisis.
The EU interior ministers want to take next Tuesday (22 September) at a special meeting for the binding distribution of another 120 000 refugees.
In view of the strong number of refugees in Germany, the federal government wants to support the federal states more strongly in the initial reception and distribution of people.
The federal government will manage this together with the countries, Merkel announced late Tuesday evening after almost four hours of deliberations with the prime ministers at the chancellery in Berlin.
Merkel underlined that those people who needed protection would also get it.
However, if you have no prospect of staying, you cannot stay in Germany.
This view had been impressively shared in the round.
The chancellor stressed that the state as a whole must now manage a "huge force effort."
Thousands of desperate refugees are now stuck on the Balkan route.
Hungary had closed its 175-kilometre-long border with Serbia during the night on Tuesday.
A few hours later, the government in Budapest announced that it would also seal off the border with a fence.
This neighbouring country, unlike Serbia, belongs to the EU.
Hungary wants to prevent smugglers from seeking evasive routes via Romania, Hungary's Foreign Minister Peter Szijjarto said on the planned new border fence.
The Romanian government immediately condemned the plan.
There were initial arrests of people who had intersected the fence.
Meanwhile, thousands more migrants made their way to the Greek border in Turkey, according to eyewitness reports.
"Cassini" spacecraft finds ocean on Saturn's moon Enceladus
Under the ice sheet of Saturn's moon Enceladus, a global ocean sloshes.
That's what researchers conclude from observations by the "Cassini" spacecraft.
Accordingly, the moon tumbles slightly on its orbit around the ring planet.
This wobbling movement is only small, but still too large for a consistently solid celestial body, according to a message from Cornell University in Ithaca (U.S. State of New York) on Tuesday.
The researchers present their analyses in the journal "Icarus".
It has long been known that there must be liquid water under the ice cover of Enceladus.
For example, "Cassini" had already discovered ice volcanoes at Saturn's moon's south pole in 2006, spewing water vapour and ice particles and thus feeding Saturn's so-called E-Ring.
At least at the south pole, there must be an underground sea, the astronomers concluded at the time.
The extent of the hidden ocean, however, had not been clarified.
Rama missing 1860 Munich for several weeks
The 1860 Munich has to forgo around a month with offensive actor Valdet Rama.
An investigation has resulted in an adductor injury to the 27-year-old, the club said.
Rama had already complained of pain earlier this week.
According to Münchner Löwen, the diagnosis "probably means four weeks of forced break."
“It’s hard for us, Rama is a bitter loss.”
"Of course we have to think now," sporting director Necat Aygün told the Bild newspaper with a view to the possible commitment of a clubless professional.
As a training guest, however, the Sixties initially presented a defensive man.
The 30-year-old Hrvoje Cale was most recently active in Belgium and used to be under contract with VfL Wolfsburg.
"The Croatian currently has the opportunity to prove his skills."
Goal chaser Rubin Okotie can return to team training after his concussion next week.
Already on Thursday, the Austrian national player is to enter the running training.
Okotie was injured in the game at Fortuna Düsseldorf (0:3) in a violent clash.
Contrary to initial fears, however, he remained without a serious loss.
No diet fruit - mangoes contain a lot of sugar
Mangos are exotic and juicy-sweet.
But if you want to pay attention to your slender line, you are often rather discouraged by them.
Because mangoes contain a relatively large amount of sugar - especially sucrose.
Nevertheless, the exotic fruits are healthy: they contain B-vitamins as well as the vitamins E and C. Above all, however, mangoes contain a lot of vitamin A and its precursor beta-carotene.
Anyone who eats the fruits thus supports eyesight, the immune system and metabolic processes in the body.
When buying, mangoes should not be too soft or too firm and smell pleasant.
The peel color alone says nothing about how ripe the fruit is - so green mangos can also taste very delicious.
They should be stored at room temperature and consumed within a few days.
Then, of course, they taste pure or in sweet food.
However, a mango also gives savoury dishes a special touch.
It softens the slightly bitter taste of some leaf salads, such as chicory and radiccio.
And a mangochutney goes well with fish, poultry, lamb and game.
The U.S. parcel service UPS is hiring up to 95000 temporary workers for the Christmas business in the U.S.
The company has already started recruiting seasonal workers, UPS said on Tuesday.
Drivers in particular are being searched.
Last year, UPS had already increased its workforce by 95000 temporary workers for the holiday season.
Faced with booming online business, UPS and its competitor FedEx have been taking precautions earlier since last year to be prepared for the Christmas business.
In 2013, delayed deliveries had earned the parcel services harsh criticism.
First foehn storm, then striking cold front
In the coming night, in the valleys of the northern side of the foehn breaks through, as Meteoschweiz announced on Tuesday.
On Wednesday and in the night to Thursday, the hair dryer blows partly with storm strength through the foehn valleys.
After blow-drying on Thursday morning, a striking cold front crosses us, bringing heavy rains, gusts of wind and a drop in temperature.
As MeteoNews writes in a note, Switzerland is currently at the front of an extensive low-pressure area above the British Isles in a powerful south-western current.
On the northern side of the Alps, the hairdryer breaks through the valleys during the night to Wednesday.
Subsequently, the hairdryer blows until Thursday morning in the foehn valleys with gusts of 60 to 100 km/h, isolated and especially in Urnerland partly also with over 100 km/h.
The hair dryer can occasionally penetrate to the adjacent Mittelland, and on the mountains hurricane gusts of over 120 km/h are even possible.
While in the north on Wednesday stormy hair dryer ensures summer temperatures of 23 to 27 degrees, the humidity begins to accumulate on the alpine southern slope.
This is where rain sets in, which lasts until Thursday evening.
In total, more than 100 liters of rain per square metre have to be expected south of the Alps in the next 48 hours after large amounts of rain have fallen in recent days.
On Thursday morning, the hairdryer runs out of air, as a result, a cold front rolls with strong, partly thundered rain from west to east over Switzerland.
Together with the cold front, the wind refreshes sharply to stormily, and the temperatures are declining significantly.
The following days present autumn in the north with 15 to 20 degrees, in the south, however, it will be sunnier and warmer on weekends with up to 25 degrees.
Rome now has a Martin Luther Square
Barely 500 years after the Reformation, there is now a Martin Luther Square in Rome.
In the presence of city president Ignazio Marino, a previously nameless square in the park on Oppio's hill was christened in the name of the German reformer on Wednesday.
On the "Piazza Martin Lutero" he is now honored as "German theologian of the Reformation".
The park is right next to the Colosseum in the middle of ancient Rome.
The naming had been requested by the working group of the Protestant churches in Rome.
Jens-Martin Kruse, pastor of the Evangelical Lutheran congregation in Rome, recalled on Wednesday that Protestants in the city of popes for centuries as a minority could often only live their faith in secret.
The square symbolically shows that their tradition of the Christian faith is welcome in Rome.
Martin Luther (1483-1546) spent a few months in Rome as a young Augustinian monk at the turn of the year 1510/11.
At that time, he found the city to be a depraved sin babel.
According to historians, his negative impressions may have reinforced him in his later fight against Rome.
With his thesises against the indulgence trade, Luther had initiated the division of the Western Church.
Hammer artist invested three years in personal interpretation of the Bible
Jesus with the (gilded) crown of thorns is one of the main works that Otmar Alt painted for his Bible.
Klaus Altepost wrote the introduction and updated Biblical texts.
In the beginning was the word?
No, not at the Otmar-Alt-Bible, which has now appeared with magnificent pictures.
In the beginning was the picture for Otmar Alt, who formulated his very personal interpretations of biblical stories on paper and canvas in 150 motifs.
The 75-year-old has retained his "language" with colorful colours and imaginative forms - which, however, is by no means synonymous with "funny" images.
On the contrary: Adapted to the respective topics, this can seem quite gloomy.
Otmar Alt spent around three years working on his Bible project, for which he made a subjective-artistic selection from the many subject areas of the "Book of All Books".
Cycles such as the crossroads, plagues, commandments or Our Father’s Prayers, have arisen.
He spontaneously fascinated several partners: This applies to Luther-Verlag with its theological orientation as well as to Kettler-Verlag with its focus on art books.
And there is above all the author Klaus Altepost, who has dealt with the respective Bible passages on the basis of painting.
He has interpreted the Bible passages selected by Otmar Alt and thus "translated" into today, into current contexts.
These texts are like verbal mirrors of Alt's painting.
They are embedded between Martin Luther’s original Bible quote and one sentence by the artist, who formulates his very personal thoughts on the respective situation here.
The jewelry cover has golden letters.
Thus, the 75-year-old almost allows a deep insight into his soul, his emotions.
That's a high level of intimacy.
Emotions were ultimately even triggers for the Bible project: Otmar Alt, born in Wernigerode in 1940 and raised in Berlin, comes from a family that belonged to the strict Order of Herrhuter.
"That's when parenting happened with the cane before," recalls Otmar Alt.
“I have tried to work this up in order to get along with the Father again.”
His second concern is to help art become more important again in fast-paced times: "The perception of pictorial worlds today is characterized by advertising, and it is loud.
Symbolism is supplanted there.
Artists have a different desire and desire to show things.
Pictures always have something mysterious and are not decorative.
“In this regard, I’m a dinosaur.”
Otmar Alt wants the viewers to "fall into" the pictures, let them take effect on themselves, discover and feel what power they have: "The image seeks its counterpart".
Klaus Altepost confessed on Tuesday at the presentation of the Otmar-Alt-Bible at the Otmar-Alt-Foundation "always" to have been a lover of Otmar Alt's art: "I like how he understands life with his pictures".
Alt did not take care not to fully grasp the Bible, but selected the topics that were important to him, which were also mysterious.
"This was not cooperation at eye level," says Altepost: "This is about the pictures, the text merely explains".
For Hans Möhler from Luther-Verlag, this work is an invitation to "deepen one's life and find freedom."
Otmar Alt's pictures speak for Möhler both the (biblical) language of love and that of responsibility.
A part of the originals - paper and screen paintings - will be presented for the first time in an exhibition that will open on November 15 at the Otmar-Alt-Stiftung.
Start of construction for BMX railway is approaching
Plans made, found space, commissioned companies, mounds modeled for a circuit: A year later, almost everything was ready - but even before the official opening of the BMX track, it was already over again.
Failures in politics and administration caused annoyance, especially among local residents, who did not want to tolerate the railway.
So deported hills, new place sought - now with formal planning and application for a building permit.
The approval will be received in October, the First municipal councillor Sabine Mosebach-Bock could now announce in the children’s and youth committee.
"I'm hoping this yever something visible will happen," she said.
It should then be started without a "further commemorative break" with the work on the former indoor swimming pool.
DFB intensifies commitment to refugees
The DFB expands its commitment to refugees until 2019, the Bundesliga sends a clear signal at the weekend.
Football is responding to the worsening situation in Germany and assures politicians their help in addressinging the problems ahead.
“I consider this to be our country’s biggest challenge since reunification.”
“In principle, nobody can say that’s none of my business, I have nothing to do with it.”
"This has to interest every individual to get to grips with this issue," said DFB chief Wolfgang Niersbach.
At the annual meeting with German Chancellor Angela Merkel on 23 September in Berlin, he intends to intensively discuss the refugee situation in Germany.
"I'll be at the Chancellery next Wednesday and I'll meet Ms Özoğuz and the chancellor there."
"That's where the issue will certainly play a central role," he announced.
The football can and wants to help.
However, politics has to set the decisive course.
The German Football Association has therefore expanded its refugee initiative "1:0 for a welcome" by four years.
"Through the Egidius Braun Foundation, we have ensured that this programme will continue until 2019," Niersbach explained on the sidelines of the UEFA conference in St Julian's/Malta.
Some federal leagues also support this program.
Already this weekend, the 36 professional clubs set a clear sign when they will wear the imprint "We help - #refugeeswelcome" on the left jersey sleeve instead of the otherwise usual sponsor logo (Hermes).
"We also want to make it clear that everyone should help where and how they can," Leverkusen's head of sport Rudi Völler told the "Bild" newspaper (Tuesday) and described the project as a "self-evidence."
“The refugee drama affects everyone, no one is allowed to look away.”
"We football leagues, no, we have to use our radiance, our influence and our opportunities to help the many refugees who have already suffered so incredibly," Hertha manager Michael Preetz said.
Hoffenheim's managing director Peter Rettig spoke of a "prominent sign of the league."
The women's national team invites 100 refugees to Halle/Saale for the European Championship qualifier against Hungary this Friday.
Niersbach described the support of refugees as "a great community work of German football."
It is great that all 36 licensing clubs have started their own projects in their locations.
It is just as great that small and smallest clubs are active.
The message "1:0 for a welcome," which we spent, is lived in football.
"That's of enormous importance," he said.
This year and next, the association, together with the DFB selection and the Federal Government, will each provide €300 000 for the integration of refugees.
"The clubs have already retrieved the whole sum for 2015," Niersbach reported.
Reports: SAP CEO loses an eye in accident
Europe's chief executive of Europe's largest software company, Bill McDermott, has reportedly lost his left-hand eye as a failure in early July.
In the accident on the stairs at his brother's house in the USA, he had fallen after midnight on a glass of water, which he had worn in his left hand, reports the "Wirtschaftswoche" on Wednesday.
"He almost bled to death, was fainting, then dragged himself out onto the road - that's what he told us last week, "WiWo" quotes SAP co-founder Hasso Plattner.
McDermott, however, can pursue his work.
"At the beginning of October, we will meet before the supervisory board meeting in Walldorf," Plattner said.
The 54-year-old manager was present at the meeting and "fully able to work".
McDermott announced in an interview with the "Süddeutsche Zeitung" (Thursday edition) that he would come back to Germany in October and continue to lead the software company.
“I’m fully there for SAP, fully in my job.”
"Of course, I have been in regular contact with the Supervisory Board and its Chairman Hasso Plattner all the time," McDermott told SZ.
McDermott usually commutes between Germany and the USA, but since the summer he was no longer in the German SAP headquarters in Walldorf.
It was important for him to go public with such a personal story, said the manager of the “SZ”: “Something like this can happen to anyone.”
“It’s important to get back up when you’ve fallen.”
McLaren replacement pilot Magnussen out of action ...
Magnussen tweeted a corresponding photo, explaining that he suffered the injury when he crashed.
He is therefore not eligible as a reserve driver for the two regular pilots Fernando Alonso and Jenson Button at the night race in Singapore on the weekend, as well as a week later at the Japanese Grand Prix.
Football: BVB without Reus against Krasnodar: Weidenfeller in goal
At Borussia Dortmund, all hopes of a comeback by Marco Reus in the first group game of the Europa League have finally shattered.
The national player will miss the leader of the Bundesliga also in a duel with FK Krasnodar on Thursday (19.00/Sky).
"We unfortunately can't resort to him," Thomas Tuchel said on Wednesday.
The BVB coach left open whether Reus will be there again in Sunday's top game against Leverkusen: "We are expecting a return from him to training on Friday".
In view of the high burden of his professionals, Tuchel will not only change his regular eleven at the Reus position.
Thus, he announced a change at the goalkeeper position.
That's why Roman Weidenfeller occupies the place of regular keeper Roman Bürki.
"He's training at an absolute top level," the football teacher praised the world champion.
However Weidenfeller will be used in all Europa League games in the future left Tuchel open.
He will almost certainly also play the second game.
Midfielder Julian Weigl considers it comprehensible: "Marco is not so wrong with Marco."
“I think that we have a good chance to pick up the title.”
Robot cars: Government wants pioneering role for Germany
According to the will of the German government, Germany should be a technological pioneer in the transformation of the car.
On Wednesday, the Federal Cabinet adopted a strategy paper from the house of Transport Minister Alexander Dobrindt (CSU), which, among other things, envisages investments in the expansion of digital infrastructure.
Dobrindt spoke of a "new mobility age" on Wednesday in Frankfurt on the sidelines of the IAA car show.
Automated and networked driving will increase road safety and cause less congestion, Dobrindt said.
He also wants to discuss the issue with his counterparts from the G7 countries, who will also meet at the IAA with industry representatives.
Among other things, the strategy paper of the Ministry of Transport emphasizes the importance of mobile broadband expansion and a rapid switch to digital radio.
For the testing of new technology, a test track is currently being built on Autobahn 9 in Bavaria.
In order not to impose any additional liability risks on the driver, the federal government wants to "review the legal framework and, where necessary, adapt it to the new developments".
Germany, for example, intends to work internationally to ensure that the permissible maximum speed for self-driving cars is raised from 10 to 130 kilometers per hour.
The Federal Government also intends to promote an amendment to the Vienna Convention, which so far only envisages people as drivers.
With automated driving, the car increasingly takes the lead, the driver becomes more and more a passenger.
The technology is based primarily on sensors and cameras that capture the environment and process the data in seconds.
According to a study presented by the Ministry of Economic Affairs, highly automated driving on motorways will be technically possible by 2020.
Nevertheless, driverless vehicles on public roads "can only be expected far after 2020."
The authors of the study estimate the added value in the field of driver assistance systems and the highly automated driving functions in Germany at around 8.4 billion euros for 2025.
"The digital networking of the car can lead to more safety and efficiency in road traffic," said SPD transport politician Sören Bartol.
However, the question of liability in the event of accidents and the safety of drivers' personal data must be clearly clarified.
However, the Germans still see self-driving cars skeptically - much more than French or Americans.
A survey of the Forsa Institute on behalf of the testing organization Dekra, presented on Wednesday at the AA, concludes that only eight percent of Germans believe that autonomous driving cars will prevail in the next ten years, 32 percent only expect it in 20 years, 31 percent do not believe in it at all.
In France, 21 percent of respondents expect autonomously driving cars by 2025, and 33 percent in the United States.
In any case, motorists in Germany aren’t ready to rely completely on a self-driving vehicle.
According to a survey by the digital association Bitkom, only seven percent would agree to relinquish control throughout the journey on all roads.
On the highway, 15 percent could imagine this in the flowing traffic, 45 percent in traffic jams.
Just over one in four would not let the car control under any circumstance.
Boll cancels team and double start at table tennis European Championship
According to the current situation, the Düsseldorfer only wants to pitch individually at the title fights from 25 September to 4 October.
The final decision is due to be made no later than September 22.
“It’s a huge loss for our team.”
“The boys found out in the morning.”
"However, we are strong enough to win the title even without Timo," said national coach Jörg Roßkopf during the European Championship course in Düsseldorf.
Boll was on the flight at that time at a long-term agreed sponsorship appointment in China.
The 34-year-old German champion is expected back on Friday.
"Without Timo, the disadvantage is, we aren't such a clear favourite anymore," declared individual European champion Dimitrij Ovtcharov.
However, the Hamelner, who leads the seeding list in Yekaterinburg, was also reminiscent of the 2013 European Championship in Schwechat.
“At that time was also lacking, and we became European champions.”
"That's what we should orientate ourselves on," explained the world number five.
Manchester United's English footballer Luke Shaw has sustained a serious injury to his right leg.
At the Champions League match at PSV Eindhoven, the defender was hit at a tackle by Héctor Moreno and replaced after long treatment.
Bastian Schweinsteiger's 20-year-old teammate pointed to a shin fracture.
Gaultier feels too old for an enfant terrible
It is a mixture of a very big fashion show and a wax figure horror cabinet: The designer Jean Paul Gaultier (63) presented an exhibition about his work in Munich on Wednesday.
The highlights: mannequins that look terrifyingly human thanks to video projections.
They wear the spectacular creations of the French designer, who, among other things, with Madonna's stage outfits, became the big star of the fashion scene - and with the fact that he put men in skirts.
“I can’t say anymore that I’m the enfant terrible of haute couture because I have white hair.”
"It used to be bleached, now it's real," Gaultier said.
For the visitors of the exhibition "From The Sidewalk To The Catwalk", which can be seen from September 18 in the Kunsthalle of the Hypo-Kulturstiftung, it is fortunate that the fashion designer has changed his initially dismissive stance to a show about his person.
Actually, he was against it, because: "For me, an exhibition sounded a bit like death," he said.
When you die, you get to the museum.
The impressive collection had previously made a stop in Montreal, Canada and Paris.
"It's about so much more than just beautiful dresses," Kunsthalle's director Roger Diederen said.
It is about tolerance, acceptance and the vision of an open society.
The exhibits give an insight into the hard-to-believed work of the man who has written fashion history without ever having completed an education.
Chronologically, the show is not set up, but it is an excellently choreographed total work of art, for which a lot of effort has been made: For example, a stylist has been hired.
According to the curator Thierry-Maxime Loriot, the makers have carted 14 tons of material to Munich.
Shortly before the start of the Oktoberfest, there was a special treat: a leather pants outfit with corset.
Heidi meets Madonna.
He has always been interested in strong women ("Women are smarter than men") and for people who are a bit different, Gaultier said.
Gaultier's old teddy can also be seen in the exhibition.
It stands for the designer's first, tentative fashionable walking attempts.
As a child (he was not a good student and bad in football) he had put on his teddy bras and dresses, Gaultier said.
Playing with a doll, his parents wouldn't have allowed him.
He later taught himself how to design fashion.
He stole fashion magazines and looked at the designs of other designers, as Gaultier recounted in Munich.
Later, of course, I bought them.
For his first own fashion show, he could not pay the models.
They didn't get anything but were allowed to keep the clothes.
Jewellery instead of curtain - Plauen lace as a fashion niche
In the past, the long embroidery machines in and around Plauen were about making meters.
The embroidered fabrics and tips were created in large quantities for customers, for example, who processed them into ceilings and curtains.
Andreas Reinhardt, Managing Director of Modespitze Plauen, explains that this alone no longer works today.
Instead, fashion is important again.
In the early days of the Plauener Spitze, fashion played a bigger role, was then supplanted by the home textile industry - and is now being rediscovered as a niche.
Reinhardt gives some examples: lace in jewelry, in accessories such as bags and regional costumes.
In general, the so-called vintage fashion, which is based on past style epochs, forms an increasing order potential.
If you don’t serve niches in the textile industry in Germany, you’re no longer on the market.
With us, they already make up half of all orders.
Together with nine other companies, Modespitze Plauen is a member of the industry association Plauener Spitze and Embroideries.
Only they are allowed to wear the protected label.
In the last 15 years, about 80 percent of sales have broke away.
Private specialty stores and also the wholesale trade almost no longer exist.
Our products hardly reach the customer in this way.
Nowadays, mass-produced goods are produced much cheaper in the Asian region and sold almost exclusively via chains.
In addition, exports are weakening, currently to Russia and the USA.
Enough reasons for new paths.
The industry association is also trying to score through environmental awareness, as Cordula Bauer from der Stickperle in Falkenstein explains: "Since this year, we have been holding a seal of quality that shows that we produce ecologically and socially compatible".
Reinhardt adds: "This also refers to the materials used".
Diploma designer Ute Schmidt teaches at the Schneeberg Branch Office of the West Saxon University of Applied Arts - and specializes in embroideries and spikes.
In order to counteract the competition from the Asian region, you have to find high-quality and innovative products.
It takes time and courage.
This creates a field of tension between the freshest ideas of the designers and the companies, which above all have to think economically," says Schmidt.
Therefore, an existing design is often varied only slightly in order to maintain a customer base.
A few years ago, textile designer Kati Reuter revived the historic snowball tip.
In some places the machine embroiders small beads.
Bianca Hallebach-Krausse from Plauen was inspired by this: "Before, I couldn't do anything with the traditional Plauener Spitze".
Now she's using the snowball tip for her jewelry.
In an elaborate process, it shapes the pattern in silver.
"Many people in the region identify with the issue of Plauen lace - young and old," says Reuter.
It also delivers to the USA, Australia and Finland - especially to customers whose ancestors lived in Plauen and the surrounding area and who would like a memorabilia.
With this, I see my jewelry as a modern ambassador of the Plauener Spitze.
Disny plans new "Mary Poppins" movie
You shouldn't touch legends, but Disney still dares to make a new film of his classic "Mary Poppins".
According to "Entertainment Weekly", however, it's not about a remake, but a kind of sequel, which takes place 20 years after the first film with the magical nanny.
It's about the nanny's experiences with the Banks family in times of the Great Depression in England.
Who plays Poppins more than 50 years after Julie Andrews is still unclear.
But the director is already certain: Rob Marshall, most recently with "Into the Woods" in the cinema.
XI urges to open up the economy more broadly to the world
The economy needs to open more broadly towards the outside to promote growth, President Xi Jinping told a group tasked with directing reform on Tuesday.
"China should work to attract foreign investment and know-how and improve strategies for business start-ups," he said in an address at the 16th meeting of the Central Leadership Group for deepening a comprehensive reform.
Promoting new start-ups and advancing reforms will bring new momentum and vitality and provide new space for economic growth, Xi said.
The leadership group has adopted a number of directives, including a negative list to regulate market access, relaxation of border control policies, promotion of state-owned enterprises (SOE), private capital, and overhaul the residents reporting system.
According to the statement, a negative list is created that identifies sectors and companies that aren’t eligible for investment.
The system is gradually tested and improved by test programs.
The initiative is important as it assigns a greater role to the market in the allocation of resources, ensuring a rule-of-law business environment and making the market more open, the statement said.
The government will also loosen checks on the powers conferred on companies and ensure that companies decide how to conduct their businesses.
The statement also said that China will allow border areas to explore new models of cross-border economic cooperation and new mechanisms to promote regional growth.
Under an offer to open state-controlled sectors, further areas will be opened up for private investment.
"The government will not change its policy towards foreign investment and will protect the legitimate interests of debt-financed companies and provide better services to them," the statement said.
In addition, it is made easier for foreigners to apply for a permanent residence permit or the "green card" by optimizing the requirements and simplifying the application process.
The right of lawyers to exercise their activities and to appoint professional judges and prosecutors was also discussed at a meeting.
The meeting was also attended by Premier Li Keqiang and key leaders such as Liu Yunshan and Zhang Gaoli, as reported in a statement released after the meeting.
EU wants to double mercury limit in predators
The European Commission wants to double the limits for mercury in large predators and thus expect consumers with a significantly higher dose of the nerve agent.
The consumer organization Foodwatch, to which a corresponding working paper of the EU is available, sharply criticized the plans: "Risks and side effects of failed industrial and environmental policy are passed on with full force to pregnant women and toddlers," explained Matthias Wolfschmidt of Foodwatch.
According to Wolfschmidt, the limits of the nerve agent for large fish at the end of the food chain, such as shark or swordfish, are to be raised from one to two milligrams of mercury per kilogram of fish for "economic policy reasons".
However, large predators are already so heavily contaminated with mercury that about 50 percent of the catches may not be sold on the basis of the still applicable limits.
After doubling the limits, only 14.5 percent would be unsaleable.
In return for this easing, the EU wants to tighten the limits for other fish from currently 0.5 milligrams to 0.1 milligrams of mercury per kilogram, according to Foodwatch.
However, Foodwatch described this as a "trick", because, such as carp, are usually burdened so low that they already comply with the planned maximum values today.
The reduction in small fish is therefore "a perfidious distraction manoeuvre that helps the economy alone," Wolfschmidt explained.
Mercury is a highly toxic heavy metal for humans, which accumulates strongly across the food chain, especially in predatory fish.
The federal government therefore warns that "already small amounts can cause damage to the nervous system, especially in unborn babies."
The Federal Ministry for the Environment declares on its homepage with regard to the applicable maximum values, however, that a "health hazard to the general population" is not to be expected.
Mercury enters the environment primarily through coal combustion.
According to written information from the Federal Ministry for the Environment, the German coal-fired power plants emit more than six tons of mercury a year, two-thirds of the total amount emitted in Germany.
The concentration of mercury in fishes, for example in the Elbe, the Rhine and the Danube was "permanently and extensively exceeded," the "Mirror" had quoted from the paper in March.
Last year, Europe-wide warning of mercury in fish was already one of the most frequently reporteded health risks in the RASFF EU warning system.
Fooodwatch urges consumers to protest against EU plans at: www.quecksilber-aktion.foodwatch.de
